🧾🟢 **F-2487-1 — A NEGATIVE RESULT, BANKED SO NOBODY SPENDS A FIRE ON IT: F-2484-1’S PER-WORKTREE SWEEP WAS EXECUTED ON *ONE* ARENA AND FIVE MORE WERE NEVER ASKED — ALL SIX ARE NOW MEASURED AND EVERY BYTE IS SAFE (s2487, 2026-09-04, NINETEENTH CONSECUTIVE DRY BOARD).** Found by executing the three-fire-old cure across its whole subject set rather than admiring it. **MEASURED over all six live `/tmp` arenas, each read’s CONTROL asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1 — every tree returned 23,942–25,483 TRACKED files, so a zero is an ANSWER not a failed read): `heat9` 0 untracked · `heat9-r2` 0 · `heat10` 0 · `gr-lane-d-edge` 2 (neither under an evidence prefix) · `heat11` 80 · **`heat8` 99, an arena no sweep had ever reached.** Both non-zero sets are present in the object database AND reachable from an ORIGIN ref: `AT RISK 0 · LOCAL-ONLY 0`.** ✅ **THE DURABLE HALF IS A JUDGING TEST THAT MECHANISES THE ONE F-2483-1 DECLINED TO GUARD:** that finding called its fifth test (*is this content already committed in another form?*) a JUDGEMENT needing you to read two files and decide equivalence. **For arena residue it is not — a detached arena SHARES main’s object database, so `git hash-object --stdin-paths` from main plus a set-membership test against `git rev-list --objects` over `refs/remotes/` answers it exactly, for thousands of files, in two processes.** ⛔ **AND THE CAVEAT I NEARLY PUBLISHED OVER: blob identity is BLIND TO THE PATH. Of the 99, `94` match a same-basename file on main, `1` is an EMPTY file (dedup matched an unrelated `replay-v2-control.err` and means nothing), and `4` are CROSS-PATH WITH A SHIFTED ATTEMPT NUMBER — arena `attempt-3.jsonl` (6.08 MB) is committed as `attempt-2.jsonl`. The bytes are safe; the arena’s LABELLING is not.** 🎯 **THE PATTERN: an arena writes evidence at a FLAT path (`artifacts/claude-opus-baron/`) and the drain commits it under its DATED home (`artifacts/claude-debut-20260901/claude-opus-baron/`) — so arena residue is a STAGING COPY and the correct verdict is almost always DO NOT SALVAGE.** ⚠️ **NOTHING IS AT RISK and this is NOT a false green — no instrument ever claimed these were safe, because until today none was ever asked.** ⛔ **DO NOT RE-SWEEP THESE SIX.** Re-run only when `git worktree list` shows a NEW arena. 🚫 **NO GUARD, restraint INHERITED not re-argued: an untracked file in a live arena is a lawful routine mid-run state, so a red fires during correct operation and is excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1) — the same call F-2357-1, F-2389-1, F-2484-1 and F-2485-1 all made for this sweep.** 💡 **REUSABLE (F-2358-1 turned on a CURE rather than a selector): when you inherit a sweep, ask not only how WIDE its axis is but HOW MANY OF ITS SUBJECTS THE AUTHOR ACTUALLY VISITED — a cure written under time pressure is validated on the one instance that provoked it, and the rest of its own subject set inherits a verdict nobody measured.** Law: `scripts/fire.md` §2E, appended after F-2486-1.

🧾🟢 **F-2486-1 — A NEGATIVE RESULT, BANKED SO NOBODY SPENDS A FIRE ON IT: FOUR WORKING TREES SIT OUTSIDE *EVERY* INSTRUMENT'S SUBJECT SET, AND THEY ARE MEASURABLY EMPTY (s2486, 2026-09-04, EIGHTEENTH CONSECUTIVE DRY BOARD).** Found by executing F-2484-1's two-fire-old cure rather than admiring it, and aimed by the corpus itself — `art-staging-audit` prints these four in its own NOT-AUDITED section on every dry board. **THE CLASS IS REAL: `worktrees/lane-{a,b,c,d}-salvage` are ABSENT from `git worktree list` (measured: `99` entries, `0` salvage), so F-2484-1's per-worktree sweep — which enumerates from that registry — and `lane-usable` are both structurally incapable of ever making them subjects; F-2484-1's rule filters to trees "OUTSIDE the repo root" and these are INSIDE it, a second independent reason it skips them; and main's own repo-wide read excludes them too (`0` of its 49 paths are under `worktrees/`). Five instruments, five keys, one hole.** ⛔ **MY SHARPER HYPOTHESIS WAS REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT AND IS RECORDED AS SUCH: each salvage tree's `.git` is a FILE reading `gitdir: …/.git/worktrees/lane-<x>`, i.e. it borrows the LANE's admin gitdir, so I predicted the prescribed read pointed there would answer about the LANE — F-2222-1's polarity, a true declaration about the wrong corpus. It does NOT. Measured: of the paths it reports, `7/7` (lane-a), `11/11` (lane-c) and `6/6` (lane-d) exist in the SALVAGE tree and `0` exist in the lane. THE READ IS CORRECT WHEN POINTED; ONLY THE POINTING IS MISSING.** ⚖️ **AND NOTHING IS AT RISK, established rather than assumed, by F-2485-1's own gitless-safe method (walk, then batch `git hash-object --stdin-paths` + `git cat-file --batch-check` FROM MAIN, with the hash count asserted equal to the file count before any zero was believed — F-2215-1): `11,935` files walked; `10,043` / `833.31 MB` are in no object database, and **100% of them are gitignored `dist/` build output and `test-results/` playwright scratch** (`9,479` files / `768.64 MB` in `.playwright-artifacts-0` alone). The genuinely non-ignored untracked set across all four trees is `29` files — and **`0` of `29` are absent from the object database.** Every one is already a blob git holds.** 🎯 **THE RESULT WORTH MORE THAN THE SWEEP, AND IT INVERTS THIS STREAK'S USUAL FINDING: THE EXISTING INSTRUMENT'S DECLARED BOUNDARY WAS CORRECT AND ITS STATED REASON MEASURABLY ACCURATE.** `art-staging-audit` names all four trees and says *"outside the scan root BY DESIGN (walking them would drown AT RISK in build output)"* — and that is precisely what the numbers show, 99.6% of the at-risk bytes being exactly build output. Twenty-odd fires have found a declared boundary hiding a hole; this one tested a boundary and found it sound. 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: gitignored build output in a scratch tree is a lawful, routine state, so a red would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate) — the same conclusion F-2357-1, F-2389-1, F-2484-1 and F-2485-1 all reached for this sweep. ⛔ DO NOT RE-SWEEP THESE FOUR, and do not "register" them to bring them into the sweep: a salvage tree BORROWS its lane's HEAD, index and branch refs, so a write there moves the live lane's refs without rewriting its disk (F-2406-1, the Mistake #2 direction).** ⚠️ **ONE METHOD TRAP PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE, and it is F-2365-1's verbatim: `lane-usable --all | grep -c salvage` returns `1`, which reads as "one salvage tree IS visible" and would have refuted the whole finding. Reading the hit shows it is an artifact FILENAME — `…act-3-gang-quit-salvage.png` — not a tree row. A bare token is not a subject; READ THE HIT, never trust the count.** 💡 **REUSABLE: a NOT-AUDITED list is a CLAIM WITH A REASON, and Mistake #4 governs reassurance exactly as it governs alarm — so test it once. But bank the confirmation when it holds, because that same line prints on every dry board, and the next idle fire will read it, feel the pull, and spend a whole budget re-deriving this. The cheapest thing a dry fire can produce is a measured negative that retires a target.**

🧾🟢 **F-2485-1 — THE PER-WORKTREE SWEEP CAN *FAIL* RATHER THAN RETURN EMPTY: A REGISTERED WORKTREE MAY HAVE NO `.git`, AND 24 GB OF DETACHED HEAT ARENA SITS BEHIND THAT ERROR (s2485, 2026-09-04, SEVENTEENTH CONSECUTIVE DRY BOARD).** Found by EXECUTING F-2484-1's one-fire-old cure rather than admiring it. **MEASURED: `git worktree list` returns `99` trees, `68` outside the repo root, and `2` are marked by git itself `prunable — gitdir file points to non-existent location` — `/private/tmp/heat7-81caa695` (`20,598` files / `12.17 GB`) and `/private/tmp/heat7g-81caa695` (`17,807` files / `11.88 GB`), both §2H detached heat arenas. The prescribed `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` with `cwd` set to either returns `fatal: not a git repository`, rc≠0, so the sweep cannot answer about `38,405` files and says so only as an error.** ⚖️ **NOTHING IS AT RISK, established rather than assumed: of `20,830` files in those two trees' `artifacts/`+`reviews/`+`tasks/runs/`+`logs/runs-archive/` (`13.2 GB`), exactly `8` (`4` distinct, `11 KB`) are in no object database, and all eight are vendor `openclaw/state/plugin-skills/*/SKILL.md` — tool scratch, not factory evidence. NOT a false green either: the failure is LOUD.** What earns it a row is the DIRECTION — a fatal error reads as *"not a real tree, skip it"*, which is right for a stale registry entry and wrong for a 12 GB arena, and the two are byte-identical at the point of the error; and no other instrument reaches these, verified by reading (`art-staging-audit` never scans `/tmp` and says so, `withheld-evidence-audit` keys on lane-runner lines an attended arena never writes, `review-evidence-audit` is citation-driven). ✅ **A SECOND, SEPARABLE NEGATIVE RESULT, banked with its mechanism so no fire re-takes it: the other arenas are SAFE. `/private/tmp/heat8-4675cfd7` holds `106` untracked files / `34.36 MB`, of which `99` are already in the object database AND ref-reachable (verified via `git log --all --find-object`, e.g. `4e75765cd`); the `7` that are not are all `.scratch-rider/` helpers — scratch by name. heat11 is s2484's, salvaged and pushed.** ⛔ **DO NOT `git worktree prune` THESE — THE OBVIOUS HYGIENE ACTION IS THE DESTRUCTIVE ONE: it clears both registry entries in one command and deletes nothing on disk, so it looks free; but the registry entry is the ONLY thing that puts these `24 GB` into the sweep's subject set, so pruning makes them invisible to the one instrument that looks at them while every instrument keeps reporting health. That is F-2398-2's shape on a new axis — there `git add`-ing `logs/guard-stats.jsonl` ends the collection it holds; here a stale registration is the precondition for the tree being seen.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, restraint inherited not re-argued: a prunable worktree is what a finished heat lawfully leaves behind, so a red fires during ordinary correct operation and is excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1) — the same conclusion F-2357-1, F-2389-1 and F-2484-1 all reached for this sweep. CURE: `scripts/fire.md` §2E now states that an error from that read is `could-not-answer` and never `nothing-here`, and gives the method that needs no `.git` in the tree — walk it, then batch `git hash-object --stdin-paths` + `git cat-file --batch-check` FROM MAIN, since a detached arena shares main's object database.** 💡 **REUSABLE, the rung UNDER F-2484-1: that finding said ask WHICH TREE the selector runs in. The sharper fact is that `git worktree list` IS A REGISTRY, NOT A CENSUS — registration outlives the checkout, so the subject set can be enumerated perfectly while some members are unreadable. Twenty-odd fires have asked how a subject set comes back EMPTY; this one comes back COMPLETE AND PARTLY UNREADABLE, and a sweep that does not separate *answered: nothing* from *could not answer* reports the two as one number.**

🧾🟢 **F-2484-1 — THE UNTRACKED-EVIDENCE SWEEP IS PER-WORKTREE, AND §2H MANDATES WRITING EVIDENCE INTO A TREE IT CANNOT SEE; 11.8 MB OF HEAT-11 EVIDENCE SAT IN NO OBJECT DATABASE (s2484, 2026-09-04, SIXTEENTH CONSECUTIVE DRY BOARD).** `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` reports on the working tree it is RUN IN, so F-2389-1’s repo-wide read — correct, and pathspec-free at its widest — is complete over main and structurally blind to every other working tree of this same repo. §2H tells every heat to build a DETACHED ARENA, and a heat writes its primary evidence there. **MEASURED: `/tmp/heat11-5e7a7c0b/artifacts/heat11/` held `80` files / `11.8 MB` untracked — every controller, tape, `gauntlet-outcome.json` and `gauntlet-report.md` for BOTH rigs across ALL THREE maps of heat 11 — while main’s own sweep returned `49` files the same minute and named none of them, correctly.** No other instrument reaches it either, verified by reading: `art-staging-audit` scans art staging (its NOT-AUDITED list names only the four `worktrees/` trees), `withheld-evidence-audit` keys on lane-runner withhold lines and an attended arena has no lane runner, `review-evidence-audit` is citation-driven and nothing cites these. ⚖️ **NOTHING WAS LOST and this is NOT a false green — no instrument ever claimed these were safe, because none was asked.** SALVAGED and PUSHED: `save/heat11-arena-evidence-s2484` = `76588f2b0` (80 files) and `save/heat11-twin-banks-results-s2484` = `c6ade6f86` (the 5 tracked-but-uncommitted e1-twin-banks result files in main’s own working tree), both confirmed on origin by `ls-remote` (F-1055-1). Built through a throwaway `GIT_INDEX_FILE` on the `salvage-art-staging.mjs` pattern, PARENTLESS so neither reads as a merge candidate; main’s and the arena’s `git status --porcelain` counts and HEADs were asserted identical before and after (19/19, 1/1). ⛔ **I DID NOT ASSERT THE PURGE MECHANISM I EXPECTED: `/etc/periodic/daily/110.clean-tmps` is ABSENT on this machine, so macOS does NOT reap `/tmp` here. The certain loss paths are the disk (Mistake #11) and the ordinary end-of-heat `git worktree remove`; a reboot’s effect on `/private/tmp` is UNMEASURED.** 🧾 **A SECOND, SEPARABLE RESULT worth the ledger on its own: heat 11’s e1-twin-banks fable DNF is NOT a rig failure. Its `ride.stdout.log` is 62 B carrying the F-2482-1 session-limit wall verbatim — "You’ve hit your session limit · resets 11:10pm (Asia/Bangkok)" — a transient, self-clearing, service-side entitlement wall that had cleared before this fire ran. opus on the same map is rc=0 / SECURED / 20 waves / 200 gold / 920 kills / `fnv1a32:ce442a62`, the first secure in county history for that map. A heat is a COMPARISON, so booking that row as a fable failure would corrupt it; the matrix still carries only the 13:29Z DNF-transport rows and its booking is the operator’s, not a fire’s.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, restraint inherited not re-argued: an untracked file in a live arena is a lawful mid-run state, so a red fires during ordinary correct operation and is excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1) — the same conclusion F-2357-1 and F-2389-1 both reached for this sweep. CURE: `scripts/fire.md` §2E now tells the sweep to run `git worktree list` and re-take the untracked read with `cwd` set to any tree outside the repo root.** 💡 **REUSABLE, a NEW AXIS after F-2358-1 (how WIDE is the selector) and F-2389-1 (can the axis EXPRESS the defect): ASK WHICH TREE THE SELECTOR RUNS IN. F-2222-1 showed a sweep reading a STALE tree answering confidently about the wrong board; this is its dual — the sweep reads exactly the RIGHT tree, and there are others. A repo is a SET of working trees, and every per-worktree instrument scopes itself to one without saying so.**

🧾🟢 **F-2483-1 — THE EVIDENCE-SWEEP JUDGING RULE HAS FOUR TESTS AND THEY ARE UNANIMOUSLY WRONG ABOUT ONE LIVE FILE; THE MISSING FIFTH IS A REDUNDANCY TEST (s2483, 2026-09-03, FIFTEENTH CONSECUTIVE DRY BOARD).** F-2357-1/F-2389-1 tell a fire to sweep `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` with NO pathspec and judge what it returns by three tests (not gitignored · not secret-bearing · has tracked siblings); F-2398-2 added a fourth (who WRITES it — is being untracked the precondition for it being written at all?). **Subject: `artifacts/gauntlet-heat11-20260903/rides/e1-twin-banks/{fable,opus}/wall.log`, the arena wrapper’s progress tail. It passes ALL FOUR — the third one loudly, with FOUR tracked siblings in its own directory (`charter.md`, `ride-meta.json`, `ride.stdout.log`, `ride.stderr.log`) — and committing it would still be WRONG.** 📊 **MEASURED: `git log --all --diff-filter=A -- '*wall.log'` returns **ZERO** — no `wall.log` has EVER been added to this repo, across **18** heat directories. That is a unanimous convention, not an omission.** ⓘ **AND ITS CONTENT IS COVERED, verified by READING both siblings rather than assumed: the transport facts (`exitCode` · `wallClockSeconds` · `wallHit` · `firstOutputAt` · `startedAt`/`endedAt` · `sessionId`) are all in the tracked `ride-meta.json` — which is also why the secret test does not bite, since tracking adds no exposure the repo does not already carry — and the ride RESULT is in the tracked `ride.stdout.log` (opus: *"Gold / kills / calls | 200 / 920 / 118"* plus the full report). Residue: the `runsSoFar`/`scored` counters and an OS pid.** ⚠️ **AND F-2398-2’s consumer test CANNOT save you here either: `ride-wall.mjs` writes the file with a plain `process.stdout.write` and probes tracked-ness nowhere, so tracking it breaks no collection — the test correctly returns "harmless", which is not the same as "correct".** ⚠️ **A METHOD TRAP I FELL INTO AND CORRECTED: I first tried to establish the redundancy from `matrix.md`, the heat’s own summary table, whose header says *"updated after EVERY ride"* — and it carried ONLY the two DNF-transport rows, not the completed relaunch. **The digest was BEHIND the evidence at the moment I judged it**, so reading it would have told me the data was unique when it was not. Check the RAW sibling, never the summary.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, NOT a false green, nothing lost — s2482 reached the CORRECT verdict on these same two files one fire earlier. What it gave was a REASON already false when written (*"a LIVE attended heat whose siblings are dirty right now"*): both files carry a terminal `END <rig> rc=… wallHit=false` line, and nothing in the 114-file tree had been written for 48 minutes, so liveness was decidable by READING the file rather than by asserting it.** 🎯 **The DIRECTION earns it: a fire applying the four tests literally commits a redundant progress tail and breaks an 18-heat convention — mild alone, but the SAME missing test is what tells you when a sweep result genuinely IS owed, which is the half F-2357-1 exists for (Mistake #11, bytes that die with the disk).** 🚫 **NO GUARD, restraint inherited rather than re-argued: "is this content already committed elsewhere?" is a JUDGEMENT requiring you to read two files and decide equivalence, so a red would fire on every legitimate untracked evidence file and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1) — the same reason F-2357-1 and F-2389-1 both declined guards for this very sweep.** 💡 **REUSABLE: the four existing tests are all properties of the FILE and its NEIGHBOURS; this one is a property of the INFORMATION. A file can be unique on disk and redundant in content, and only the second decides whether losing it costs anything. Ask what you could NOT reconstruct from the tracked files beside it — if the answer is "nothing", the sweep found debris, not evidence.** LANDED: `scripts/fire.md` §2E, appended to F-2398-2’s own four-test sentence.

🧾🟢 **F-2482-1 — A THIRD AUTH-REFUSAL WORDING EXISTS AND F-2480-1’S CENSUS DOES NOT NAME IT; ITS TWO QUOTED STRINGS MATCH NEITHER OF THE FOUR FIRES THAT DIED IMMEDIATELY BEFORE ME (s2482, 2026-09-03, FOURTEENTH CONSECUTIVE DRY BOARD).** F-2480-1 establishes the short (≤9 s) `rc!=0` bucket as auth/quota refusal rather than a runner outage, and tells the next fire to verify BY READING the lines — quoting exactly two: org-disabled and *"weekly limit"*. **The four fires at 22:57–23:07 today refused with a THIRD wording, *"You’ve hit your session limit · resets 11:10pm (Asia/Bangkok)"*, which is in ZERO law surfaces (measured across all six via `scripts/law-surfaces.mjs`).** 📊 **RE-MEASURED per-fire and POSITIONALLY over the same 56 logs — a refusal is a line between a `FIRE START` and its PAIRED `FIRE END` — with the control asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1): the harness reproduces F-2480-1’s `636` org-disabled EXACTLY. Short bucket = `1,026`: `636` org-disabled · `380` weekly · **`4` session** · `2` API 5xx · `4` unclassified.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY HONEST AND NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, not a false green, nothing broken — all four took NO LOCK, stranded nothing, the archive chain stayed intact, and the wall self-cleared at its stated reset (s2482 booted five minutes later and ran normally).** 🎯 **The DIRECTION earns it: a fire matching neither quoted string falls through to “unclassified” and is aimed back at the RUNNER — §2.0b’s `ps`, §2.0c’s `health-watch`, the restart — the exact wrong subject F-2480-1 exists to name and prices at ~2h26m.** 🔑 **AND THE KEY MUST BE POSITIONAL, NOW PRICED: a raw grep for *"usage limit"* scores `893` across 8 days and the honest per-fire count is `0` — every one is a `STATUS.md` handoff archive echoed into a transcript. F-2102-1’s narration confound at full strength: 2.7× inflation there, a TOTAL false positive here. This row is itself the mechanism, so count between paired markers, never repo-wide.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, restraint inherited: an entitlement wall is a lawful service-side condition, so a red fires during ordinary correct operation and is excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1).** ➡️ **Treat it as the weekly wall — transient, self-clearing, service-side. Do NOT raise a `CODEX-WALL` (that flag is about the implementer; codex was never asked) and do NOT restart the runner.** 💡 **REUSABLE: a census that classifies by ENUMERATING the wordings it has seen is complete only over its own corpus, and VENDOR PROSE IS NOT A STABLE KEY. Bank the UNCLASSIFIED count beside every classified bucket — mine is `4`, the only number here that can tell the next fire the vocabulary moved again.** LANDED: `scripts/fire.md` §1.1, at F-2480-1’s own two-string sentence.

🧾🟢 **F-2480-1 — F-2204-1’S rc!=0 CENSUS IS ARITHMETICALLY RIGHT AND CAUSALLY WRONG: ITS 95.9% “RUNNER-LEVEL OUTAGE (CLAUDE NEVER BOOTED)” BUCKET IS 99.4% AUTH/QUOTA REFUSAL, AND IT AIMS THE NEXT FIRE AT THE RUNNER (s2480, 2026-09-03).** Re-measured over 56 fire logs / 4,664 anchored ends / 1,120 `rc!=0`. **(1) The key is ambiguous and only one reading is true:** “0–9 s successor” read as the gap to the next `FIRE START` gives **0 of 1,120** (launchd’s cadence puts every successor ~300 s out); read as the fire’s own START→END DURATION — which is what the gloss “Claude never booted” describes — it gives **1,069 / 1,120 = 95.4%**, reproducing s2204’s 95.9% on a wider corpus. **(2) The cause is not an outage:** 1,063 of those 1,069 (99.4%) are service-side refusals, verified by READING the lines, not by a regex — `636` × *“Your organization has disabled Claude subscription access for Claude Code”* and `427` × *“You’ve hit your weekly limit”*. Claude booted, reached the service, and was turned away in under nine seconds. **(3) The shape corroborates:** all 1,069 fall on NINE days (subscription 07-10, 08-04/05/06; quota 07-14/15, 07-24/25, 08-01, 08-07/08, 08-27/28/29), episodic exactly as an entitlement outage is and a flaky local runner is not; nothing since 08-29. **(4) A third class the clause cannot express:** `10` fires died on a server-side `API Error: 529/500` after MINUTES — `8` of them longer than 9 s, so they fall in neither named bucket. Two are s2480’s own predecessors (233 s and 218 s, both rc=1, both logging only the 529) and are the “API outage” the attended `lane/b` + `lane/d` salvage commits cite by name; they took no lock, so there was no F-2403-1 cascade and the archive chain was intact. ⚖️ **NOT a false green; realised cost ZERO** — both readings agree such a fire holds no lock, which is the only thing §1.1 acts on, so the clause’s purpose and its 5 confirmed silent exits are untouched. 🎯 **What earns it a correction is the DIRECTION:** “runner-level outage” points a diagnosing fire at §2.0b’s `ps`/`tty` probe, §2.0c’s `health-watch` read and the `start-lane-runner.sh` restart — all the WRONG SUBJECT for an entitlement wall the runner cannot fix; §2.0b prices that same wrong-premise error at ~2h26m. The right surfaces are §2C’s credit-wall retry law and §2.0’s CODEX-WALL machinery. 🚫 **No guard, measured restraint:** every one of these is a lawful service-side state the factory correctly rides out, so a red fires during ordinary correct operation and is excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1). 🛠️ **CURED IN `scripts/fire.md` §1.1 IN THE SAME COMMIT AS THE MEASUREMENT** (F-2414-1’s lesson: the severity sentence names its reader, so it IS the edit instruction). 💡 **REUSABLE — a new axis:** twenty-odd fires have asked *how can this measurement be WRONG?* (a swallow, a guard, a cwd, a stale tree, a filename, a timezone, a window). This one is RIGHT AND CAUSALLY WRONG. A census bucket carries a COUNT and a STORY; only the count is measured, and the story is what the next reader acts on. When you bank a bucket, state how you would have told the causes apart — and if the answer is “I read the count and inferred the cause”, label it inference.

🧾🔴 **F-2479-2 — THE HANG TEST FOR THE MANDATED BATTERY FIRES ON HEALTHY RUNS, AND ITS PRESCRIBED DIAGNOSTIC RETURNS THE HANG SIGNATURE ON A GREEN ONE — I WAS ONE SAMPLE FROM KILLING A PASSING GATE (s2479, 2026-09-03, ELEVENTH CONSECUTIVE DRY BOARD).** 📊 **THE MEASUREMENT: `npm run test:ledger-guards`, run as this fire’s mandated last act on a QUIET machine (load average `3.58`, no lane runner, no drain in flight), returned **rc=0, GREEN, `436.6 s` — 7.3 minutes**. `scripts/fire.md` quotes `77–99 s` quiet (F-2414-1) and `131–166 s` loaded (F-2452-1), so this is **2.6× the top of the loaded band and 4.4× the quiet top** — and it is a fully GREEN aggregate, not a failure. The battery has also grown again: **`105` node test files** plus ~20 chained leaves, against the `97` F-2414-1 cites.** ⛔ **AND THE LAW TOLD ME TO CALL IT A HANG: `fire.md` states *“AND IF IT RUNS PAST ~3 MINUTES, THAT IS A HANG AND NOT LOAD — READ F-2428-1 BEFORE YOU WAIT”*. At 3:55 I was past that threshold on a run that had 7 more green minutes to go.** 🚨 **THE DANGEROUS HALF IS THE DIAGNOSTIC, NOT THE THRESHOLD: F-2428-1 sends you to a `node`-routed `ps` *“looking for a 0%-CPU child”*, and on this GREEN run that test returned the WEDGE SIGNATURE — runner pid 67859 at `0.36 s` cumulative CPU and its sole child pid 89475 at `0.05 s`, **both flat across a 6-second and then a 40-second sampling window**. The reason is mechanical and general: **a process blocked on NETWORK I/O is indistinguishable, by CPU, from one wedged in `uv_thread_join` at platform teardown.** F-2429-1’s real specimens were on the machine at the same time (two `law-pointer-guard.mjs` corpses, `PPID 1`, aged `02-18:30`), which made the false reading MORE convincing, not less.** 🔑 **THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT WORKS IS ONE LEVEL DOWN, AND IT IS WHAT SAVED THE RUN: ask whether the idle child has a LIVE, RECENTLY-SPAWNED GRANDCHILD. Filtering `ps -Ao pid,ppid,pcpu,etime,args` on the child’s pid as PPID showed `bash scripts/health-watch.sh status` at **3 seconds old** — a network edge probe, i.e. PROGRESS. F-2429-1’s wedge is by its own description a LEAF (`law-pointer-guard.mjs` contains zero subprocess calls), reparented to `PPID 1`, aged in HOURS. So: a fresh grandchild = alive; no grandchild + `PPID 1` + hours = wedged.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: NOTHING WAS BROKEN AND NOTHING SHIPPED WRONG — the battery was green, I did not kill it, and no false green was ever reported. The realised cost this fire was ZERO.** 🎯 **What earns it a red row is the DIRECTION at the only moment it fires: the instruction and its diagnostic AGREE on the wrong answer, they fire on a fire’s MANDATED LAST ACT, and the prescribed response is to stop waiting and *“run individually the guards that read YOUR OWN edits and SAY in the handoff that the aggregate did not complete”* — i.e. **kill a passing gate and hand the next fire a battery reported as incomplete when it was green.** A fire under closing-time pressure, told by its own law that 3 minutes means a hang, will take that exit.** 🛠️ **CURE, landed in `scripts/fire.md` in the same fire as the measurement (F-2414-1’s own lesson: when your finding’s severity sentence names a reader, THAT SENTENCE IS THE EDIT INSTRUCTION): the cost clause now carries `436.6 s` beside its predecessors as a sequence rather than a replacement, and the `~3 MINUTES` clause is RETIRED AS A TRIGGER — superseded wording kept verbatim beneath it under the RETENTION LAW, because the wedge it describes is REAL and its mechanism stands. The new trigger is `~8 min`, and it is explicitly NOT sufficient on its own: no hang may be concluded without the grandchild check.** ⚠️ **A STORY I TRIED AND REFUTED, recorded because the refutation is worth more than the guess: I watched `edge-probe-composite-guard.test.mjs` hold the runner as the SOLE live child across ~60 s of sampling and was ready to name it the hot leg. **Run alone it is `13.0 s`** (sibling `edge-alarm-clock-guard` `14.6 s`). The attribution is FALSE and the `436.6 s` is DISTRIBUTED, not concentrated. Per F-2182-1: publish the measured BAND, never a plausible story — and note that “the last child still standing” is a seductive but invalid attribution, because with `node --test` concurrency the tail file is simply whichever finished last.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is inherited rather than re-argued: F-2414-1 and s2413 both declined a guard on this exact figure because a pinned duration reds on every slower machine and would be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). That argument is STRONGER now that the observed spread is `77 s → 436.6 s`. The cure is the sentence in the law.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS for this streak’s long-running question: twenty-odd fires have asked *how can this measurement be WRONG?* — a swallow, a guard, a cwd, a stale tree, a filename, a timezone, a hidden load parameter. This one is a DIAGNOSTIC WHOSE SIGNATURE IS SHARED BY TWO OPPOSITE STATES. Flat CPU means “not computing”, which covers both “dead” and “waiting on the network”, and only a SECOND observation at a different level of the process tree separates them. **When a symptom is defined by an ABSENCE, ask what else produces that same absence before you act on it — and prefer a positive sign of life (a fresh grandchild, a growing file, a new log line) over any amount of stillness.**

