scripts/fire.md:26:E. **PIPELINE REFILL (owner directive 2026-07-06: "4–5 lanes working constantly")**: after drains, check every lane queue; for each EMPTY queue, `cp` the top unblocked item from `tasks/BACKLOG.md`'s ladder for that lane (respect its GATE lines; stale-check masters older than ~2 days against current main — refresh or flag, never blind-queue). **SALVAGE LIFECYCLE (owner UX ruling 2026-07-06 late): `save/<name>` = re-land pending (counts as waiting, keep visible); when its re-land MERGES, rename to `archive/<name>` in the same fire — archives never count as waiting and never rot anyone's experience. A save/* older than ~3h with a known conflict gets a RE-LAND ruling, not a hand-merge.** **LANE-SAFETY LAW (s61 forensics, 2026-07-06): NEVER refill a lane that has an undrained done-move or a lane branch ahead of main — lane pre-flights `reset --hard`, which DESTROYS unmerged predecessor output (w1-03 light-pass and polish-02 were lost exactly this way; w1-04 survived only as a reflog orphan). Drain the lane first, refill second. Any lane task you AUTHOR must use a drain-gated pre-flight: check the worktree/branch is clean-vs-main before resetting, else STOP and report.** ⚙️ **AND ASK IT WITH THE INSTRUMENT, NOT A FILE-LEVEL EYEBALL (F-1081-9, cured by mechanism s1272 `0db281e0`; the law surface wired up s1278, F-1278-1).** `node scripts/lane-freeze-classify.mjs <branch>` classifies EVERY path the lane’s ahead-commits touched into DUPLICATE / LANE-ONLY / MAIN-ONLY / BOTH-MOVED, **prints the denominator it actually looked at** (`paths=N`), and exits 0 only when the lane holds nothing main has not absorbed (exit 1 = HOLDS n, exit 2 = misuse — read from the code, s1278). ⚠️ **`BOTH-MOVED` is a triage bucket, NOT a loss verdict**: a file lands there whenever main moved at all, *including when main moved by absorbing the lane’s own hunk and adding more.* Ask the one-directional line-level question with `node scripts/lane-absorbed-lines.mjs <branch> <path> [<path> ...]` — ⓘ **the RUNNABLE form and its argument shape are stated here because the law carried only the bare filename until s2392, while `lane-freeze-classify` has printed the full invocation below its own HOLDS banner all along (F-2392-1, measured: `node scripts/lane-absorbed-lines.mjs` appeared 0 times across all six LAW_SURFACES). MINOR and deliberately not inflated — the surrounding sentence is imperative, so a reader was always told WHICH tool to ask; what was missing was only how to type it.** s1271 froze three lanes for days on the coarse file-level read and **two of them were never at risk**, while the third held 140 lines main had never seen — a conservative verdict at the wrong resolution is not safety, it is a different way of being wrong. 🚦 **BUT THAT INSTRUMENT ANSWERS THE SAFETY QUESTION, NOT THE REFILL QUESTION — ASK BOTH (F-1298-4, mechanism landed s1299).** `lane-freeze-classify` asks *would a reset LOSE anything?*; a master's pre-flight asks *is this branch clean vs main?* and **STOPs on any unmerged commit, absorbed or not**. s1298 measured `lane/m4` LOSS-FREE, refilled it, and the runner burned **26,940 tokens for zero edits and zero tests** stopping at exactly that line — a lane can be **safe-to-reset and unusable at the same time**, and the fire had no instrument that said so. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BEFORE ANY REFILL: `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs <slot|branch>`** (or `--all` for the fleet). It resolves slot→branch from `git worktree list` itself (**lane slot names are NOT branch names** — ⚠️ **and the mapping ROTS: this parenthetical read "lane-a is `lane/m3`, lane-b `lane/m4`, lane-c `lane/e2-arsenal`, lane-d `lane/perf`" until s1464 measured it FALSE. `git worktree list` shows `worktrees/lane-a..d` checked out on `lane/a`, `lane/b`, `lane/c`, `lane/d`. All eight branches still EXIST, which is what makes this dangerous — the stale names resolve, they just answer about the wrong ref: `lane/m3` reads `ahead=5 behind=347` and `lane/m4` `ahead=7 behind=982`, so a fire measuring by this prose would conclude lanes a and b HOLD unmerged content and refuse to refill two lanes that are actually `ahead=0` USABLE — a false freeze. F-1464-3.** ➡️ **NEVER read the mapping out of this sentence — resolve it from `git worktree list`, which is what `lane-usable.mjs` does itself), and returns one word: **USABLE** (rc 0, `main..branch` empty + clean → refill freely) · **AHEAD-BUT-ABSORBED** (rc 1 — safe to reset but the pre-flight WILL stop; `--cure` archives the tip to `archive/<branch>-s<N>-absorbed-<sha>` **before** resetting, history first, then verifies `main..branch` is empty) · **HOLDS** (rc 2 — content main never absorbed: drain it or rule on the listed paths by hand; `--cure` REFUSES) · **DIRTY** / **BUSY** (rc 2). ⚠️ **Read the WORD, not just the code** — HOLDS and DIRTY and BUSY share rc 2 deliberately, because all three mean *don't refill* while naming three different owed acts. 💀 **AND A BUSY LANE HAS THREE CAUSES, NOT ONE — A CRASHED-RUNNER CORPSE READS EXACTLY LIKE A LIVE RUNNER (F-2366-1, measured and cured s2366).** The BUSY test established WHO holds the slot and **never asked whether that holder is ALIVE**: a dead pid is finite, positive and not one of our ancestors, so it landed on the same verdict a live runner does, and the report printed the same *"a runner holds this slot — leave it alone"*. ⚖️ **`lane-runner-v3.sh` DISAGREES, and it is the authority because it is the pidfile's WRITER: its poll loop tests `kill -0`, and on a dead pid it salvages the run to `tasks/failed/CRASHED-*` and removes the pidfile. Two readers of one file; only the runner's answer was true.** 📊 **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING, control asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1 — the minted pid was confirmed gone by `kill -0` before anything was believed): a spawned child allowed to EXIT and one still RUNNING both return `true` from `isSlotBusy`, byte-identically.** ⚙️ **REACHABLE exactly when the runner dies mid-run — a reboot (F-2343-1's 37h50m outage), a `kill -9`, a crash — because the self-heal is performed by the thing that died. The window is "as long as the runner is dead".** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: LATENT, realised cost ZERO — `tasks/running/` was EMPTY and all four lanes read USABLE on the day this landed, so no lane was ever wrongly frozen.** 🚫 **THE VERDICT IS DELIBERATELY NOT FLIPPED, and the restraint is MEASURED rather than timid.** (1) A false NOT-busy is the DESTRUCTIVE direction — it lets a fire refill an occupied lane and `reset --hard` over live output (Mistake #2), which is exactly what this file's own fail-safe comment exists to prevent. (2) **At the only moment it fires, USABLE would be the WORSE answer anyway**: a dead holder means the RUNNER is dead, so a fire told *"refill freely"* queues a master into a lane nothing can consume — F-2343-1's armed trap, where the board looks MORE worked the instant it becomes incapable of working. ➡️ **So the cure DECLARES and does not CLEAR: same verdict, same exit code, one more fact — a BUSY row now names its cause (`live` / `stale` / `unverifiable`), and a corpse routes you to the `runner :` line (§2.0c) and the §2.0b restart instead of to a refill. Do NOT hand-remove the pidfile.** ⓘ **Only `ESRCH` counts as absence: `EPERM` means the process EXISTS and is merely not ours, and reading it as gone is the destructive direction. The declaration prints on EVERY BUSY cause including the ordinary one (F-2208-1), but is scoped to the BUSY branch — an always-on line across a 30-row fleet listing is the noise that decays a declaration into a formality.** ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): 6/6 byte-identical stdout and rc across `--all`, all four lanes and `--json`, with the control arm asserting it produced 28,421 B before any diff was believed.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/lane-usable-stale-pid-guard.test.mjs` — 11 arms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`.** 🔬 **TEETH, 11 variants on scratch copies