 dies on a server-side `API Error: 529 Overloaded` after MINUTES.** Measured s2480: `10` such fires (7 × 529, 3 × 500) across 5 days, of which `8` ran LONGER than 9 s and therefore land in NEITHER of this clause's two named buckets. **Two of them are s2480's own immediate predecessors — 21:48:42→21:52:35 (233 s) and 21:57:35→22:01:13 (218 s), both `rc=1`, both logging nothing but the 529** — and they are the same outage the attended salvage commits on `lane/b` and `lane/d` cite by name. **They took no lock and stranded nothing, so there was no F-2403-1 cascade to recover; the archive chain was intact.** ⓘ **A 529 is transient and self-clearing (s2480 ran normally four minutes later): note it in the handoff, do NOT raise a wall on it, and do NOT restart the runner for it.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is measured rather than lazy: every one of these states is a LAWFUL, service-side condition the factory correctly rides out, so a red would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). The cure is the sentence you are reading.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS for this streak's long-running question: twenty-odd fires have asked *how can this measurement be WRONG?* — a swallow, a guard, a cwd, a stale tree, a filename, a timezone, a window. This one is ARITHMETICALLY RIGHT AND CAUSALLY WRONG. A census bucket carries a COUNT and a STORY, only the count is measured, and the story is what the next reader acts on. When you bank a bucket, state how you would have told these causes apart — and if the answer is "I read the count and inferred the cause", label it as inference.** ⚠️ **RARE IS NOT CHEAP, BECAUSE THE FAILURE IS UNBOUNDED AND COMPOUNDING: every successor re-reads the same corpse and exits again, so the stall ends only when the lock crosses 45 min.** **2026-07-20: `s752` locked 06:54, died `rc=1` 07:01:17; fires at 07:06 (46 s) and 07:22 (98 s) BOTH stood down citing that same lock at 12 and 28 min old — a ~38-minute stall.** **2026-07-30: `s1260` died 15:58:13; its 16:03 successor exited in 91 s having checked three signals — owning commit 13 min old, live-looking pids, a fresh handoff-script mtime — every one of which a dead fire also produces.** 🧩 **AND THE LOCK IS NOT ALL THAT STRANDS: a fire killed between its lock commit and its handoff leaves the PREVIOUS fire’s line-1 dropped and unarchived (§4), so the takeover owes that recovery too** — s2203 died 4 min after locking and left s2202’s entire handoff in no bullet at all; s2204 restored it verbatim from the owning commit in its own lock commit. Land a dead fire’s stranded deliverables **verbatim** rather than rewriting its account of its own work. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it is why this clause exists at all rather than a mechanism: the cure was already known and had nowhere to live.** It was measured on 2026-07-30, written into an **untracked, per-machine** fire memory whose own *How to apply* line reads *"add the fire-log tail to the §1 lock check"* — and 23 days later `scripts/fire.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/skills/**` still contained **ZERO** mentions of `FIRE END` as a lock signal (re-measured s2204; the 28 hits in `STATUS.md` are all handoff archives, the surface F-1654-1 proved a fire does not read at decision time). **s2204 avoided a third stall only by recalling that memory, not by reading the law.** A cure parked outside the tracked law is a cure the factory does not have — **when you measure one, land it in the surface that governs the moment it applies.**\0122. Otherwise: rewrite line-1 to `ACTIVE <ISO-stamp> (s<N> fire) — <one-line intent>`, commit it (`s<N>: lock ACTIVE — <intent>`), and proceed. A >45-min-stale ACTIVE lock is dead: archive it honestly in your handoff and take over.\0123. **STAMPS COME FROM A COMMAND, NEVER FROM ARITHMETIC (F-1039-2, closed s1053).** Derive every stamp by running `date "+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ"` and pasting the result. Hand-computing keeps drifting stamps into the **future** — s1038 **+46 min**, s1046 **+56/+50 min**, and s1053's own mid-fire refresh **+14 min**, written by a fire that had just read this very finding. That is not cosmetic: §1.1 tells the next fire to **EXIT SILENTLY** while an `ACTIVE` lock is <45 min old, so a future-dated stamp left by a fire that then dies **stalls the entire factory for the skew plus 45 minutes**, and the staleness law's own clock is the thing that is wrong. ⚠️ **AND THE STAMP TEXT IS NOT TRUSTWORTHY, SO DO NOT JUDGE STALENESS BY IT.** Measured s1053: the trailing `Z` is a **mislabel** — Robin's Mac is Bangkok (**UTC+07**), so a `date`-derived stamp reads `02:21Z` when true UTC is `19:21Z`. **The convention is genuinely INCONSISTENT across fires, which is the real hazard:** s1050/s1051/s1052 all stamped **local** (s1050's own text says "it is 00:56 local" against a `00:56+07` commit), while s1047–s1049 were recorded as switching to **true UTC** (`date -u`). Two conventions, ~7 h apart, both labeled `Z`, plus hand-computed errors of **+14 to +56 min**. ➡️ **THEREFORE THE BINDING RULE FOR §1.1 IS: judge a lock's age by its OWNING COMMIT, not by the stamp text** — `git log -1 --format=%cI` on the commit that wrote line-1. That is timezone-proof and arithmetic-proof. A ~7 h "gap" is far more likely a UTC-vs-local stamp than a dead fire; **never take over a lock on stamp text alone.** Write new stamps with plain `date "+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ"` (local, matching the three most recent fires) for continuity, and fix the label only as a deliberate flag-day landing in every fire at once — **never as a drive-by.**\012\012## 2. Triage (strict order — first match wins; DRAIN BUDGET: up to 3 drains per fire or ~35 minutes, whichever first — owner throughput ruling 2026-07-06. **PILE MODE: while ≥4 drains are waiting, the budget rises to 5 drains / ~50 minutes — merge throughput outranks fire brevity when the pile is deep [owner ruling, 2026-07-06 evening]**)\012§2.0 **CODEX-WALL MODE**: if `tasks/CODEX-WALL` exists, read it and obey: NO refills (§2E suspended), NO re-queues of wall-class failures; drains/gates/merges/assayer/bookkeeping/authoring CONTINUE (Claude-side, unaffected). Once per fire, establish whether the implementer is serving — ⚠️ **BUT NOT WITH A BARE `codex` PROBE. THIS CLAUSE PRESCRIBED EXACTLY THAT UNTIL s1640, AND IT IS STRUCTURALLY INCAPABLE OF EVER REPORTING A WALL LIFTED (F-1636-4, filed s1636, owed three fires, DISCHARGED s1640).** A fire's bare `codex` resolves to `/opt/homebrew/bin/codex` — `which -a codex` finds **only** that one (re-measured s1640) — and it is **0.133.0**, below the **0.144.1** floor `lane-runner-v3.sh:448` names for knowing 5.6 ids (was `:422` before the s2319 F-2319-1 factory-accounting withhold, +26 lines; `:172` before the s1651 codex-client-floor block, +47 lines — cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts), so a `-m gpt-5.6-sol` probe returns HTTP 400 *"requires a newer version"* **whatever the quota is**. The runner NO LONGER invokes bare `codex` — as of s1651 (F-1651-1) it resolves a client meeting the 0.144.1 floor before consuming a master (`lane-runner-v3.sh:449`, was `:423`, `:340`, `:173`), self-healing past a wrong-shell PATH and refusing loudly rather than shredding the queue. Before that fix it invoked bare `codex` but from Robin's nvm-shimmed Terminal, so **PATH decides and the two shells do not share a binary** — which is why the s1631 wall outlived its cause by ~3 hours. ➡️ **PRIMARY INSTRUMENT — ASK THE ARTIFACT THE FACTORY ACTUALLY PRODUCED. IT COSTS NOTHING AND HAS NO BINARY QUESTION AT ALL: the newest `tasks/runs/*.log` banner.** Every run log opens with `OpenAI Codex v<ver>` + `model: <id>` (verified s1640 against the then-live `20260810-212739` lane-d log: `v0.145.0`, `model: gpt-5.6-sol`). A run whose banner names the model and which does **not** die on a usage-limit message proves the implementer served at that timestamp — which is the entire question the probe was trying to answer. **FALLBACK, only when no run is recent enough to speak:** probe with a binary that meets the floor, **resolved rather than remembered** — iterate `~/.nvm/versions/node/*/bin/codex`, keep one whose `--version` is ≥ 0.144.1, and invoke it through **node** (`execFileSync`), because the bash gate refuses a direct binary path (measured s1640; the gate denies YOU, not the factory). 🚫 **DO NOT resolve that fallback by picking the highest node version: `v24.14.0`'s codex is BROKEN TODAY — its vendored binary is missing outright (`ENOENT`), where F-1636-4 recorded it as merely old at `0.115.0`, so this rotted inside four fires — and "newest node" therefore selects a non-functional client whose failure is indistinguishable from a wall.** Only `v23.11.1` (**0.145.0**) currently qualifies, but resolve it, never paste it. ⚖️ **A bare-`codex` failure is evidence about the FIRE SHELL only and is a known-false wall signal: never raise or re-raise a wall on it.** Re-raise only by quoting a **fresh run log dying on a usage-limit message** (F-1636-4's standing rule). On a verdict of serving: delete the flag, re-queue wall-class failures once each (these do NOT count toward the twice-then-escalate rule), resume refills, and note CODEX-RESUMED in the handoff.\012§2.0b **A STOPPED FACTORY MAY BE A FIRE'S OWN PROCESS — MEASURE `tty`/`ppid` BEFORE YOU ESCALATE TO THE OWNER, AND RESTART IT WITH `bash scripts/start-lane-runner.sh`, NEVER BY HAND (F-1652-1's owed clause, discharged s1653; the sibling half is F-1653-1).** ⚠️ **THE DIAGNOSTIC FIRST, because it is one command and it decides who owns the problem: `ps -o pid,ppid,tty,stat,lstart -p <runner-pid>`.** ⛔ **BUT NOT IN THAT FORM — BOTH HALVES OF THIS SECTION'S RECIPE ARE REFUSED BY YOUR OWN PERMISSION GATE, AND THIS IS THE FOURTH AND FIFTH REFUSED FAMILY AFTER `bash` (F-2350-1), `npm run test:*` (F-2359-1) AND `sqlite3` (F-2360-1) (F-2400-1, measured s2400).** 🔬 **CONTROL-ISOLATED, each arm run BARE — no `cd`, no pipe, no command substitution, because s2350 records compound commands as a confound — with positive controls in the SAME shell so a refusal cannot be a blanket state: `ps -o pid,ppid,tty,stat,lstart -p 1` → `This command requires approval`; `kill -0 1` (signal 0 sends NOTHING, so this probes the binary and not the act) → `This command requires approval`; CONTROL `ls -d tasks/running` → RAN; CONTROL `which -a codex`, §2.0's own prescribed probe a few paragraphs up → RAN, printing `/opt/homebrew/bin/codex`.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: this is NOT a false green — the refusal is LOUD — and the realised cost on the day it was found was ZERO, because the runner read `ALIVE` and §2.0b was never entered.** 🎯 **What earns it a clause is the DIRECTION at the only two moments it fires. §2.0b exists to abolish an over-politeness that cost ~2h26m of a ~5h03m outage, and its cure is *"measure `tty`/`ppid` BEFORE you escalate to the owner"* — so a fire that types the prescribed `ps` gets a refusal, and the diagnostic fails toward ESCALATING, the exact reflex this section was written to kill. Then the restart: a fire that HAS diagnosed a corpse cannot `kill -TERM` it, so the outage PERSISTS with the fire holding the only cure and unable to type it. Both are F-2350-1's polarity, one section over.** ➡️ **THE FALLBACK, and it is the only thing you need from this clause — route through `node`, which IS allowlisted (the gate denies YOU, not the factory):** `node -e "const{execFileSync}=require('child_process');console.log(execFileSync('ps',['-o','pid,ppid,tty,stat,lstart','-p','<pid>'],{encoding:'utf8'}))"` — and for the restart, `spawnSync('kill',['-TERM','<pid>'])`, or `process.kill(<pid>,'SIGTERM')`, which needs no binary at all. **Both routes were exercised s2400: the `ps` route printed pid 1's row, and `process.kill(1,0)` reached the OS and returned `EPERM` — an answer from the kernel, not a refusal from the gate.** ⓘ **`which -a codex` is UNAFFECTED and needs no wrapper; do not wrap what already runs.** 🔒 **Mechanised so the measurement cannot decay into prose: the `ps -o` and `kill -TERM` families are now families 4 and 5 of `scripts/law-bash-prescription-guard.test.mjs`, whose patterns select the two IMPERATIVES and deliberately match none of the prose that merely narrates `kill -0`/`kill -9`.** ⚠️ **BUT READ WHAT THAT GUARD DOES AND DOES NOT BUY, because it was measured before it was landed and it is LESS than it looks: it is FILE-SCOPED, and `fire.md` already carried `execFileSync` once — in §2.0d, thousands of words from here, naming `bash` and `npm` and neither of these — so adding the families does NOT red arm 1 and never could. The guard scored §2.0b as "documented" on the strength of a fallback written for other commands entirely. F-2360-1 named that same harm for `sqlite3` and did not cure it; it is still not cured, deliberately, because section-scoping means answering "how near is near enough?" — a judgement, and a guard built on a judgement fires on honest prose and is excused into uselessness (F-1460-1). **The cure for the harm is the sentence you are reading; the guard only stops a FUTURE surface from prescribing these with no fallback at all.** **`PPID 1` + `TTY ??` means NOBODY IS SITTING AT IT** — no controlling terminal, reparented to launchd: that is a **fire's own detached runner**, not a process in Robin's Terminal, and it is **yours to stop and restart**. It is also the **CORRECT** state for a healthy runner, not a symptom — re-measured s1653 against the live pid 52675 (`PPID 1`, `TTY ??`, `Ss`, started 10:11:02). 💰 **s1651 read that state as a reason to stand off and escalated instead; s1652 measured it in one `ps` and took over. The gap between those two readings cost ~2h26m of a ~5h03m total outage, all of it AFTER the diagnosis was complete and the cure had landed.** 🚫 **AND THE ESCALATION ITSELF WAS UNRUNNABLE, which is the part that makes this a law rather than a note: with a runner alive, `bash scripts/lane-runner-v3.sh` CANNOT succeed** — `:146` fails to `mkdir` the held lock, `:149` counts `others=1`, `:157` exits 1 with *"another instance running. **Remove if stale.**"* — and a process with **no TTY cannot be Ctrl+C'd**, so the obvious way to clear it was closed too. ⚠️ **NEVER follow that error text: do NOT `rmdir tasks/.runner.lock`.** Re-read the code (s1653): `:146–:158` **already self-heals a genuine corpse** — no other runner process → `rmdir` + retake, automatically. **So a lock that is actually held means a LIVE instance, always**, and clearing it by hand yields **two runners on the same queues**. To replace a live runner, `kill -TERM <pid>`: its trap at `:161` releases the lock cleanly (measured 4.5 s) (**all four coordinates in this clause were `:120`/`:123`/`:131`/`:135` until s2343, every one rotted by exactly +26 and INVISIBLE to `law-pointer-guard` because a bare `:NNN` carries no filename for it to resolve — re-based by RE-GREPPING the lock block and reading `:145–:162` by eye, not by carrying a delta; cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts**). ➡️ **THEN START IT WITH THE SCRIPT, WHICH EXISTS SO THE RECIPE CANNOT BE MISTYPED UNDER PRESSURE:** `bash scripts/start-lane-runner.sh` resolves a client meeting `CODEX_FLOOR` (**resolve, never paste** — `v24.14.0`'s codex is an `ENOENT` corpse, so "newest node" selects a non-functional client whose failure is indistinguishable from a wall), prepends its bin dir to `PATH`, scrubs `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` + `CLAUDECODE`, starts **detached**, and verifies `PPID 1`. `--check` reports the environment and starts nothing — it stays runnable while the factory is healthy, so the one time it matters is not the first time it runs. 🔑 **WHY EACH CLAUSE, since the owed clause overstated the first and never named the second — re-measured s1653, not inherited:** **codex is NO LONGER load-bearing** (s1651's `resolve_codex_bin` at `lane-runner-v3.sh:197` picks a floor-meeting client **before** the master-consuming `mv`, so dispatch self-heals a wrong-shell PATH); but **`node`/`npm`/`npx` STILL ARE**, because the runner passes `"$codex_bin"` explicitly and **never modifies `PATH`** (`:384`) — so every `npm run build` / `npx tsc` / `npx playwright` / `node --test` a lane task runs resolves from the **runner's inherited** PATH. A fire shell has no nvm (`PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin:…`, `node v26.4.0`, `codex 0.133.0`), so a hand-started runner silently runs every lane gate under a **different interpreter than the one that produced every baseline on the board** — proven live by `--check`, which flips the child from `v26.4.0` to `v23.11.1`. (Whether v26 actually breaks the build is **UNMEASURED**; the defect is the uncontrolled swap under the evidence, not a known break.) And **`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` must be scrubbed in the opposite direction**: `playwright.config.ts` keys `workers: isFireShell ? 1 : undefined` on its **presence**, so leaving it set pins every **LANE** run to one worker — §3.1's mechanism firing exactly where it forbids. 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/runner-restart-recipe.test.sh`** (rooted in `test:ledger-guards`), whose six red paths were each **proven by manufacturing the defect** on scratch copies; it also reds on `CODEX_FLOOR` drift between the helper and the runner, which is the one thing that can silently rot because the helper deliberately does not source the runner. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it generalises past runners: when a predecessor declines an action for a stated reason, the reason is a CLAIM — Mistake #4's subject — so re-measure it before inheriting the restraint.** Two fires and five hours were spent honouring a premise that one `ps` call refuted.\012§2.0c **AND A STOPPED FACTORY MAY HAVE NO PROCESS AT ALL — §2.0b's DIAGNOSTIC PRESUPPOSES A `<runner-pid>` YOU DO NOT HAVE, SO NOTHING IN TRIAGE EVER SENDS YOU TO LOOK (F-2343-1, measured and cured s2343).** §2.0b is reactive: every one of its arms — `ps -o pid,ppid,tty,stat,lstart -p <runner-pid>`, the held-lock reading, `kill -TERM` — begins with a runner you have already noticed. **When the runner does not exist, there is no pid to measure, no lock to read, and no trigger anywhere in §2 telling a fire to ask.** 🔌 **THE MECHANISM IS A REBOOT, AND IT IS ASYMMETRIC BY CONSTRUCTION: `com.goldrush.fire.plist` is a launchd job, so THE FIRES COME BACK. The runner has NO launchd job, so it does NOT.** Measured s2343: `kern.boottime` = **Thu Aug 27 16:00:27 2026**, and `logs/runner-headless.log`'s final write is **15:46** — fourteen minutes earlier, the trap at `lane-runner-v3.sh:161` firing on shutdown and writing its ordinary, innocent-looking *"stopped (background tasks finish on their own)"*. **The log of a machine going down is byte-indistinguishable from a clean deliberate stop**, which is why s2343 checked `kern.boottime` before deciding whether the owner had stopped it on purpose — and that check is what converted "do not touch, this may be his" into "restart it, this was the power". 📊 **BLAST RADIUS: 36 fires booted after that reboot (7 of 22 on 08-27, 24 of 24 on 08-28, 5 of 5 on 08-29, anchored on `^[fire-runner] .*FIRE START`); 35 of them ran, found a decapitated factory, and did not notice — 37h50m dead. Their logs carry ZERO mentions of `health-watch`, `start-lane-runner`, `runner : DEAD` or `runner_pids`.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: the realised cost was ZERO. The board was dry throughout — 0 queued, 0 in-flight, 0 fire-authorable — so nothing was starved, no drain was missed, and every DRY verdict those 35 fires published was TRUE.** This is not a false green about work that existed; it is an instrument that cannot tell a factory AT REST from a factory that CANNOT RUN. 🎯 **WHAT EARNS IT A CLAUSE IS THE DIRECTION AND THE ARMED TRAP: §2E's dry-board allowance lets a fire author up to TWO masters and `cp` them into a lane queue — and with no runner they would sit there forever while EVERY prescribed §2F instrument kept reporting health.** `dry-board-probe` reads `tasks/done/` and says DRY (correct — a queued master is not a done-move); `lane-usable` reads branches and says USABLE (correct); `tasks/running/` is empty (correct, and it is the *symptom*); and `authorable-candidates` would report FEWER planned leaves, because queueing flips them out of `planned` — **so the board would look MORE worked the instant it became incapable of working.** 🔁 **This is the known sibling one level worse: a LIVE runner refusing a queued master at least writes a refusal per poll cycle (s2257: `e10s-1c-ember-shore-inert-landing`, 5h15m, 1,100 logged refusals, and the prescribed cure is `grep -c "REFUSE <name>" logs/runner-headless.log`). A DEAD runner writes NOTHING, and that cure's own trigger — a non-empty queue beside an empty `tasks/running/` — is STRUCTURALLY INCAPABLE of firing here, because on a dry board the queue is empty too.** ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING, AS PART OF §2 TRIAGE ON EVERY FIRE: run `bash scripts/health-watch.sh status` — two seconds, one screen — and READ THE `runner :` LINE.** It prints `ALIVE` or `DEAD`, annotates `⚠️ STALE` when the live pid predates commits to `lane-runner-v3.sh` (F-2137-1: alive is not the same as current), and hands you the queue/in-flight counts in the same breath. **On `DEAD`: confirm with `. scripts/runner-processes.sh; runner_pids` (empty = dead), then restart with `bash scripts/start-lane-runner.sh` per §2.0b — NEVER by hand, and NEVER by `rmdir tasks/.runner.lock`.** Run `--check` first if you want the environment reported without starting anything. 🕳️ **THE INSTRUMENT WAS NEVER MISSING — ONLY ITS READER WAS, WHICH IS THIS FACTORY'S MOST-REPEATED FINDING AND THE THIRD TIME IT HAS LANDED ON A TOOL THAT ALREADY EXISTED.** `health-watch.sh` has reported `runner : ALIVE|DEAD` all along, **`CLAUDE.md` §1 makes it read-first #4** (*"the live board in 2 seconds"*), and it even carries its own auto-restart arm (`:143–:154`, routed through `start-lane-runner.sh` since F-1653-1). **Measured s2343 across the six `LAW_SURFACES` — imported from `scripts/law-surfaces.mjs`, not transcribed: `health-watch` appears `1` time in `CLAUDE.md` and `0` times in `scripts/fire.md`, the law that governs every fire's triage.** So the attended resume protocol prescribes it and the fire protocol never did — and **no launchd job references `health-watch` or `lane-runner` at all**, so that auto-restart arm has never once been scheduled. **F-2153-1 (`authorable-candidates`) and F-2204-1 (the `FIRE END` lock signal) are the same story: the cure existed, in a working tool, and had nowhere in the law a fire reads at decision time to live.** 🚫 **NO GUARD IS PROPOSED, AND THE RESTRAINT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN LAZY: a red on "runner not alive" would fire during every legitimate reboot, every deliberate `kill -TERM`, every window between stopping and restarting — a routine, LAWFUL state — and would be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). The correct surface is a triage READ, not a gate.** 🔺 **THE PERSISTENT FIX IS AN OWNER CALL AND IS ON THE DESK AS F-2343-2: a launchd job for the runner (or a scheduled `health-watch.sh watch`, whose remediation arm already exists) would make this self-healing across reboots. That is a permanent job on Robin's machine, so a fire does not install one — it restarts the runner and asks.** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it sharpens the streak's own question: twenty fires have asked *how can this subject set come back EMPTY?* — a throw, a guard, a `catch`, a stale tree, a cwd. This is the axis under all of them: AN EMPTY QUEUE IS THE SHAPE OF A HEALTHY BOARD AND ALSO THE SHAPE OF A BOARD NOTHING CAN CONSUME, and no probe that reads only the WORK can tell them apart. When every instrument agrees the board is at rest, ask once whether anything is still ALIVE to move it — the question is about the CONSUMER, not the corpus, and it costs one command.**\012§2.0d ⛔ **AND THAT ONE COMMAND IS REFUSED BY YOUR OWN PERMISSION GATE — EVERY `bash scripts/*.sh` THIS LAW PRESCRIBES IS UNRUNNABLE IN THE FORM IT IS WRITTEN, INCLUDING §2.0c's BINDING TRIAGE READ AND §2.0b's EMERGENCY RESTART (F-2350-1, measured s2350).** §2.0c landed ONE FIRE EARLIER and makes `bash scripts/health-watch.sh status` a duty on EVERY fire. **s2350 typed it as prescribed and got `This command requires approval`.** 🔬 **CONTROL-ISOLATED BEFORE IT WAS BELIEVED, because the first two refusals were compound commands and `cd … && …` is a confound: the BARE form `bash scripts/health-watch.sh status`, no `cd`, no pipe, is refused on its own, while compound commands built from allowlisted binaries (`cd … && git …`, `cd … && node …`) passed dozens of times in the same fire. The refused token is `bash` itself.** 📊 **MEASURED, not inferred: `89` allow entries across the two settings files — `74` in `.claude/settings.json` (TRACKED) and `15` in `.claude/settings.local.json` (untracked) — and **ZERO** permit `bash`. `Bash(node:*)` IS allowed.** ⚙️ **IT IS STRUCTURAL FOR EVERY FIRE, NOT AN ARTIFACT OF ONE SESSION: `fire-runner.sh` invokes `"$CLAUDE_BIN" -p "$(cat scripts/fire.md)" --model "$FIRE_MODEL"` with NO `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, so every fire that has ever run has been under this gate.** 📋 **BLAST RADIUS, censused over the six `LAW_SURFACES` imported from `scripts/law-surfaces.mjs` (not transcribed): `8` prescribed `bash scripts/*.sh` invocations across `3` surfaces — `scripts/fire.md` 6, `CLAUDE.md` 1, `.claude/skills/playtest-intake/SKILL.md` 1 — naming `5` distinct scripts: `health-watch.sh`, `start-lane-runner.sh`, `lane-runner-v3.sh`, `deploy.sh`, `dashboard-gen.sh`.** ⛔ **ONE OF THOSE FIVE IS A CARVE-OUT AND THE GENERIC FALLBACK MUST NOT BE APPLIED TO IT — `deploy.sh` IS `PUBLISH-GATED: DO NOT WRAP` (F-2371-6, filed by s2371 against ITSELF for wrapping it; DISCHARGED s2372 by the next fire, which hit the same case and took the carve-out instead).** Every other refused script in this census is a READ or a LOCAL REPAIR, where a permission stop costs the factory a duty and the node route restores it — that is the whole harm §2.0d exists to prevent. **`deploy.sh` PUBLISHES TO THE PUBLIC WEB.** There a permission stop is not an obstacle to route around: **it IS the owner's live control over what reaches the family's browser**, and the standing fire memory has said since s1332 that wrapping deploy is the one case where the node trick must not be used. ➡️ **THEREFORE: attempt `bash scripts/deploy.sh` exactly as DEPLOY LAW prescribes; if the gate refuses, DO NOT re-run it through `node` — record the refusal in your handoff and let the owner publish.** ⓘ **s2372 executed precisely this: it merged gameplay-affecting code (`a73790d9a`), attempted the prescribed form, was refused, and did NOT wrap it — the first fire to take the carve-out prospectively rather than discover it afterwards.** ⚖️ **Note what this does NOT weaken: the content was still fully gated and merged, and nothing about the drain waits on the owner. Only PUBLICATION does, which is the point.** 🔒 **MECHANISED SO IT CANNOT BE ARGUED AWAY, which was F-2371-6's own stated condition (*"it must be expressible to `law-bash-prescription-guard` or it will be argued away"*): that guard now carries a `PUBLISH_GATED` set and REDS if a surface prescribing `deploy.sh` loses this carve-out token, so deleting the sentence breaks the battery rather than quietly restoring the defect.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: this is NOT a false green and NOTHING silently returned a wrong answer — the refusal is LOUD, and s2350 discharged both duties anyway via the fallback below. `deploy.sh`'s case was already known (the DEPLOY-law memory), so the class is not new.** 🎯 **WHAT EARNS IT A CLAUSE IS THE DIRECTION AT THE TWO MOMENTS THAT MATTER: a refusal reads as "I cannot do this", and §2.0c's read fails toward SKIPPING the liveness check — the exact silent blind spot §2.0c exists to close and prices at 37h50m / 35 fires; §2.0b's restart fails toward the OUTAGE PERSISTING, with the fire holding the only cure and unable to type it.** ➡️ **THE FALLBACK, AND IT IS THE ONLY THING YOU NEED FROM THIS CLAUSE — route through `node`, which IS allowlisted (the gate denies YOU, not the factory):** `node -e "const{execFileSync}=require('child_process');console.log(execFileSync('bash',['scripts/health-watch.sh','status'],{encoding:'utf8'}))"` — and the same shape for `start-lane-runner.sh --check`, for `npm run test:ledger-guards` (whose own battery chains SIX `bash scripts/*.test.sh` legs), and for every other refused leg. **Both were exercised s2350: `health-watch` returned `runner : ALIVE`, and `start-lane-runner.sh --check` returned `rc=0, 1013 B` reporting `codex 0.149.1` and `environment is READY; started nothing`.** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT IS THIS FACTORY'S MOST-REPEATED FINDING FOR THE THIRD TIME IN THE SAME FILE: THE CURE ALREADY EXISTED, IN THIS LAW, ONE SECTION UP, AND THE NEW CLAUSES NEVER ADOPTED IT.** §2.0 has said since s1640 — *"invoke it through **node** (`execFileSync`), because the bash gate refuses a direct binary path (measured s1640; the gate denies YOU, not the factory)"* — and §2.0b (s1653) and §2.0c (s2343) were both written afterwards, both prescribe `bash …`, and **neither carries the word `node`.** That is F-2153-1 (`authorable-candidates` named in zero law surfaces) and F-2204-1 (the `FIRE END` lock signal parked in untracked memory) a third time: **a known cure is only as good as the surface a fire reads AT THE MOMENT IT APPLIES.** When you write a clause prescribing a command, type the command and run it before you land the clause. 🔺 **THE PERSISTENT FIX IS AN OWNER CALL AND IS ON THE DESK AS F-2350-3: adding `Bash(bash scripts/*)` to `.claude/settings.json` would make the prescribed forms simply work. That file is TRACKED, so a fire editing it would be SELF-GRANTING its own permissions — a thing a fire must not do (§7.3). The node fallback costs nothing and needs no ruling, so the factory is not blocked either way.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/law-bash-prescription-guard.test.mjs`: for every `bash scripts/*.sh` a law surface prescribes, either `bash` is allowlisted (and the prescription is runnable as written) or that surface must carry the node fallback. Mechanical, self-extending, and it retires itself the day the allowlist changes.** ⏫ **WIDENED s2359 (F-2359-1) — AND THE WIDENING IS THE LESSON: THAT GUARD SELECTED ON ONE COMMAND FAMILY WHILE THE CLAUSE ABOVE NAMES TWO.** This section has told you since s2350 to route `npm run test:ledger-guards` through node — and the guard censused only `bash scripts/*.sh`, so it passed **8/8 green while blind to the npm half of its own finding**. 🔬 **MEASURED s2359 with the controls isolated, because `cd … && …` is a known confound: `npm run test:ledger-guards` and `npm run test:node-guards`, BARE and unpiped, both return `This command requires approval`; CONTROL `npm -v` runs and prints `11.17.0`; and the node fallback returns **rc=0, 871 node assertions + 83 bash legs**. So it is not `npm` that is refused — `npm run build`, `npm test` and `npm -v` are all allowlisted — it is these two forms, which are the batteries F-1300-4 and F-1460-1 make mandatory.** ⚖️ **NOT a false green and the realised cost is ZERO: this surface DOES state the npm fallback, so its reader was served. What was missing was the mechanism — the protection was a convention nothing enforced, which is exactly how the `fire-runner.sh` pointer rotted at s1456.** ➡️ **The guard now censuses BOTH families (12 arms) and retires each one independently as the allowlist grows. 💡 REUSABLE: when a finding names N members of a class and its guard enumerates one, the guard is not a partial cure — IT CERTIFIES THE UNCOVERED MEMBERS AS CLEAN.**\012Drains within one fire remain STRICTLY SERIAL: each one fully gated + committed before the next begins (never batch-gate). After each completed drain, refresh your line-1 ACTIVE stamp (keeps the 45-min staleness check honest) and re-triage from the top — a new failed run or fresher priority may outrank the queue you planned. Non-drain actions (bookkeeping, correctives, re-queues) don't count against the budget.\012§2H **HEAT ARENAS:** prepare every detached arena with `npm ci`, never `npm install`, and prove the arena clean with `git status --short` before minting its engine probe. `npm ci` installs the committed dependency tree without rewriting the lockfile; the narrowed engine identity excludes package and lock files, so an accidental lockfile rewrite no longer rotates a reel's identity either.\012A. **Uncommitted bookkeeping** (host-side STATUS/task edits from attended sessions): commit them first — this may be your whole first act some fires. **ATTENDED-COEXISTENCE LAW (s61 correction, 2026-07-07 ~12:55, after a 74-min drain stall): "attended session live" is NOT a no-drain condition — that over-politeness starved the board while the owner watched. The precise rule: drains are blocked only by WHO OWNS THE DIRT. Check `git status` tracked-dirt ownership: runner output (src/e2e files from a done-move) = DRAINABLE regardless of attended activity; attended dirt (docs/tasks/specs/logs) = disjoint, drain anyway with path-scoped commits; STOP only if attended holds the STATUS line-1 lock or uncommitted src/ edits exist that belong to NO done-move. When in doubt: drain the runner's output, leave the attended files alone.** **AMENDMENT (s61, 2026-07-08 morning, after the 29-stand-down night jam): three clarifications with the force of law — (1) MAIN-SLOT runner output is ALWAYS uncommitted working-tree dirt + a done-move file; the runner auto-commits LANES ONLY. Uncommitted src WITH a matching done-move in tasks/done/ = runner output = DRAINABLE. The heuristic "uncommitted src = attended hand-work" is FALSE for the main slot and caused a full night of blocked drains. (2) done/ task files are ALWAYS untracked — untracked done-moves prove nothing about authorship. (3) a RESIDENT attended pid with zero fs-writes >30 min = IDLE, not live-driving; idle attended NEVER blocks drains. Match dirt to done-moves by the task's TOUCH-ONLY list; drain what matches.** **POST-WALL RECOVERY LAW (s61, 2026-07-08 ~15:30, after SIX vanished casualties — 055/057/ac-02/e2-science-tree/ss-02/audio-002 all lost silently to wall gaps + lane resets): the FIRST fire after any codex wall/auth gap runs a mandatory recovery sweep — (1) every rc1/CRASHED in tasks/failed/ from the gap window: verify the master's content against main by FILE-PROBE (never memory/messages); absent → re-queue the master; (2) every done-move of the prior 6 hours: same verification; (3) write the sweep's findings into the handoff. Walls eat silently; the sweep is not optional.** **BACKUP LAW (owner ruling 2026-07-07): if `git remote get-url origin` succeeds, `git push origin main` after every handoff commit (plus current lane branches weekly). Never force-push — force is deny-listed. If the push fails (auth/network), note it in the handoff and continue; backup failures never block drains.** **DEPLOY LAW (owner ruling 2026-07-07): after the backup push, IF this fire MERGED gameplay-affecting code, run `bash scripts/deploy.sh` — it self-skips when wrangler/auth/project are missing and never blocks; the family plays the latest gated build at the Pages URL. Bookkeeping-only fires skip deploy.** **DEPLOY MIRROR LAW:** /opt/goldrush is a positive runtime allowlist; a new runtime dependency means extending that allowlist and passing deploy-mirror-allowlist.test.mjs, never adding an exclude.\012B. **Runner output to drain**: done-moves in tasks/done/ newer than the last handoff, or dirty main from a finished main-slot task → GATE IT (see §3). One drain per fire. Priority: main-slot output > lane done-moves (serial, oldest first) > art raws processing. **EXCEPTION (owner-priority, 2026-07-06): a lane drain flagged OWNER-PRIORITY in HANDOVER §4 outranks main-slot output — starving owner-named work is worse than a one-fire main delay. Currently flagged: lane-a demo-profiles (Demo Day; Robin asked by name).**\012C. **Failed runs**: tasks/failed/ entries whose run log tail shows "turn interrupted" + large token count = Codex credit wall → re-queue from tasks/<name>.md master, at most once per fire; two consecutive interrupts on the same task = STOP and flag Robin in the handoff. Any other rc≠0: READ the log before deciding (real failures get findings written, not blind retries).\012D. **Standing corrections owed** (check STATUS "Next fires" and "owes" lists): test correctives, LEDGER upkeep, contract wiring for processed art, spec refreshes. **Plus the ASSAYER duty (standing, 2026-07-06): if `assets/crafting-queue/pending/` is non-empty, verdict ONE order this fire — generate the item WITHIN the profile's epoch contract (currently contract.v1: vocabulary, stat caps), validate contractVerdict + simVerdict honestly (canon §9.2/9.4 applies — no firearms, naming rules), write `approved/<id>.json` or `rejected/<id>.json` matching the 033 fixture shapes exactly (they are the reference), one-paragraph note in the handoff. Generator proposes, contract disposes — reject rather than stretch vocabulary. Item APPLICATION stays unwired until its slice, so verdicts are display-safe. First live case: Robin's multi-target Spark Rig order (07:16Z).** 🔎 **AND BEFORE YOU TREAT ANY `stopped`/`superseded`/`blocked` LEAF AS AN OPEN THREAD, ASK THE INSTRUMENT INSTEAD OF READING THE JSON: `node scripts/drain-block-check.mjs <taskfile>` PRINTS THE CLOSURE REASON UNDER EVERY SPELLING (F-1582-1, s1582).** It resolves across all six accepted keys plus the `note_s*` family and prints the whole note, the refusal arm, the BACKLOG context line and what to do about it; `--all` lists every blocked leaf AND every terminal-closed one by name. ⚠️ **NEVER conclude "no record" from the absence of one field name.** That is F-1261-4 — refuted in s1274, guarded ever since by `scripts/goal-closure-reason.test.mjs` — and it recurred anyway: s1580 flagged `eight-winds-wiring-e2-enemies` as an orphan for having *"no `drainNotes` at all"*, s1581 carried it, s1582 answered it in one command from a `stopNote` + `reason` that had been sitting there since s1324. ⓘ **Field-name lookup is the wrong instrument for every reader, every time — measured s1582: 27 distinct closure-field spellings across 723 leaves** (`drainNotes` 113 · `note` 71 · `reason` 40 · `stopNote` 4 · `drainNote` 3 · `notes` 3 · `closedReason` 1 · ~35 one-off `note_sNNNN`). 💡 **The guard for this was green the whole time and prevented nothing, because the misreading happens while a fire reads JSON with its eyes during triage — a moment no battery observes. That is why this is a pointer and NOT a new mechanism: a second resolver would duplicate `drain-block-check`'s and re-commit the very sprawl the existing guard's header warns against.**\012E. **PIPELINE REFILL (owner directive 2026-07-06: "4–5 lanes working constantly")**: after drains, check every lane queue; for each EMPTY queue, `cp` the top unblocked item from `tasks/BACKLOG.md`'s ladder for that lane (respect its GATE lines; stale-check masters older than ~2 days against current main — refresh or flag, never blind-queue). **SALVAGE LIFECYCLE (owner UX ruling 2026-07-06 late): `save/<name>` = re-land pending (counts as waiting, keep visible); when its re-land MERGES, rename to `archive/<name>` in the same fire — archives never count as waiting and never rot anyone's experience. A save/* older than ~3h with a known conflict gets a RE-LAND ruling, not a hand-merge.** **LANE-SAFETY LAW (s61 forensics, 2026-07-06): NEVER refill a lane that has an undrained done-move or a lane branch ahead of main — lane pre-flights `reset --hard`, which DESTROYS unmerged predecessor output (w1-03 light-pass and polish-02 were lost exactly this way; w1-04 survived only as a reflog orphan). Drain the lane first, refill second. Any lane task you AUTHOR must use a drain-gated pre-flight: check the worktree/branch is clean-vs-main before resetting, else STOP and report.** ⚙️ **AND ASK IT WITH THE INSTRUMENT, NOT A FILE-LEVEL EYEBALL (F-1081-9, cured by mechanism s1272 `0db281e0`; the law surface wired up s1278, F-1278-1).** `node scripts/lane-freeze-classify.mjs <branch>` classifies EVERY path the lane’s ahead-commits touched into DUPLICATE / LANE-ONLY / MAIN-ONLY / BOTH-MOVED, **prints the denominator it actually looked at** (`paths=N`), and exits 0 only when the lane holds nothing main has not absorbed (exit 1 = HOLDS n, exit 2 = misuse — read from the code, s1278). ⚠️ **`BOTH-MOVED` is a triage bucket, NOT a loss verdict**: a file lands there whenever main moved at all, *including when main moved by absorbing the lane’s own hunk and adding more.* Ask the one-directional line-level question with `node scripts/lane-absorbed-lines.mjs <branch> <path> [<path> ...]` — ⓘ **the RUNNABLE form and its argument shape are stated here because the law carried only the bare filename until s2392, while `lane-freeze-classify` has printed the full invocation below its own HOLDS banner all along (F-2392-1, measured: `node scripts/lane-absorbed-lines.mjs` appeared 0 times across all six LAW_SURFACES). MINOR and deliberately not inflated — the surrounding sentence is imperative, so a reader was always told WHICH tool to ask; what was missing was only how to type it.** s1271 froze three lanes for days on the coarse file-level read and **two of them were never at risk**, while the third held 140 lines main had never seen — a conservative verdict at the wrong resolution is not safety, it is a different way of being wrong. 🚦 **BUT THAT INSTRUMENT ANSWERS THE SAFETY QUESTION, NOT THE REFILL QUESTION — ASK BOTH (F-1298-4, mechanism landed s1299).** `lane-freeze-classify` asks *would a reset LOSE anything?*; a master's pre-flight asks *is this branch clean vs main?* and **STOPs on any unmerged commit, absorbed or not**. s1298 measured `lane/m4` LOSS-FREE, refilled it, and the runner burned **26,940 tokens for zero edits and zero tests** stopping at exactly that line — a lane can be **safe-to-reset and unusable at the same time**, and the fire had no instrument that said so. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BEFORE ANY REFILL: `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs <slot|branch>`** (or `--all` for the fleet). It resolves slot→branch from `git worktree list` itself (**lane slot names are NOT branch names** — ⚠️ **and the mapping ROTS: this parenthetical read "lane-a is `lane/m3`, lane-b `lane/m4`, lane-c `lane/e2-arsenal`, lane-d `lane/perf`" until s1464 measured it FALSE. `git worktree list` shows `worktrees/lane-a..d` checked out on `lane/a`, `lane/b`, `lane/c`, `lane/d`. All eight branches still EXIST, which is what makes this dangerous — the stale names resolve, they just answer about the wrong ref: `lane/m3` reads `ahead=5 behind=347` and `lane/m4` `ahead=7 behind=982`, so a fire measuring by this prose would conclude lanes a and b HOLD unmerged content and refuse to refill two lanes that are actually `ahead=0` USABLE — a false freeze. F-1464-3.** ➡️ **NEVER read the mapping out of this sentence — resolve it from `git worktree list`, which is what `lane-usable.mjs` does itself), and returns one word: **USABLE** (rc 0, `main..branch` empty + clean → refill freely) · **AHEAD-BUT-ABSORBED** (rc 1 — safe to reset but the pre-flight WILL stop; `--cure` archives the tip to `archive/<branch>-s<N>-absorbed-<sha>` **before** resetting, history first, then verifies `main..branch` is empty) · **HOLDS** (rc 2 — content main never absorbed: drain it or rule on the listed paths by hand; `--cure` REFUSES) · **DIRTY** / **BUSY** (rc 2). ⚠️ **Read the WORD, not just the code** — HOLDS and DIRTY and BUSY share rc 2 deliberately, because all three mean *don't refill* while naming three different owed acts. 💀 **AND A BUSY LANE HAS THREE CAUSES, NOT ONE — A CRASHED-RUNNER CORPSE READS EXACTLY LIKE A LIVE RUNNER (F-2366-1, measured and cured s2366).** The BUSY test established WHO holds the slot and **never asked whether that holder is ALIVE**: a dead pid is finite, positive and not one of our ancestors, so it landed on the same verdict a live runner does, and the report printed the same *"a runner holds this slot — leave it alone"*. ⚖️ **`lane-runner-v3.sh` DISAGREES, and it is the authority because it is the pidfile's WRITER: its poll loop tests `kill -0`, and on a dead pid it salvages the run to `tasks/failed/CRASHED-*` and removes the pidfile. Two readers of one file; only the runner's answer was true.** 📊 **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING, control asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1 — the minted pid was confirmed gone by `kill -0` before anything was believed): a spawned child allowed to EXIT and one still RUNNING both return `true` from `isSlotBusy`, byte-identically.** ⚙️ **REACHABLE exactly when the runner dies mid-run — a reboot (F-2343-1's 37h50m outage), a `kill -9`, a crash — because the self-heal is performed by the thing that died. The window is "as long as the runner is dead".** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: LATENT, realised cost ZERO — `tasks/running/` was EMPTY and all four lanes read USABLE on the day this landed, so no lane was ever wrongly frozen.** 🚫 **THE VERDICT IS DELIBERATELY NOT FLIPPED, and the restraint is MEASURED rather than timid.** (1) A false NOT-busy is the DESTRUCTIVE direction — it lets a fire refill an occupied lane and `reset --hard` over live output (Mistake #2), which is exactly what this file's own fail-safe comment exists to prevent. (2) **At the only moment it fires, USABLE would be the WORSE answer anyway**: a dead holder means the RUNNER is dead, so a fire told *"refill freely"* queues a master into a lane nothing can consume — F-2343-1's armed trap, where the board looks MORE worked the instant it becomes incapable of working. ➡️ **So the cure DECLARES and does not CLEAR: same verdict, same exit code, one more fact 