ould not tell you which corpus its licence came from while its sibling arm had been saying so since s2213.** 🛠️ **CURE: `goalEvidence()` returns `{ source: 'goals' | 'absent', leaves }` — a STRING for F-2212-1's reason — carried in `--json` ALWAYS including the happy path (F-2208-1), with the human channel printing only a DEVIATION to match this file's own `reviewsSource` convention. `absent` DECLARES AND DOES NOT REFUSE (F-2218-1's restraint): a root without `goals.json` is LAWFUL — it is what all 12 legacy fixtures use and how `mainReviews` already treats a non-git root — so refusing there would red every fixture and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1). The parse path is DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE; re-coding a working cure in passing is a drive-by.** ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): all four modes `4/4` byte-identical on stdout and stderr, rc unchanged.** 🔒 **`scripts/master-shipped-goals-source-guard.test.mjs` — 8 tests, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; pre-cure restored verbatim reds 4 of 8 with both reverse controls green.** ⚠️ **A REVERSE CONTROL CORRECTED MY PREDICTION for the first time in three fires: I expected *refusing on `absent`* to red control 5 alone; it reds FOUR arms (2,3,4,5), because arms 2–4 read the absent-state classification a refusal replaces with a banner. Benign — over-coverage, not under — but recorded, because the streak's recent note has been that predictions hold, and one clean fire is not evidence the practice can be dropped.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is an AIMING rule rather than another census shape: A CENSUS'S DECLARED LIMIT IS THE MOST VALUABLE THING IN A HANDOFF — WORTH MORE THAN THE SITE IT NAMES.** s2218 handed over a hedged target (`art-staging-audit.mjs:373`, still unmeasured) *and* an admitted blind spot (*"my census is lexical and single-line"*). **Widening the census on that admission — multi-line guards as well as single-line ones — took its `6` sites to `13` single-line plus `4` multi-line, and found a live one in NEITHER list, because widening changed which FILES were subjects at all, not merely which lines.** When a predecessor tells you what their instrument cannot see, go there before you go to the site they named. ⚖️ **A priced leaf is NOT a closed one** — the tool prints that caveat itself; the price estimates the AUTHORING cost and never renders a verdict on the goal. If the ladder has nothing ready for an idle lane, **AUTHOR the next master yourself** (idle-fire authoring precedent: s41 m3-02, s42 m4-02) — but ONLY from an existing spec slice plus its evidence chain (predecessor's review file, audit verdicts, playtest findings — read them, quote them in the task). Follow the house task pattern (goal / scope / firewall / self-check, gates, sequencing laws), mark the header **FIRE-AUTHORED (attended review welcome)**, max one authored master per fire, no drain-budget cost — ⚖️ AMENDED (owner 2026-08-09, F-1511-5 ruling "Allow 2 masters when dry"): when the board is DRY (no drain existed this fire) and ≥2 lanes sit idle, the cap is TWO authored masters in that cycle; the cap protects quality, not idleness. HARD LIMIT — never invent scope: if the lane's next work has no spec slice, needs a design fork, or bends canon, do NOT author — flag **PIPELINE-DRY: <lane> (needs spec/owner)** in your handoff instead. Refills are non-drain actions (no budget cost). **MAIN THROTTLE (s72 lesson, 2026-07-06): while ≥3 undrained done-moves are waiting, do NOT refill the main queue beyond one small corrective — every main task holds the working tree dirty and blocks ALL lane merges. Drains outrank main throughput; re-queue paused main items (queue-paused/) once the pile is below 2.** 📮 **DISPATCH ORDER IS A LAW, NOT A HABIT — COMMIT THE MASTER AND ITS EVIDENCE FIRST, REFRESH THE LANE SECOND, `cp` THIRD, AND RUN THE MASTER'S OWN CITATION GREP BETWEEN 2 AND 3 (F-1424-3, measured s1424; folded in s1426 after being deferred one fire for a lane collision that has since cleared).** s1424 cured lane-c *before* committing the master, so the lane sat **exactly one commit behind** — the commit carrying the citation the master tells the runner to grep. The runner claimed the slot inside a minute and STOPPED: **44,007 tokens, zero files touched, zero work lost**, saved only because that master wired the grep as a hard STOP. ⚠️ **Curing the message could never have prevented this** — the fault is ordering, and no instrument was being asked about it. ➡️ **AND THE KEY ITSELF MUST BE PROVED BEFORE IT IS WRITTEN DOWN: grep the candidate span against the SOURCE FILE ON MAIN first — if it does not match *there*, it is not a key, it is a paraphrase (F-1425-2).** F-1310-1 says cite by CONTENT because coordinates rot; what neither clause said is that **`grep` is line-oriented and prose wraps**, so a key spanning a line break matches **nowhere, including in the file it was copied from**. s1425 caught this on its own dispatch: the key returned 0 in the lane and its first instinct was a stale lane, but `lane-usable` read `ahead=0 behind=0` and the same grep returned **0 against main too**. That failure is nastier than staleness because **the master specifies 0 to mean "the lane drifted"** — a false STOP whose error message accuses the wrong subject, sending the next fire chasing a phantom refresh. ⓘ **This is not a rare shape: s1426 hit it again while authoring, and all three of its first-draft keys returned 0 on main** — the fire had written the review file itself minutes earlier and its own prose still wrapped. Prefer a span that visibly sits on one line, verify **1 on main**, then verify **1 in the lane** after the refresh, and only then `cp`.\012**GOAL REGISTRATION LAW (owner ruling 2026-07-16): every authored master adds its leaf to `tasks/goals.json` IN THE SAME COMMIT; every drain updates that leaf's status and merge hash IN THE DRAIN COMMIT.** Missing goal-tree bookkeeping means the author/drain duty is unfinished. ⏱️ **AND THE GUARDS THAT JUDGE THAT ROW RUN BEFORE IT EXISTS, SO THEY MUST RUN AGAIN AFTER — `npm run test:ledger-guards`, AS THE LAST ACT OF EVERY FIRE THAT WROTE A LEDGER ROW (F-1300-4, mechanism landed s1301).** This is a property of the prescribed ORDER, not of anyone's carelessness: §3 runs the battery on the MERGED tree, and the merged tree by definition **precedes** the drain-bookkeeping commit — so every guard that reads `tasks/goals.json`, `tasks/BACKLOG.md` or tracked `tasks/**/*.md` evaluates the board **as it was before the fire wrote its own row**. A fire that runs the battery once, exactly as ordered, is therefore **structurally incapable of seeing a defect it is about to introduce**. s1300 wrote `mergeHash: "818aa398"` where `scripts/goal-tracker.test.mjs:80` asserts `/^[0-9a-f]{40}$/`, and `test:node-guards` went **rc=1 on a tree whose code was green**; it was caught only by a re-run that an unrelated edit happened to prompt — luck wearing diligence's clothes. It is the citations-run-LAST rule with a wider denominator. 📋 **The denominator was DERIVED s1301, not inherited — and then WIDENED the same fire when it proved too narrow, which is the more useful half of the story.** First pass asked *which guards read the live LEDGER* and found six: `goal-tracker.test.mjs` (live `goals.json` **and** `tasks/done` — the second source was not in the finding) · `goal-closure-reason.test.mjs` · `ruling-propagation-guard.test.mjs` **and** `ruling-propagation-guard.mjs` · `findings-state-guard.mjs` · `blocker-panel-closed-guard.mjs` · `citation-title-guard.mjs`. ⚠️ **Then s1301's own bookkeeping reddened `test:node-guards` in two ways that those six are blind to** — `gate-caller-audit` (the new npm script was an un-rooted gate) and `law-pointer-guard` (inserting a comment block pushed `workers: isFireShell` from `:30` to `:50`, rotting the coordinate that **this very clause** and `.claude/skills/drain/SKILL.md` both cite). Neither reads the ledger; both were caught only by the full battery. ➡️ **So the real class is not "guards that read the ledger" — it is GUARDS WHOSE SUBJECT A FIRE MUTATES LATE IN ITS OWN RUN**, and a fire mutates three such subjects: ledger rows, **law surfaces** (`scripts/fire.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/skills/**` — every coordinate in them rots when anyone inserts a line above it), and **gate topology** (a new npm script must be rooted or grandfathered with a reason). `test:ledger-guards` is now those **eight** as of s1301, since GROWN DELIBERATELY — **RE-MEASURED s1539 BY RUNNING IT: 13 node test files carrying 97 tests (97 pass / 0 fail, 15.3 s) PLUS 11 chained leaves — ⚠️ ALL FOUR FIGURES SUPERSEDED s2099, SEE THE CORRECTION BELOW** (findings-state, blocker-panel, ruling-propagation, citations, desk-declaration, status-archive-audit, attended-owed-audit, main-lock-gate-guard.sh, janitor-request-rejection.sh, lane-dispatch-safety-guard.sh, nul-audit). The figures this clause carried until now — *eleven guards / 12 leaf files / 38 tests*, true when written (s1334 desk-declaration, s1341 status-archive-audit, s1343 attended-owed-audit) — are RESTATED not deleted, because the drift is the provenance: the set has grown ~2.5x in tests and nothing decayed. 📈 **RE-MEASURED AGAIN s2099 (F-2099-1) — EVERY ONE OF THOSE FOUR FIGURES HAS DRIFTED TOO, IN THE SAME DIRECTION: `18` node test files carrying `180` tests (`180 pass / 0 fail / 0 skipped`, **`49.9 s`**) PLUS **`17`** chained leaves** (was 13 / 97 / 15.3 s / 11 at s1539 — **RESTATED, NOT DELETED**, per the Retention Law: the drift is the provenance, and it is the second cost citation this one fire found stale in the same clause-family). 📈 **RE-MEASURED AGAIN s2414 (F-2414-1) — ALL FOUR FIGURES HAVE DRIFTED A THIRD TIME, AND THIS TIME IT IS THIS CLAUSE’S OWN CLOSING INSTRUCTION THAT WENT UNEXECUTED: `97` node test files carrying `948` assertions (`948 pass / 0 fail`, **`77.2 s`** measured s2414) PLUS **`20`** chained leaves, the last of which contributes a further `83` bash legs** (was `18` / `180` / `49.9 s` / `17` at s2099 — **RESTATED, NOT DELETED**, because the sequence IS the finding: files `13 → 18 → 97`, assertions `97 → 180 → 948`, leaves `11 → 17 → 20`). ⚖️ **PRICED HONESTLY, BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS READING IS WRONG AND s2413 GOT THIS RIGHT: it did not get SLOWER, it got BIGGER — assertions grew `5.3×` while wall grew `~1.7×`, so cost PER ASSERTION FELL ~68%. That is a healthy battery growing exactly as F-1300-4 intends, and the growth must NEVER be pruned back to make a number here true.** ⏱️ **BUT CITE A FLOOR AND A RANGE, NEVER A MEAN — THIS CORRECTS s2413 AS WELL AS s2099: that fire measured four BACK-TO-BACK runs inside one fire (`87.7 · 97.7 · 96.5 · 99.3`, mean `95.3 s`) and I measured `77.2 s` in the next fire on an idle board, so the observed spread across two fires is `77–99 s`, ~29% wide. A mean taken inside ONE fire measures that fire’s load, not the battery. Treat `~75 s` as the FLOOR, budget `~100 s`, and expect both to grow. 📈 **RE-MEASURED s2478 UNDER A NAMED LOAD CONDITION, WHICH IS THE ONLY KIND OF MEASUREMENT THIS FIGURE ACCEPTS (F-2452-1): TWO runs in the one fire, `166.3 s` then `131.0 s`, both rc=0 and green — with a live attended heat-11 arena driving `claude -p` rides against the production API throughout, load average `5.16`. **The LOADED band is therefore `131–166 s`, 1.3–1.7× the top of the `77–99 s` quiet band above**, which is RESTATED, NOT DELETED: it was honest at s2413/s2414 and remains the QUIET reading. ⚠️ **Two observations are a band and not a distribution, and the spread is 27% wide on a SINGLE machine in a SINGLE fire with the load nominally unchanged — so do not read either endpoint as the number; s2413 made exactly this mistake in the other direction by averaging four back-to-back runs inside one fire and calling it the battery’s cost.** ⚖️ **Stated honestly and not inflated: nothing is broken, the battery is GREEN, and this is ONE observation, not a distribution — `logs/guard-stats.jsonl` collects a different roster (`GATE_GUARDS`, per-leg) and cannot corroborate the aggregate.** 🎯 **It is recorded because this clause names its own reader — a fire budgeting its CLOSING minutes — and because the loaded condition is the one that actually obtains: a fire runs this at the end, while lanes gate and arenas ride, exactly as F-2452-1 measured for the ART audit (2.6×) and F-2161-1 for `test:release-base` (2.1×). Three suites, three independent ratios, same direction.** ➡️ **So budget `~100 s` quiet and `~170 s` when anything else is running. 📈 **RE-MEASURED s2479 (F-2479-2) — AND BOTH HALVES OF THAT SENTENCE ARE NOW MEASURABLY WRONG, THE SECOND ONE DANGEROUSLY: ONE GREEN RUN TOOK `436.6 s` (7.3 MINUTES), rc=0, ON A QUIET MACHINE (load average `3.58`, no lane runner, no drain, one attended arena idle).** That is **2.6×** the top of the loaded band and **4.4×** the quiet top, and it is a fully GREEN aggregate — **RESTATED, NOT DELETED**, because the sequence IS the finding: `49.9 s` (s2099) → `77–99 s` quiet (s2414) → `131–166 s` loaded (s2478) → **`436.6 s`**. The battery also grew again: **`105` node test files** plus ~20 chained leaves, against the `97` F-2414-1 cites. ⛔ **DO NOT PRUNE IT BACK TO MAKE A NUMBER HERE TRUE — it got BIGGER, not slower, and that is F-1300-4 working as intended.** ⚠️ **AND THE COST IS NOT ATTRIBUTABLE TO ONE LEG — I TRIED AND REFUTED MY OWN STORY, WHICH IS THE HONEST RESULT: I watched `edge-probe-composite-guard.test.mjs` hold the runner as the sole live child across ~60 s of sampling and was ready to name it the hot leg; run ALONE it is `13.0 s` (its sibling `edge-alarm-clock-guard` `14.6 s`), so the attribution is FALSE and the cost is distributed. Per F-2182-1: publish the measured BAND, never a plausible story.** ➡️ **THEREFORE BUDGET `~100 s` QUIET AND UP TO `~450 s` OTHERWISE, and treat every figure in this clause as a FLOOR.**** 🎯 **WHAT EARNS IT A ROW IS WHO READS IT AND WHEN: this battery is the mandated LAST ACT of every ledger-writing fire, so a fire budgeting its closing minutes off the words that follow under-budgets by ~45 s at the worst possible moment.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: NOT a false green and nothing is broken — the battery is GREEN (`948 pass / 0 fail`, rc=0) and every figure it produces is correct. The defect is entirely in the PRICE quoted to its reader.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, AND THE RESTRAINT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN LAZY — s2413 argued this for wall time (a pinned duration reds on every slower machine) and the argument is STRONGER for the counts, which ARE machine-independent and derivable from `package.json`: the set grew `18 → 97` files across s2099→s2414, i.e. **one growth event roughly every 4 fires**, every one of them lawful. A red on “the cited count no longer matches” would therefore fire during ordinary correct operation more often than any other guard in this battery and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). Derive the counts when you need them — `node -e` over `package.json`’s `test:ledger-guards` leg answers files, leaves and legs in one command — and re-state them here when you do.** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT IS THIS FACTORY’S MOST-REPEATED FINDING FOR THE SEVENTH TIME WITH A GENUINELY NEW TWIST (F-2153-1 · F-2204-1 · F-2350-1 · F-2360-1 · F-2365-1 · F-2403-1): in all six prior instances the cure’s author did not know which surface governed the moment it applied. HERE THE AUTHOR NAMED THE SURFACE, IN WRITING, INSIDE THE FINDING ITSELF** — s2413 wrote *“this battery is the mandated last act of every ledger-writing fire, so a fire budgeting its closing minutes off ‘seconds’ under-budgets by ~40 s at the worst possible moment”* — **and then landed the number in `tasks/BACKLOG.md` and a guard comment (`61c47292c` touches exactly two files, neither of them a law surface), leaving this clause citing `49.9 s` and “still seconds”. The gap is not KNOWLEDGE, it is that “paste it into the surface that states the duty” is a step no checklist contains. When your finding’s own severity sentence names a reader, THAT SENTENCE IS THE EDIT INSTRUCTION — make the edit before you write the handoff, in the same commit as the measurement.** ⓘ **It is a MINUTE AND A HALF, not a second battery** (this sentence read *“It is still seconds”* from s2099 until s2414 — **RESTATED, not deleted**: it was TRUE at 49.9 s, and it is how a reader was mispriced for ~315 fires) ⏱️ ⛔ **THE `~3 MINUTES` THRESHOLD BELOW IS RETIRED AS A HANG TEST — s2479 MEASURED A FULLY GREEN `436.6 s` RUN, SO "PAST 3 MINUTES" NOW FIRES ON HEALTHY BATTERIES AND ITS DIRECTION IS TO KILL WORKING WORK AND REPORT A GREEN YOU NEVER GOT (F-2479-2). 🚨 WORSE, THE PRESCRIBED DIAGNOSTIC RETURNED THE HANG SIGNATURE ON THAT HEALTHY RUN: the `node`-routed `ps` this clause sends you to look for a 0%-CPU child gave runner `0.36 s` CPU and its child `0.05 s`, BOTH FLAT across a 6-second and a 40-second window — because a process blocked on NETWORK I/O is indistinguishable, by CPU, from one wedged in `uv_thread_join`. ➡️ THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT ACTUALLY WORKS IS ONE LEVEL DOWN: ask whether the idle child has a LIVE, RECENTLY-SPAWNED GRANDCHILD (`ps -Ao pid,ppid,pcpu,etime,args` filtered on the child's pid as PPID). A 3-second-old grandchild is PROGRESS; F-2429-1's real wedge is a LEAF with no grandchild at all, at `PPID 1`, aged in hours. s2479 was one sample away from killing a green battery and only the grandchild check stopped it. ⚖️ The wedge in F-2428-1/F-2429-1 IS REAL and its mechanism stands — what is retired is the 3-minute trigger and the sufficiency of the CPU test. Use `~8 min` as the earliest point at which a hang is even plausible, and never conclude one without the grandchild check.** ⓘ **THE SUPERSEDED WORDING FOLLOWS, kept because the mechanism it teaches is still correct: AND IF IT RUNS PAST ~3 MINUTES, THAT IS A HANG AND NOT LOAD — READ F-2428-1 BEFORE YOU WAIT (measured s2427 at 14m37s, diagnosed and part-cured s2428).** A battery leg that spawns a child with NO `timeout` blocks forever if that child wedges, and because `spawnSync` BLOCKS NODE’S EVENT LOOP, the outer `--test-timeout=300000` is **a timer that can never fire** — so the run has no upper bound at all. ⛔ **DO NOT INHERIT s2427’S DIAGNOSIS: both of its specifics were REFUTED by measurement s2428.** `guard-stats-persistence.test.mjs` is INNOCENT (`11 pass` in **2.5 s**, and its spawn is bounded at `:26`), and the grandchild-holds-the-pipe mechanism is IMPOSSIBLE here — `scripts/law-pointer-guard.mjs` contains **zero** subprocess calls in 470 lines, so it is a leaf with no grandchild to leave. ➡️ **WHAT TO DO: confirm with a `node`-routed `ps` (the bare form is gate-refused, F-2400-1) looking for a 0%-CPU child; then run individually the guards that read YOUR OWN edits and SAY in the handoff that the aggregate did not complete — never report a green you did not get.** ⚖️ **The three sites that could produce an UNBOUNDED hang are cured (`law-pointer-guard.test.mjs`), so a wedge there is now a LOUD red.** ✅ **WIDENED s2430 (F-2430-1): ALL 83 unbounded SYNC node-child spawns across 61 `test:ledger-guards` legs are now bound at `240_000` with `killSignal: 'SIGKILL'`, so a wedge ANYWHERE in this battery is now a LOUD, SUBJECT-NAMING `ETIMEDOUT` rather than an unbounded hang.** ⚠️ **That does NOT make a long run harmless — this battery can still be genuinely SLOW, and the instruction above is unchanged: confirm with a `node`-routed `ps` for a 0%-CPU child before you wait.** ✅ **AND THE `test:node-guards` RESIDUE IS NOW ZERO — THIS SENTENCE CARRIED `28` OWED SITES FOR FOUR FIRES AFTER THEY WERE BOUND (F-2436-1, measured s2436).** It read *"`test:node-guards` still carries `28` unbounded sites (ask `node scripts/spawn-bound-census.mjs --list`)"* — **RESTATED, NOT DELETED, because the staleness IS the finding.** 📊 **s2432 discharged it at `cad8fa371`, paying the exact verification cost the clause below names as its reason for deferring: `npm run test:node-guards`, `565 s`, run ALONE, zero failures among the 19 files it bound. RE-VERIFIED THREE INDEPENDENT WAYS s2436, none of them inherited: (1) the instrument THIS SENTENCE ITSELF PRESCRIBED — `spawn-bound-census --list` → `EXPOSED inside a mandated battery leg: 0 across 0 files`, from a DECLARED corpus of `184` mandated legs (`ledger 109 · node-guards 84`) and `481` spawn sites, so the zero is a READ and not an empty set (F-2208-1); (2) the commit's own stat — 19 files, each gaining `timeout: 240_000` + `killSignal: 'SIGKILL'`; (3) a direct grep at HEAD proving the bounds SURVIVE today (`frontier-registry` · `skillmd-guard` · `site-contract` · `probe-base` all carry them).** ⓘ **The `30` sites the census still reports are all OUTSIDE any mandated leg — chiefly retained `tmp-s*` probes that F-1665-1 protects as cited evidence — so they are a different question and NOT battery risk.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: NOT a false green, nothing is broken, and the DIRECTION is CONSERVATIVE — a stale "still unbounded" makes a fire MORE patient with a slow battery, never less careful. Realised cost ZERO.** 🎯 **What earns it a correction is F-1600-1's exact harm one level up: *a check that reports a discharged duty as owed reports it forever, and every fire pays the judgement cost again* — except here the reporter is the LAW, which no sweep audits and which has no `--strict` to red.** 💡 **AND IT IS THIS FACTORY'S MOST-REPEATED FINDING FOR THE EIGHTH TIME (F-2153-1 · F-2204-1 · F-2350-1 · F-2360-1 · F-2365-1 · F-2403-1 · F-2414-1), WITH THE SHARPEST ATTRIBUTION YET — THE DISCHARGE WAS NOT MERELY LANDED, IT WAS *VERIFIED AND WRITTEN DOWN*, IN THE WRONG SURFACE.** s2433 re-measured it deliberately rather than inheriting it and recorded the result in its own words — *"I verified it by re-measurement rather than inheriting it: `spawn-bound-census` reads `0 exposed inside mandated legs`"* — **and filed that in `tasks/BACKLOG.md` (F-2433-1), which is one of the two surfaces F-1654-1 proved a fire does not read at decision time.** `scripts/fire.md` kept saying `28` for three more fires. **s2435 then carried it forward as next-fire priority (C) on the strength of these words, and s2436 was the fire that would have spent its budget re-deriving a discharge that was already three fires old and had been affirmatively measured twice.** ➡️ **The gap is never KNOWLEDGE — it is that "paste it into the surface that states the duty" is a step no checklist contains. When you retire a number, grep the LAW SURFACES for it in the same commit as the measurement.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is measured rather than lazy: a red on "the cited residue no longer matches" is the SAME guard F-2414-1 declined for the battery counts four clauses up, for the same reason — the number is MEANT to move as the corpus grows, so it would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate).** 💡 **REUSABLE: when a clause states a NUMBER *and* names the instrument that derives it, the number is the perishable half and the instrument is the durable one — so state the instrument and let the reader run it. A law that caches its own measurement outlives that measurement, and the cached figure is believed precisely because it sits beside a citation that looks fresh.** ✅ **AND THE ROOT CAUSE IS NO LONGER UNRESOLVED — IT WAS DIAGNOSED s2429 FROM FIVE LIVE SPECIMENS, AND IT IS NOT A DEFECT IN ANY GUARD (F-2429-1).** Three fires tried to REPRODUCE the wedge (s2412 ~1-in-10; s2413 0/28; s2428 0/2) while **five wedged processes sat on the machine at `PPID 1`, 0.0% CPU, for fifteen hours — recorded by s2427 in the ledger and never once opened.** `sample` + `lsof` on all five give one identical stack: `node::Environment::Exit` → `DisposePlatform()` → `NodePlatform::Shutdown()` → `WorkerThreadsTaskRunner::Shutdown()` → `uv_thread_join` → `__ulock_wait`. **The child FINISHES its work, calls `process.exit(0)` (`scripts/law-pointer-guard.mjs:470`), and deadlocks in NODE'S OWN platform teardown joining a V8 worker thread that never parks. The parent then waits forever because `spawnSync` needs an EOF that only the child's exit can deliver.** ⛔ **So do NOT chase the pipe: the subject writes 1,571 B against an 8,192 B socketpair buffer and no stack shows a write frame — the `->(none)` peers in `lsof` are a CONSEQUENCE of the parent being killed later, and reading them as the cause is exactly backwards.** 🔑 **AND DO NOT SIZE A REPRODUCTION AGAINST A PER-RUN RATE: this is a scheduling race at exit, so its odds track machine LOAD, not run count — which is why 0/28 was never surprising.** 📐 **THE ACTIONABLE RESIDUE, RE-MEASURED: s2428 left its remaining sites on the ground that they are *"overwhelmingly `git`, which does not wedge this way."* **The `git` half is CONFIRMED — a `git` child cannot deadlock this way at all — but the exposure was understated: of `398` unbounded spawn sites across `scripts/*.mjs`, `160` (40%) spawn a NODE child and ARE reachable.** That set is the priced work (F-2429-2), it is mechanical, and it needs no reproduction to justify: bound the node ones, leave the `git` ones alone. ⓘ **160 is a FLOOR — the detector is lexical and under-counts spawns routed through a wrapper; it was validated first by returning ZERO for all three already-bounded files.** ✅ **EXECUTED s2430 FOR THE `test:ledger-guards` HALF — 83 sites across 61 legs — AND THE COUNT WAS RE-DERIVED RATHER THAN INHERITED, WHICH FOUND THREE CORRECTIONS, TWO OF THEM IN THE DIRECTION THAT MANUFACTURES WORK (F-2430-1). ⛔ DO NOT WORK FROM THE `160`: (1) TRAILING COMMAS** — `spawnSync(a, b, {...},\n)` splits a final whitespace-only argument, and reading THAT as the options bag scores an **already-bounded** site as unbounded; **13 phantom sites in `gr-sim.test.mjs` alone, every one already carrying `timeout: 30_000`.** **(2) SYNC vs ASYNC, the load-bearing one, and it was never in the key: the mechanism REQUIRES a SYNCHRONOUS spawn, because the entire harm is that the event loop is blocked.** An async `spawn`/`exec`/`execFile`/`fork` leaves the loop free, so `--test-timeout` and any per-test `{ timeout }` **DO** fire and the wedge is already LOUD — **24 node-child sites are async and NOT exposed, including all four surviving `gr-sim.test.mjs` sites.** **(3) SPAWNERS NAMED IN PROSE** — this repo's guards carry long headers that QUOTE code, so a bare regex counts `execFileSync('npm', …)` inside a `/* … */` block as a call site (measured at `law-bash-prescription-guard.test.mjs:33`); **45 phantom sites.** 📊 **TRUE FIGURES: `143` exposed repo-wide before the cure, `60` after; inside mandated battery legs `123` → `28`.** 🧰 **AND IT IS NO LONGER PROSE — ASK THE INSTRUMENT: `node scripts/spawn-bound-census.mjs [--list] [--strict --max=N]`,** which derives the battery legs from `package.json` (F-2207-1), declares its corpus on stdout always (F-2208-1), anchors to `import.meta.url` (F-2220-1), resolves EXTRACTED `const` option bags — the exact key s2429 declined a guard for being unable to handle — and is advisory by default for F-1460-1's reason. 📐 **THE BOUND VALUE IS A MEASUREMENT, AND s2428's DOES NOT GENERALISE: both mandated batteries run at `--test-timeout=300_000` and the slowest known battery test is `fixture-teardown`'s at 69.6 s (F-2099-1), so the `60_000` s2428 used is BELOW the slowest lawful observation and would red a lawful leg. Use `240_000`.** ⛔ **AMENDED s2465 (F-2465-2) — `240_000` IS A DEFAULT, NOT A CONSTANT, AND APPLYING IT MECHANICALLY TO THE VERY TEST THIS SENTENCE CITES WOULD RED A LAWFUL RUN.** The `69.6 s` above is **RESTATED, NOT DELETED** — it was honest at s2099 — but it has drifted the way every figure in this clause drifts: `fixture-teardown` spawned **34** subjects then and **116** now. **RE-MEASURED s2465 by replicating its exact method: the sum of its children is `869.9 s`, and ONE child — `gr-sim.test.mjs`, run ALONE with an isolated `TMPDIR` and no bound — is `326.2 s` at `status=0`, `22 tests / 20 pass / 0 fail`.** So `fixture-teardown.test.mjs:35`'s spawn has a slowest LAWFUL observation of **326.2 s**, i.e. **above** the house bound, and binding it at `240_000` is F-1410-2 in the opposite costume: curing a hang by shrinking the bound until honest work fails. ⓘ **`--test-timeout` cannot save you either, and that is F-2429-1's own mechanism restated: `spawnSync` BLOCKS THE EVENT LOOP, so the outer timer can never fire.** ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING: size a bound against the slowest lawful child of THAT spawn, re-measured, never inherited. `240_000` remains right for a short-lived CLI child (which is 8 of the 9 sites F-2465-1 surfaced); a spawn whose child is itself a full battery needs its own measurement and generous headroom.** 💡 **REUSABLE: a house constant is a measurement someone took on a different subject. When a law hands you a number AND names the observation it came from, check that the observation still describes the thing you are about to apply it to.** ⚖️ **THE `28` ARE DISCHARGED — s2432 PAID EXACTLY THE COST THIS SENTENCE NAMED AS ITS REASON FOR DEFERRING (F-2436-1, see the re-verification above).** It read *"THE REMAINING `28` ARE `test:node-guards`-ONLY LEGS, LEFT DELIBERATELY AND PRICED: that battery is 529.8 s (F-2166-2), and a fire that edits 28 sites it cannot afford to verify ships an unverified change to the drain gate"* — **RESTATED, NOT DELETED: the deferral was CORRECT when written, and the reason it gave was a COST, not a correctness objection, which is exactly why a later fire could retire it by paying rather than by arguing.** ⓘ **A predecessor's stated reason for declining is a CLAIM WITH A PRICE ON IT, never a constraint — re-measure the price before you inherit the restraint (the §2.0b lesson, where two fires and five hours went to honouring a premise one `ps` call refuted).** 🚫 **The tempting `process.exit()`