 there the whole time, in the same tool, one corpus over. ➡️ **Comparing OUTPUTS finds instruments that are already wrong; it is structurally incapable of finding instruments that are merely CAPABLE of being wrong. To find those, ask WHAT EACH VERDICT IS COMPUTED FROM — and check that every corpus feeding a verdict is the one the QUESTION names — rather than what the tool currently PRINTS.** ⓘ **This cure ADDS a `child_process` importer to the census s2212–s2216 built (`98` → `99` non-test spawners), so a fire that edits a spawner moves the denominator it is measuring — s2216's note, now true of me.**\012\012🧭 **AND THE PROBE ITSELF TELLS YOU ITS ANSWER IS INCOMPLETE, ONE LINE BELOW ITS OWN `✅ DRY` — SO ASK THE LANES BEFORE YOU SAY THE WORD: `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs --all` (F-2365-1, measured and cured s2365).** `dry-board-probe` closes EVERY run, including the happy path, with an ALWAYS-ON advisory: *"this reads `tasks/done/` only. A lane branch can hold unabsorbed content with no done-move at all — ask `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs --all` too."* **That instruction was in the TOOL and in no law.** 📊 **MEASURED s2365: the command `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs --all` appeared NOWHERE in `scripts/fire.md` — not in §2F, not anywhere in the file.** ⚙️ **AND THE ONE PRESCRIPTION THAT DOES EXIST CANNOT FIRE HERE, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THIS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT: §2E's trigger is worded *"THEREFORE, BEFORE ANY REFILL"* — and a dry board with zero fire-authorable candidates performs NO REFILL, so the lane check is least prescribed at exactly the moment it is the only thing left to check.** That is F-2343-1's shape verbatim (a reactive clause whose trigger presupposes the very state that is missing), and it is the FIFTH instance of this factory's most-repeated finding — the cure exists, in a working tool, and no surface a fire reads AT THE MOMENT IT APPLIES carries it (F-2153-1 `authorable-candidates`, F-2204-1 the `FIRE END` lock signal, F-2350-1 the `bash` prescriptions, F-2360-1 the `sqlite3` key-read). ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: LATENT, and the realised cost is ZERO.** The whole fleet reads `ahead=0` — **30 of 30 worktrees**, measured, not assumed — so nothing was hidden on the day this was written, and no fire has declared a false DRY on it. Recent fires DO ask the lanes: s2364 and s2365 both did, by name. 🎯 **What earns it a clause is the DIRECTION at the one moment it fires: `DRY` is the verdict that ENDS a fire, and a fire reading §2F literally would declare it off a probe that says, one line below its own banner, that it cannot see lane content at all.** 📉 **A PROXY MEASUREMENT, WITH ITS LIMIT DECLARED BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS READING OF IT IS WRONG: across the last six fire-days (`129` FIRE STARTs) the fire logs narrate `dry-board-probe` `55` times and `lane-usable` `12`. ⚠️ That is NOT an execution count — `node scripts/` appears **0** times in both sampled logs, so these files carry a fire's NARRATION, not its command transcript. It establishes only that far fewer fires REPORT the lane check, never that any fire skipped it.** The finding does not rest on it: the gap is established by READING. 🪤 **THE TRAP, AND IT IS THE PART WORTH CARRYING PAST THIS CLAUSE: THE OBVIOUS NEEDLE PASSES ON THE DEFECT.** The bare token `lane-usable` ALREADY appeared in §2F **four times** when this was written — every one incidental prose, and one of them F-2207-1 saying in so many words *"an instrument §2F does not prescribe"*. **A guard keyed on the tool's NAME would have gone green on the very defect it existed to catch, and certified it.** ➡️ **The discriminator is the RUNNABLE COMMAND, because that is what separates a prescription from a mention** — and it was absent from the whole file while the name was present four times in the one section. 🚫 **NO RED GUARD OVER THE BOARD ITSELF, and the restraint is measured rather than lazy: a lane ahead of main is a LAWFUL routine state — it is simply undrained work — so a red there would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). This is a triage READ, exactly as §2.0c concluded for the runner and F-2357-1 for the evidence sinks.** ⓘ **HOW TO READ THE `--all` OUTPUT, since it is noisier than the four lanes: it returns the COMPLETE worktree set (30 rows today), of which `lane-a..d` are a subset and the rest are attended-owned `worktree-agent-*` / `sol/*` / `gr-task-*` trees — **those are NOT yours to drain**. Asking by name is a strict SUBSET and can miss a non-lane tree; ask `--all`, read the four lanes as your own work, and treat anything else that is ahead as an attended matter to REPORT, not to touch.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/dry-board-advisory-prescription-guard.test.mjs` — 8 arms, 0.17 s, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`. The subject set is DERIVED FROM THE TOOL and never transcribed: it locates the advisory STRUCTURALLY (everything printed after the last verdict branch and before the exit, so it survives a rewording), extracts every backticked `node scripts/*.mjs` command, and requires §2F to carry each verbatim — so if anyone changes the tool's advice, the guard starts asking about the new advice in the same commit.** 🔬 **TEETH — the guard reddened on the LIVE tree before the cure, arm 4 ALONE, naming the missing command, with the other 7 green (so it was not reding for structural reasons); and it carries its own refusals: an advisory that is deleted or moved out of the always-on region EMPTIES the subject set, which arm 2 REFUSES rather than passing vacuously (F-2217-1 — a `for` loop over nothing registers no assertions and reports success), arm 6 is an over-reach control asserting the extractor does NOT pull `drain-block-check` (which the probe references elsewhere, so the exclusion is load-bearing rather than vacuously true), and arms 7–8 run the real CLI against fixture roots to prove the advisory is printed on the DRY path AND a NOT-DRY path — because a follow-up that appears only on failure re-creates the ambiguity it removes (F-2208-1), and in advisory mode stdout is the whole interface (F-2210-1).** 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, and it is the aim s2364 priced, now discharged with a result rather than a hunch: A PASS IS NOT THE WHOLE VERDICT WHEN THE TOOL PRINTS INSTRUCTIONS BELOW IT — so sweep the prescribed instruments for what they say AFTER their banner, and then ask whether the LAW carries it.** ⓘ **The sweep was taken over all 13 cheap advisories named in the six `LAW_SURFACES` (imported from `scripts/law-surfaces.mjs`, not transcribed) and came back otherwise CLEAN — `art-staging-audit`'s NOT-AUDITED list, `authorable-candidates`' residue and `lane-usable`'s `package.json` amber are all DECLARED BOUNDARIES rather than owed work, and each says so in its own words. `dry-board-probe` was the only one naming a second instrument. **Do NOT re-take this sweep.** ⚠️ And one method note, paid for in this fire: my first pass keyed on a leading verdict glyph and reported *"no banner"* for **8 of 13** subjects — the selector was narrower than the question, which is the exact defect this streak has spent twenty fires curing in other people's tools. **Reading 13 short tails cost less than a better regex would have, and could not lie.**\012\012## 3. Gate protocol (evidence, not vibes)\012**§3.0 IS IT ALLOWED? — THE FIRST COMMAND OF EVERY DRAIN, BEFORE CLASSIFICATION AND BEFORE YOU FORM AN OPINION (F-1104-7, guard shipped s1105):** run `node scripts/drain-block-check.mjs <done-move filename | taskfile | branch>`. **Exit 1 = STOP: do not drain, do not gate, do not "just check the merge."** Everything else in this section asks *is it READY* — and a policy block is **not a property of the tree**, so no git probe can ever see it. s1104 merged owner-gated rf-34 with `main..lane/m3` genuinely 1 ahead, a real two-dot diff, main never having moved the file, and a runner report it judged sound — then had to reverse it the same fire. **A well-argued runner report is not an unblock; it is often exactly what made the fork worth reserving for the owner.** The block lives in `tasks/goals.json` (`status:"blocked"` + `blockedReason`) keyed by `taskFile`, which every done-move filename already contains — so this is a lookup, not a judgement, and it costs one second. A block is lifted by the OWNER only, never by a green battery — ⚠️ **AND THE "ALREADY SHIPPED" ARM COULD DISARM ITSELF SILENTLY, IN THE PERMISSIVE DIRECTION (F-2212-1, measured and cured s2212 — the NINTH consecutive fire to find a live instance of F-2204-1's class, and the first to find it OUTSIDE `dry-board-probe`).** `drain-block-check`'s `--queue` path refuses a master whose `mergeHash` is already an ancestor of main — that is the Mistake #8 guard, the 824k Flail. It used to wrap both git calls in a bare `catch { return false }`; but **`merge-base --is-ancestor` uses EXIT 1 AS A LEGITIMATE VERDICT ("not an ancestor"), so the catch could not separate that verdict from a crash** — F-2211-1's class one file over, with the dangerous polarity: `false` ⇒ *"not shipped"* ⇒ **CLEARED FOR DISPATCH**. ⚙️ **AND THE TRIGGER IS MUNDANE, NOT EXOTIC: those two calls carried the ONLY 2-second bound in the file** (every other git call there is unbounded or 30 s), **so a load spike kills THIS check alone and leaves everything else working** — the fire shell under a concurrent ~9-minute `test:node-guards` battery is the documented load case (F-2166-2). 📊 **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING, not by reading: fixture leaf `status="queued"` with a `mergeHash` that IS an ancestor of main, git made slow ONLY on those two calls — healthy git → `⛔ ALREADY SHIPPED — DO NOT QUEUE` (rc=1); 2 s timeout → `✅ CLEAR` (rc=0), no crash, no warning.** An affirmative clearance to re-derive already-merged work. 🔑 **THE DISCRIMINATOR, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, AND IT IS THE REUSABLE PART: `cat-file -e` on a missing object exits **128**; `is-ancestor` says "no" with exit **1 AND EMPTY STDERR**; a timeout is `status=null` + `ETIMEDOUT`. So **exit-1-and-silent is the ONLY non-zero code that means anything** — which is exactly the test `blocker-panel-closed-guard.mjs:115–116` already applies to `grep`, in this same repo, with a comment explaining why.** The corpus already held the correct pattern; this one file had not adopted it. 🛠️ **CURE: `ancestryOfMain()` returns `'yes' | 'no' | 'absent' | 'unverifiable'`, and `unverifiable` REFUSES (`⛔ CANNOT VERIFY — DO NOT QUEUE`) instead of clearing. Deliberately a STRING, not a boolean: any careless truthiness test at a call site coerces `'unverifiable'` to TRUE, i.e. toward refusing rather than clearing — fail-safe by construction rather than by discipline.** The bound is raised 2 s → 10 s, which is **not** "raise it until it goes green" (F-1410-2): the failure mode is now a LOUD refusal, so a generous bound trades false alarms for patience and can no longer hide anything. ✅ **VERIFIED BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL ON THE LIVE TREE BEFORE LANDING (the F-1274-2 standard): all 593 leaves resolve 550 `yes` / 43 `absent` / ZERO `unverifiable`, and `dry-board-probe` reads an identical 0/3/8/32 after the cure** — no master that queues today starts being refused. 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/drain-block-ancestry-guard.test.mjs` (6 tests, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`), every arm proven by manufacturing the defect on a throwaway repo — including the sharpest one, a failure WEARING the verdict's own exit code (exit 1 with non-empty stderr), and TWO REVERSE CONTROLS.** ⚠️ **The reverse controls are the ones that matter: a cure one level too general — refusing on ANY non-zero exit — passes every crash arm while breaking the ordinary path, because "not an ancestor" IS exit 1.** 📐 **AND THE CLASS IS NOW CENSUSED, SO THE NEXT FIRE NEED NOT RE-SWEEP IT.** s2211 handed over this parameterisation framed by its SYMPTOM — *"wherever one instrument greps another's output"* — naming `gazette-backfill-sweep`, `desk-state-audit`, `authorable-candidates`. **All three are REFUTED: the latter two import no `child_process` at all, and the sweep spawns only `git` with ZERO catch blocks, so it dies loud.** ➡️ **Framed by its PRECONDITION instead — a tool must CATCH a subprocess failure AND classify its output, because a non-zero exit is a legitimate verdict in its protocol — the set is decidable by `grep -c catch`: of 99 non-test scripts that spawn, 46 have ZERO catch and are structurally incapable; 20 both catch and read a failed child's output.** Among the live decision instruments there: `dry-board-probe` (cured, F-2211-1), **`drain-block-check` (this finding)**, and three that are CORRECT BY CONSTRUCTION and worth copying — `lane-usable.mjs` (an explicit `{ok}` flag plus an `ABSENT` sentinel at every call site), `run-node-guards.mjs` (`stdio:'inherit'`, nothing captured to misclassify, `child.status ?? 1`), `blocker-panel-closed-guard.mjs` (the exit-1-and-silent test). 💡 **REUSABLE: a class framed by its SYMPTOM yields ~90 candidates and no verdict; framed by its PRECONDITION it yields 20 and a census in minutes. When a predecessor hands you a class to parameterise, RE-FRAME IT ON THE MECHANISM BEFORE YOU START SWEEPING — and check whether your own corpus already contains the correct pattern, because here it did, in a guard, with the reasoning written out.** ⚠️ **AND THAT CENSUS'S COUNTING METHOD IS THE NEXT DEFECT — `grep -c catch` IS FILE-LEVEL, SO IT COUNTS CATCHES THAT ARE NOWHERE NEAR THE SPAWN (F-2213-1, measured and cured s2213 — the TENTH consecutive fire to find a live instance of F-2204-1's class).** ✅ **s2212's mechanical figure REPRODUCES EXACTLY — 46 non-test spawners with ZERO catch — and its `20` was a hand-narrowing of what is really `52` (I measure `98` non-test spawners to its 99, a one-file boundary).** 🔬 **But the file-level count both over-admits and mis-aims: `gate-caller-audit.mjs` carries FIVE catches and is CORRECT BY CONSTRUCTION — all five wrap `fs.readFileSync`, while its actual spawn tests `r.status !== 0`, returns `null`, and REFUSES with exit 2.** ➡️ **THE DECIDABLE QUESTION IS WHETHER THE SPAWN SITS LEXICALLY INSIDE THE TRY — brace-match `try {`, test the body for a spawn, then read the handler. Measured s2213: `26` files have a spawn inside a try; of those `6` are try/**finally** with NO catch (the error propagates — loud by construction), leaving ~19 real swallowers, of which the live decision instruments are a handful.** 🕳️ **ONE WAS LIVE, AND IT IS THE *OTHER* MISTAKE #8 INSTRUMENT — `master-shipped-classifier.mjs`'s `mainReviews()`, the tool §2E names for *"is this banked master already SHIPPED?"*.** Its bare `catch` fell back to walking the working tree's `reviews/`. ⚙️ **THE DEFECT IS NOT "IT SWALLOWS AN ERROR" — IT IS THAT THE FALLBACK SILENTLY ANSWERS A DIFFERENT QUESTION.** `git ls-tree main` asks *what has MAIN shipped?*; the walk asks *what is in THIS directory?* Those coincide only when the working tree IS main. 📊 **MEASURED, AND THE ASYMMETRY IS THE FINDING: on main the two arms are IDENTICAL (955/955). In `worktrees/lane-d` (behind 103) the walk is 4 paths short; in `worktrees/lane-a` (behind 508) it is 32 short and gains ZERO. Loss is MONOTONE, and lost review evidence drops a master `SHIPPED → NO-TRACE` — i.e. INTO the `CANDIDATES` set, the 824k Flail polarity.** 🎯 **CONTROLLED EXPERIMENT (same `--root`, same masters/goals/traces; the ONLY variable is git's health, so anything that moves is the catch arm alone): main checkout → `0` verdicts flipped. lane-a → `1` flip, `CANDIDATES 266 → 267`, and the master that flips is `e10s-1b-ember-shore-schema-and-data` — the owner-forked E10 slice, the only real work on the board.** ⚖️ **HONEST SEVERITY: LATENT, exactly like F-2212-1. On main with healthy git the cure is behaviour-neutral and I measured it so before landing.** Reachability is the same mundane pair: load, or **`maxBuffer` — node's `execFileSync` default is 1 MiB and this listing is already `152,901` bytes over `3,081` paths (14.6%), growing by a line every drain.** 🔑 **AND FOR THE SECOND FIRE RUNNING, THE CORPUS ALREADY HELD THE CURE: `gate-caller-audit.mjs` and `row-quote-currency.mjs` both pass `maxBuffer: 64 << 20` on exactly this call shape. This file had simply never adopted it.** 🚨 **THE SHARPEST RESULT, AND IT GENERALISES PAST THIS FILE: THE GUARD SUITE WAS STRUCTURALLY DEPENDENT ON THE DEFECT. All 12 tests in `master-shipped-classifier.test.mjs` build a non-git `mkdtemp` root, so `git ls-tree main` exits 128 and EVERY ONE OF THEM RUNS THE CATCH ARM. The designed `ask main` path was never executed by any test, and the suite silently certified the fallback as if it were the product.** 🛠️ **CURE: a DISCRIMINATOR, not a refusal — because the fallback is genuinely load-bearing for non-git roots. `mainReviews()` returns `{ ok, source: 'main' | 'worktree' | 'unverifiable', files }`, `source` a STRING for F-2212-1's reason (careless truthiness coerces toward noticing). Codes MEASURED, not assumed: `rev-parse --verify --quiet main` exits **128** outside a repo and **1** in a repo with no `main` (both legitimate → walk silently); `.error`/any other code is a CRASH → `unverifiable`, which prints `⛔ CANNOT VERIFY — DO NOT QUEUE off this run` and reds `--strict` in its own right** (without that last clause a degraded run whose candidate count happens to be 0 exits 0, byte-identical to a clean board — F-2208-1 one file over). ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): `SHIPPED 704 · NO-TRACE 66 · CANDIDATES 1 · DISAGREES 97`, unchanged, and all 12 legacy tests still green.** 🔒 **`scripts/master-shipped-review-source-guard.test.mjs` — 6 tests, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; restoring the pre-cure `mainReviews` verbatim on a scratch copy reds all 6.** ⚠️ **THE REVERSE CONTROLS ARE AGAIN THE POINT, AND THEY CORRECTED MY OWN PREDICTION: an over-general cure (refuse on ANY non-zero exit) passes all four crash arms and is caught ONLY by my two reverse controls — while the 12 legacy tests stay GREEN, which I had predicted would break. They assert `verdict` alone, and the over-general arm still returns the walked files, so the entire discriminator is invisible to them.** 💡 **REUSABLE, three ways: (1) when censusing a class, ask whether the risky call is INSIDE the guarded block — a file-level `grep` counts handlers that guard something else entirely and will exonerate the wrong files and accuse the innocent. (2) When a fallback answers a DIFFERENT QUESTION from the primary, "behaviour-neutral on main" is NOT neutrality — measure it where the two questions diverge, which here meant walking into a stale worktree on purpose. (3) If a guard suite's fixtures cannot reach the primary path, the suite is certifying the fallback; check what your fixtures make reachable before trusting what they attest.** ⚠️ **AND THE CENSUS METHOD IS BLIND ONE LEVEL DEEPER AGAIN — A SPAWN REACHED THROUGH A SAME-FILE WRAPPER IS INVISIBLE TO THE "LEXICALLY INSIDE THE TRY" TEST (F-2214-1, measured and cured s2214 — the ELEVENTH consecutive fire to find a live instance of F-2204-1's class).** ✅ **s2213's headline REPRODUCES EXACTLY — `26` non-test scripts have a spawn lexically inside a `try` — though I count `7` try/**finally** with no catch, not 6, leaving `19` real swallowers.** 🔬 **The proof that the test is still too narrow was sitting in s2213's OWN negative results: it named `lane-freeze-classify.mjs:52` as catching a spawn, and that file does not appear in the lexical census AT ALL, because the try body calls `git([...])` — a one-line helper wrapping `execFileSync`. It found that file by READING, not by its instrument.** 📊 **Resolving same-file wrappers transitively adds `13` files carrying a catch over an INDIRECT spawn and no lexical one anywhere (`agent-seat-room`, `assay-worker`, `capture-footage`, `desk-birth-guard`, `gen-audio`, `lane-freeze-classify`, **`master-shipped-classifier`**, `review-evidence-audit`, `seed-ladder`, `status-line1`, `test-accounts`, `test-multiplayer`, `test-stats`), plus 2 extra sites inside already-flagged files — a true subject set of `32`, so the sharpened census under-counted by 68%.** 🕳️ **ONE OF THE 13 IS `master-shipped-classifier.mjs` ITSELF, AT `:213` — THE FILE s2213 HAD JUST CURED.** ⚙️ **Its CLI `catch` set `process.exitCode = 0` unconditionally while F-2213-1's `--strict` refusal sat INSIDE the `try` above it, so ANY throw skipped the refusal and exited **0 with EMPTY stdout** — byte-identical to a clean board. The cure landed one fire earlier was defeated by the arm one line beneath it.** 📊 **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING: a malformed/mid-splice `tasks/goals.json` (fires splice it EVERY drain) → `rc=0, 0 B`; `--root` one directory off → `rc=0, 0 B`; CONTROL, a genuinely clean board → `rc=0`. Indistinguishable.** ⚖️ **LATENT — measured, nothing invokes `--strict` today.** 🛠️ **CURE: the advisory DEFAULT is preserved (its comment protects a real intent: never block a drain on this tool's own absence); only `--strict` changes, separating **2 = "could not answer"** from **1 = "answered, and the answer refuses"**, the convention `drain-block-check` and `dry-board-probe` already carry — the corpus supplied the pattern for the third fire running. The `⛔ CANNOT VERIFY` banner now reaches STDOUT too, since per F-2211-1 an stdout-classifying caller would otherwise read an empty string as silence.** ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): `SHIPPED 704 · NO-TRACE 66 · DISAGREES 97`, default `rc=0`, `--strict rc=1`, identical to s2213's figures.** 🔒 **`scripts/master-shipped-crash-exit-guard.test.mjs` — 6 tests, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`.** ⚠️ **THE REVERSE CONTROLS CORRECTED MY PREDICTION FOR THE THIRD FIRE RUNNING: I predicted the pre-cure catch would leave both reverse controls green; measured, it reds FIVE of six, because arm 3 asserts the BANNER as well as the advisory exit code. And the OVER-GENERAL cure (`exitCode = 2` on every catch) passes ALL FOUR crash arms, caught only by reverse controls 3 and 6 — it would have handed an advisory tool the power to block a drain by its own failure.** 💡 **REUSABLE: (1) A CENSUS INHERITS THE BLIND SPOT OF ITS OWN RESOLUTION — file-level → lexical → indirect, three fires, three live instruments; expect the next sharpening to find one too, and here the tell was a one-line wrapper. (2) CURING A FILE DOES NOT CLEAR IT: your census's blind spot travels with you. (3) A NEGATIVE RESULT IS A MEASUREMENT — record the MECHANISM that makes an instrument sound, not the verdict, so the next fire inherits a reason. The four s2213 named are sound: `citation-title-guard:280` renders `null` as UNKNOWN at both call sites; `lane-usable:266` fails safe to BUSY; `row-quote-currency:169` fails LOUD (unreadable pairs are never scored absent); `run-node-guards:39` is printed advice that gates nothing.** ⚠️ **AND THE CLASS HAS LEFT THIS FAMILY OF INSTRUMENTS ENTIRELY — IT IS NOW LIVE, NOT LATENT, IN A TOOL A LAW FILE TELLS EVERY DRAIN TO RUN (F-2215-1, measured and cured s2215 — the TWELFTH consecutive fire to find a live instance of F-2204-1's class, and the first outside the Mistake #8 / drain-block family).** `review-evidence-audit.mjs`'s `main()` printed `REFUSING` to **stderr** and set **no exit code**, so every crash exited **0 with EMPTY stdout** — byte-identical, **on both channels the caller reads**, to *"audited fine, nothing untracked"*. The word REFUSING promised a refusal the exit code did not keep. 📊 **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING, four arms, subject truth "the audit did not run", every one rc=0 against a clean control also rc=0: absent review path · `--root` one directory off · unknown option · advisory mode.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY IS THE PART THAT DIFFERS FROM ITS THREE PREDECESSORS: F-2212-1, F-2213-1 and F-2214-1 were all honestly LATENT. THIS ONE IS LIVE, AND ITS TRIGGER IS THE ORDINARY ORDER OF OPERATIONS.** `.claude/skills/drain/SKILL.md` §4 tells every drain to run this **on the review it is AB