()` RECURSES (`repoRoot` → `tryGit` → `git` → `repoRoot`…), so the manufactured defect died on a stack overflow and the arm went green having tested NOTHING. The tell was WALL TIME — 11.5 s against ~200 ms for every other arm — not any assertion. It now resolves the forced root up front and asserts the variant produced output and classified the lane before reading its verdict. A control whose defect arm CRASHES is F-2215-1's silence wearing a different costume: assert the arm RAN, and treat an outlier duration as a defect report.** 💡 **REUSABLE, AND IT RETIRES THE KEY MY PREDECESSOR HANDED ME: s2220 aimed the next fire at a one-grep census over all 326 scripts for the TOKEN `process.cwd()`. That key is ANTI-CORRELATED WITH THE DEFECT AND SCORES ZERO ON BOTH FILES s2220 ITSELF CURED — measured s2221 against the pre-cure blobs, `nul-audit.mjs` **0** occurrences, `attended-owed-audit.mjs` **0**; the occurrences in those files TODAY were ADDED BY THE CURE. Across the corpus the token appears in `7.3%` of files with an implicit corpus root versus `8.7%` of the rest: no discriminating power at all.** 🔭 **WHY, and this is the durable half: CWD-DEPENDENCE IS THE DEFAULT — it is written by SAYING NOTHING (a bare relative path, a spawn with no `cwd` option). The token `process.cwd()` marks an author who THOUGHT about the root, so it tags the SAFER population. Ask instead HOW THE CORPUS GETS ITS ROOT — anchored (`import.meta.url` / an absolute constant / a git-resolved root) versus IMPLICIT. Measured on that key: `96` of `326` non-test scripts have an IMPLICIT corpus root.** ⓘ **A NEGATIVE RESULT BANKED WITH ITS MECHANISM (F-2214-1), because it is a live thread and the obvious reading of it is wrong: `scripts/collection-guards-cwd-invariance.test.mjs` — the ONE guard in the repo literally named for this invariant, rooted in `test:node-guards` — runs its subjects from `os.tmpdir()`, i.e. the exact cwd s2220 proved is the WRONG parameterisation. It is nevertheless **ADEQUATE**, and by luck of its subject: `npx playwright test --list` fails LOUD from a subdirectory (measured `rc=1`, `Total: 0 tests in 0 files`), so a regression of either subject's `import.meta.url` anchor reds in tmpdir just as it would in a subdirectory. **Its real exposure is its SUBJECT SET — a hardcoded list of exactly TWO playwright collection guards, to which neither file F-2220-1 cured, nor either function cured here, was ever added.** That is F-2208-1 at the next level out: a guard asserting a principle where it holds and nowhere it fails. Widening it is unclaimed work. ✅ **CLAIMED AND CURED s2363 — AND BOTH HALVES OF THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE ARE CORRECTED, WHICH IS THE REASON THIS AMENDMENT EXISTS RATHER THAN A TICK (F-2363-1).** The **VERDICT SURVIVES**: every non-root defect arm reds, so the guard really was adequate and nothing was ever falsely green. What was measurably wrong is the **MECHANISM** — and s2182 already priced exactly this failure, *"a hedge that names a MECHANISM invites the next fire to trust the mechanism and stop measuring."* 📊 **MEASURED s2363 BY MANUFACTURING THE DEFECT (each subject's anchored root → `process.cwd()`) ACROSS THREE CWDS, ground truth = a subject whose corpus root is cwd-dependent: `whole-suite` → repo-root **PASS (blind)** · tmpdir **FAIL `EPERM: operation not permitted, scandir`** · `scripts/` **FAIL `ENOENT: tasks/goals.json` after 75.8 s**; `town-spec` → repo-root **PASS (blind)** · tmpdir **FAIL, same EPERM** · `scripts/` **FAIL `No tests found.`** Of the four non-root arms exactly ONE — town-spec from a subdirectory — reds for the reason recorded above.** The tmpdir arms red on a **macOS sandbox refusal to scan the temp dir**, which has nothing to do with playwright collection; the whole-suite subdirectory arm reds because playwright run from `scripts/` **fails to find the config and sweeps `scripts/*.test.mjs` instead**. Three accidents and one real mechanism — so the sentence generalised a single subdirectory measurement onto tmpdir, where the cause is entirely different. ⛔ **AND THE STATED EXPOSURE WAS NOT ACTIONABLE AS WRITTEN, which is why it sat unclaimed for 149 fires: `nul-audit.mjs` and `attended-owed-audit.mjs` are NOT test files and cannot be run under `node --test`, so they could never have joined a list this guard feeds to `node --test`. They already carry their own dedicated guards (`nul-audit-corpus-guard`, `attended-owed-anchor-tree-guard`, `lane-usable-pathspec-root-guard`). The real exposure was the HARDCODING.** 🛠️ **CURE, in F-2207-1's inversion — enumerate what to SKIP so the set fails SAFE: the subject set is now DERIVED from an opt-in marker (`@cwd-invariant-collection-guard`) instead of transcribed, each subject ALSO runs from a repo SUBDIRECTORY (the cwd s2220 proved is dangerous, kept alongside tmpdir rather than replacing it), and an EMPTY subject set REFUSES instead of passing vacuously — a `for` loop over nothing registers no assertions and reports success (F-2217-1).** 🚫 **THE MARKER IS AN OPT-IN AND MUST STAY ONE, measured rather than stylistic: a content sniff (`/playwright/ && /--list/`) selects the guard AND its meta-guard, both of which merely quote list-mode, and the guard would then spawn ITSELF — the tautology this repo names in *"a pgrep liveness probe inside the command it probes"*.** ⚙️ **ONE IMPLEMENTATION NOTE WORTH MORE THAN THE CURE, because it is a trap in every meta-guard: the selector had to be EXTRACTED to a plain module (`scripts/collection-guard-subjects.mjs`), because `node:test` REGISTERS TESTS AT MODULE LOAD — so a meta-guard importing the selector from the guard's own test file also ran that file's five playwright arms. Measured: 11 tests / 5.26 s, of which 5 tests and 4.87 s belonged to the file it was only trying to read a function from, landing the SLOW battery's work inside the CHEAP one. After extraction: 6 tests / 0.46 s.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/collection-guards-subject-set-guard.test.mjs` — 6 arms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; `gate-caller-audit` PASS.** 🔬 **TEETH, six variants manufactured on scratch copies, and the sweep doubled as a reachability audit per s2226's duty — EVERY arm reddens under at least one variant, so none is decoration: pre-cure verbatim reds 3,4,5 · drop the empty-set refusal reds **5 ALONE** · drop self-exclusion reds **6 ALONE** · drop the subdirectory cwd reds **3 ALONE** · a real subject losing its marker reds 1,2 · a runner that ignores subject failure reds **4 ALONE**.** ⓘ **All five predictions HELD this time — recorded plainly because the streak's running note has been that reverse controls keep repricing what their author was surest of, and one clean sweep is one data point against that, not a licence to stop building them.** ⚠️ **ONE GAP IN MY OWN FIRST DRAFT, found by the sweep and worth carrying: dropping the subdirectory cwd initially reddened NOTHING, because the fixture defect is caught by tmpdir too — so the arm I had just argued for was untested. Arm 3 now asserts BOTH cwd labels appear in the guard's output. A new parameterisation is not covered by a defect that any parameterisation would catch.** **ART-SLOT LAW (F-1045-1 + F-1044-4, written into law s1053 — both findings ordered it and neither had ever been written down): (1) any fire that TOUCHES the ART slot — refill, drain, or raws processing — ⏱️ **OR THAT FINDS A DRY BOARD — AMENDED s2159, executing F-2158-1's own priced recommendation.** The touch-trigger is structurally unobservable exactly when the slot is IDLE, and for retention **idleness is not the quiet period, it is the ACCRUAL period**: **583 files / 527.95 MB** aged up to **42.3 days** in no object database while s2154/s2155/s2156/s2157 each correctly reported *"ART untouched, no staging audit owed"* — every one of them right by the letter and blind by construction. A dry board is the one condition that DOES recur, so the duty now attaches to it as well: **if no drain existed this fire, run the audit.** 💰 **Cost measured s2159