yFiles: 0` — ALL THREE MACHINE CHANNELS byte-identical to a genuinely safe board. Same for a transient failure of that one call.** The only dissent was an unlabelled line of **git's own stderr**, which no caller classifies and `--json` drops entirely — so per F-2211-1 it is not a signal, it is noise that happens to be true. ⚖️ **SEVERITY, STATED HONESTLY: on the live board the call SUCCEEDS, so every `LOCAL-ONLY 0` this streak has reported was TRUE — verified, not assumed. The defect is that the instrument is structurally incapable of telling you when it stops being true, and its consumer is not a gate but THIS LAW: the ART-SLOT duty makes every dry-board fire report that number to the OWNER, and seven consecutive fires have now reported it.** 🎯 **AND IT BEARS DIRECTLY ON AN OPEN DESK ITEM: F-2159-1 asks whether to root `art-staging-audit --strict` in `test:ledger-guards`. Answering YES before this cure would have rooted an arm that greens on its own blindness — a gate certifying the one thing it cannot see.** 🛠️ **CURE: DECLARE THE SOURCE'S COVERAGE, exactly as F-2196-1 declares the scan's BOUNDARY forty lines up in the same file — the corpus supplied the pattern for the FIFTH fire running. `mainTreeSource` is a STRING for F-2212-1's reason (a careless truthiness test on a failure value is TRUE, i.e. toward declaring), it discriminates `absent-ref` from `unverifiable` because those name two different owed acts (fetch/push vs investigate), it is printed on stdout **ALWAYS — including the happy path** since a declaration that appears only on failure re-creates the very ambiguity it removes (F-2208-1; the §4 desk-header precedent: if the desk is genuinely empty, still write the header), it is carried in `--json`, and `--strict` now separates **2 = "could not answer"** from **1 = "answered, and the answer refuses"** — the convention `drain-block-check`, `dry-board-probe`, `master-shipped-classifier` and `review-evidence-audit` already carry. **The advisory default still exits 0 by design, and this does NOT root the audit's `--strict` in any gate: F-2159-1 is the owner's question and a cure must not prejudge it.**  ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): stdout byte-identical but for the one new declaration line, rc unchanged in both modes, `AT RISK 0 · LOCAL-ONLY 0 · SALVAGED 797 files 600.08 MB`. Per F-2215-1 the control asserted its OWN validity first — "pre-cure arm produced 43 lines, it really ran" — because a control whose failure mode is silence cannot be told from the silence it measures.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/art-staging-local-only-source-guard.test.mjs` — 6 tests, 2.8 s, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; pre-cure reds 3 of 6, and each over-general cure is caught by exactly the reverse control built for it (collapsing 1 into 2 → arm 1 · declaring only on failure → arm 5 · refusing unconditionally → arms 1 and 6).** ⓘ **AND FOR THE FIRST TIME IN FIVE FIRES THE REVERSE CONTROLS DID NOT CORRECT MY PREDICTION — all four manufactured defects reddened exactly the arms I predicted. Recorded because the streak's running note has been the opposite; one clean prediction is not evidence the practice can be dropped, it is one data point against four.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is the census question turned on a different axis: s2215 retired *"what does the TRY contain?"* in favour of *"what does the HANDLER do?"* — LOUD vs SILENT. That is right, and it found this site. But the polarity test is per-HANDLER while the harm is per-CONSUMER, and this handler has BOTH polarities at once: the same swallow makes `AT RISK` bigger (loud, self-announcing) and `LOCAL-ONLY` smaller (silent, a false zero). A handler is not sound because SOME caller notices; ask which number the LAW tells a fire to REPORT, and test that one.** 🔭 **AND THE `catch`-KEYED CENSUS IS ITSELF ONE INDIRECTION SHORT, MEASURED s2216: `spawnSync` NEVER THROWS on a failed child — it reports by RETURN VALUE — so s2212's *"46 non-test spawners with ZERO catch are structurally incapable"* is TRUE only for the throwing APIs. Measured: of 98 non-test scripts importing `child_process`, **63 use `execFileSync`/`execSync` (throw) and 28 use `spawnSync` (never throws), 18 of those with ZERO `catch` in the file** — a subject set every census in this streak has scored as incapable BY CONSTRUCTION. **Swept: all but one test `.status`, and that one is low-stakes (`gen-audio.mjs:194` reads `afinfo` stdout without testing either field, though it degrades to `null` rather than to a wrong number). The live decision instruments on this axis are SOUND and worth copying — `drain-block-check.mjs:320` (**was `:292` s2262–s2332 — re-based s2333, +28, when the F-2333-1 detached-HEAD refusal landed ~30 lines above it: the THIRD rot of this coordinate and the THIRD RUNNING caused by a fire editing the very script the law cites, predicted by this clause and cured in the same landing commit. Re-grepped, NOT arithmetic — the sibling pointer in this same file moved +76 in the same commit, so one remembered delta would have been wrong for the other**; was `:277` s2224–s2246 — re-based s2247, +15, when the F-2247-2 retired-reason-class exclusion landed above it; was `:212` s2216–s2223; re-based s2224, +65, when the F-2223-1 corpus-tree discriminator landed above it) is F-2212-1's own cure, and `red-inventory-lookup.mjs:64`/`:234` both test `.status` and degrade to `null` / an explicit `SNAPSHOT main commit UNKNOWN` line.** So this axis is clean TODAY, but it is not covered by the polarity test and the next fire should not assume it is. ⚠️ **AND THE POLARITY TEST HAS THE SAME BLIND SPOT EVERY CENSUS BEFORE IT HAD — MEASURED ON MY OWN NUMBERS, NOT PREDICTED. s2215 wrote that asking *what does the HANDLER do?* "needs NO reachability analysis, so it CANNOT be blind to an indirection BY CONSTRUCTION." That holds for the TRY side and FAILS for the HANDLER side: deciding whether a handler is LOUD means resolving what its calls DO, and a `process.exit` behind a one-line