it is an armed Silent No-Op (Mistake #1):** the stopped run that filename names **left zero diff** by its own preserved record, so a successor obeying the instruction gates a run that changed nothing, and looks for a `blocked` leaf to flip that does not exist. 🚫 **No mechanism is proposed and the reason is the point: the obvious one — have this tool print the filename-vs-leaf disagreement — is cheap and purely mechanical and STILL would not have prevented this, because s1665 never ran the tool.** It had the filename in hand and read its words. **A tool only helps the fire that asks it** (the F-1582-1 gap: the misread happens while a fire reads with its eyes, a moment no battery observes). ➡️ **The cure is therefore the one command you already have, invoked at the moment of ASSERTION rather than the moment of action — and it generalises: any name the factory mints to describe a state (done-move prefixes, `save/` vs `archive/`, row markers) is a SNAPSHOT of a claim, while the ledger IS the claim. On disagreement the ledger wins and the name is what must be re-read.**\012**§3.0b CUSTODY — NEVER PLACE CONTENT YOU HAVE NOT *DECIDED* TO MERGE INTO MAIN'S WORKING TREE. GATE UNDECIDED CONTENT IN A DETACHED WORKTREE (F-1295-1, s1295 — MADE STANDING s1342).** ⚠️ **This cure was declared "adopted" by exactly one fire and then written into NO law surface for 47 fires** — re-measured s1342 across `scripts/fire.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/skills/**`, `AGENTS.md` and every `^- **s9` bullet in `STATUS.md` (**140 total = 18 genuine lettered law bullets** — `s9i`/`s9ab`/`s9al`/`s9ar`/… — **plus 119 numeric handoff archives and 3 bare `- **s9 ` VM-era env bullets (obsolete on native, per the blanket VM-era clause)**; the loose phrase "140 law bullets" would over-state the law corpus 7.8×, so it is written out here — ✅ **RE-MEASURED s1538, 196 fires later: EXACTLY 140 / 18 / 119 / 3, every figure unchanged. DO NOT RE-DERIVE THIS ONE AGAIN.** It is stable *by construction*, which is the part worth knowing: the `^- **s9` pattern matches only the closed historical range s900–s999, so it cannot grow no matter how many fires run — today's archives are minted as `- **s15NN`. A figure that looks like a growing census is in fact a frozen one, and this line now says so): the only "detached worktree" in the corpus is `CLAUDE.md:50` (Mistake #12), which is about **contention**, a different question F-1295-1 itself explicitly distinguishes. §3's clean-main rule asks whether *someone else's* dirt contaminates your gate; **this asks the inverse — whether YOUR gate leaks.** 🎯 **THE INCIDENT:** s1294 checked four lane paths into main's tree to evaluate a slice, wrote **VERDICT: HOLD — NOT MERGED**, reverted with `git checkout HEAD -- <paths>`, confirmed `git status` clean, and was **wrong** — a concurrent attended session's broad `git add` had already committed all four at **`b37c1fc6`**, under a commit message about a rehearsal that named none of them, **thirteen minutes before the handoff denying it.** 🔑 **WHY THE VERIFICATION COULD NOT WORK, WHICH IS THE REUSABLE HALF: every probe that fire had returns the SAME answer whether the revert worked or the content was committed out from under it.** `git status` cannot detect a sweep, because **a sweep's signature is the ABSENCE of dirt**; and `git checkout HEAD -- <paths>` restores *from* a HEAD that has itself moved, so the "revert" **re-installed** the very content it was undoing. The only discriminating probe is a **blob-hash comparison against a PRE-sweep ref** — which is why F-1295-1 is recorded in hashes. ⚖️ **A HOLD verdict has NO ENFORCEMENT SURFACE:** it lives in a review file and a goal leaf, and **nothing in git defends it**. The same window ships an **owner-BLOCKED** slice just as readily — the version that costs a design fork, and the one §3.0 exists to prevent. **What must NOT be concluded is "the sweep turned out fine, so the sweep is fine":** that slice was exonerated by a control run *afterwards*, and had it gone the other way the defect would have shipped past a verdict that said no, with the fire that wrote the verdict still believing it held. Cost of the cure: one `git worktree add` plus a `node_modules` symlink. ⓘ **SCOPE, STATED HONESTLY SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT OVER-CORRECT ME: F-1295-1 measured the WORKING-TREE case only.** Gating by `git merge --no-ff` and planning to revert on a red (§3, "revert the merge") puts undecided content on **HEAD** rather than in the working tree — structurally the same bet against concurrency, but **UNMEASURED as of s1342**; treat it as a caution, not a finding, and do not cite it as proven. ✅ **NO LONGER UNMEASURED — THE HEAD-SIDE CASE WAS MEASURED s1589 AND THE CAUTION IS NOW A FINDING (F-1589-5).** s1589 left drain 2's merge staged in main's index while writing its review; **ninety seconds later** a concurrent Cowork agent committed **`9371c1c8d`** — parents `e15858b13` (the fire's own stamp commit) and `153d30ba6` (the lane tip) — sweeping the entire staged merge into a commit headlined *"the operator falls at twin-banks w16 (697 kills) — notebook gen 2"*, which names none of it. ⓘ **Nothing was lost and nothing was corrupt** (`main..lane/b` empty, artifacts byte-identical, the fire's other drain untouched) — **the casualty was attribution, and the EXPOSURE is far worse than the outcome: had that gate gone RED, the plan was `merge --abort`, and the sweep would have SHIPPED the rejected content anyway.** That is the same failure as F-1295-1 with the staging area swapped for the index, and it retires the "structurally the same bet" hedge above: it is not merely structurally the same, it has now happened. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING: never leave a merge staged on main.** Gate undecided content in the detached worktree exactly as this section already mandates, then **merge and commit as ONE act** — `git merge --no-ff <branch> -m "…"`, never `--no-commit` followed by review-writing. The review file, ledger rows and goal leaf land in a FOLLOWING commit; that is already the house sequence (§3.0's two-commit rule exists because a commit cannot contain its own hash), so this costs nothing and closes the window. ⚠️ **Do not read "the sweep turned out fine" as evidence the window is safe** — that is the exact reasoning this section already forbids one paragraph above, and s1589 is the second fire to be saved by luck rather than by design.\012**§3.1 EVERY FIRE-SIDE PLAYWRIGHT COMMAND PASSES `--workers=1`. NOT AN OPTIMISATION — A CORRECTNESS REQUIREMENT OF THE INSTRUMENT (F-1270-1, measured s1270).** Until `d1a0846d` `playwright.config.ts` set no `workers` key, so a fire's default was **6 obtained workers**, and at 6 the fire shell's per-job CPU ceiling (F-1269-1: the ceiling is the fire's process context, measured 7.53× lane vs 3.47× fire at eight children) starves each chromium until timing-sensitive assertions go red. **Interleaved, same shell, same hour, s1270: `--workers=1` → 3/3 runs rc=0, 18/18 instances passed, 0 drift reds · default → 3/3 runs rc=1, 17 drift reds / 18.** ⭐ **And it costs nothing, which kills the only objection: w=1 mean wall 55.94 s vs default 60.74 s — the serial arm is 7.9% FASTER in the fire shell**, because the parallel arm is fighting the ceiling. ⚠️ **This is a factory-gate correctness issue, not a game bug: every fire-side gate ever run at default workers was a known-unreliable instrument, and its reds were the shell's, not the slice's.** `.claude/skills/drain/SKILL.md:33` has carried the flag since s114 — incidentally, with no stated reason, which is exactly why six fires never knew it was load-bearing; this clause states the reason so it cannot decay again. ✅ **THE LAW NOW HAS A MECHANISM, SO THE FLAG IS A BELT-AND-BRACES, NOT THE ONLY DEFENCE (F-1270-3, landed s1270 `d1a0846d`): `playwright.config.ts:50` carries `workers: isFireShell ? 1 : undefined`, keyed on `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` being PRESENT** (**was `:30`; re-based s1301 when the F-1296-3 `testIgnore` comment block was inserted above it — cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts) — launchd sets it to `~/.claude-fires` and `scripts/fire-runner.sh:130` (**was `:129` — re-based s2500 by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`129 → 130`, **+1**), the rot caused by the ATTENDED commit `304ff2396` (2026-09-04T12:45:12, the codex engine switch) landing MID-FIRE and inserting the WALL-on-primary echo directly above it. The cited line is INTACT and still supports the claim — re-read s2500: `:130` is the `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude-alt"` alt-fire invocation, so `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` is still SET for fires and the `workers: isFireShell` key still holds. ⚠️ **Two sibling pointers rotted by the same +1 in the same commit (`CLAUDE.md` → `:136→:137`, `.claude/skills/drain/SKILL.md` → `:129→:130`) — re-grep EACH, never carry one delta across three surfaces; they agreed here only by luck of a single-line insertion.** `:126`, `:121` same-day (+5,+3), `:102`, `:86`, `:81` before that; rotted attended 2026-08-12 by exactly 19 lines when the owner-ruled fire-engine switch landed above it, ALT line re-verified intact; before that rotted s1536 by exactly 16 lines when the F-1536-1 dry-board-guard cure replaced the hardcoded lane list at `:63`, and before that s1456 by 5 lines when the owner's `02bea28c` auth fix inserted the `.env.local` token block above it — cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts**) to `~/.claude-alt`, while lanes and attended sessions never set it, so **lanes keep full parallelism** (the lane shell runs 6 workers ~3.5× FASTER — F-1267-1 — and must not pay for a fire-only defect). Still pass `--workers=1` explicitly: it is free, it survives anyone editing the config, and it keeps the intent legible at the call site. 🚫 **What remains forbidden is pinning `workers` UNCONDITIONALLY** — a flat `workers: 1` taxes every lane run, and it passes a fire-only check while doing so, which is why the guard below asserts BOTH directions. ⚠️ **DO NOT "restore" this law by deleting the `workers: isFireShell` line** — an earlier revision of this very clause read *"`playwright.config.ts` sets no `workers` key"* and *"Do NOT fix this by setting `workers` in `playwright.config.ts`"*, both written **before** the mechanism landed in the same fire; a session obeying that sentence literally would silently re-open F-1270-1. 🔒 **Guarded since s1273 (F-1273-1): `scripts/fire-shell-serialisation.test.mjs`** in `test:node-guards` resolves the config in both env states and reds if the fire shell stops serialising **or** if lanes start being pinned. ⚖️ **A red seen at default workers is not evidence until it reproduces at `--workers=1`.**\012For a drain: `npx tsc --noEmit` + `npm run build` + the slice's own spec + affected adjacent suites + a boot probe (zero console/page errors, desktop + 390px). Full native runs — no splits needed. 🧮 **AND IF THE SLICE'S DIFF TOUCHES `src/sim/`, `src/systems/` OR `src/entities/`, THE BATTERY ALSO INCLUDES `npm run test:node-guards` (F-1460-1, second half of the cure; landed s1462 with the re-pin).** A slice-local spec is structurally incapable of seeing a **cross-cutting sim pin**, because the pin does not live in the slice's own suite — so a drain gated correctly and completely on the slice's specs can still move a number that another guard asserts, and nothing in the prescribed battery will say so. ⚠️ **That is not hypothetical, it is how F-1460-1 was born:** `4ab48743` (f1452-1 fort-solidity — gates the stuck-watchdog on `route.blocker` in `src/entities/Enemy.ts`, +139/-31 in `src/systems/BuildSystem.ts`) changed routing, therefore engagement, therefore **8 fewer kills over 20 waves**; it was deliberate and spec-green and reached main via drain `07213c73`, whose evidence reads *"4/4 own specs both projects"*. The Baron pin in `scripts/gr-sim.test.mjs` went stale that instant, `test:node-guards` went red **on every fire from 2026-08-04 to s1462**, and — the expensive part — **the red was wearing an excused class label (`cross-engine`), so for five fires nobody investigated it.** A red nobody investigates is worse than no test. 💡 **The general shape: a change is cross-cutting when it moves BEHAVIOUR the sim replays, not when it edits many files** — routing, blockers, targeting, spawn order and damage resolution all qualify while touching two files. Judge by the directory, not by the diff size; that is why this rule is keyed on paths a human cannot forget rather than on a judgement call. ⓘ **Cost, measured s1462 and stated honestly rather than waved at:** `scripts/gr-sim.test.mjs` is the battery's dominant term at **55.6 s** (its Baron test alone 8.5 s); the full battery is **284 tests / 281 pass / 3 skipped** and runs in one command. That is cheap against two days of a red board. 📈 **RE-MEASURED s1537 (F-1537-1) — every number in the previous sentence has drifted, and the DIRECTION is what matters: the battery is now `363 tests / 360 pass / 0 fail / 3 skipped` in `181.3 s`, and `gr-sim` alone is `12 tests / 79.9 s` (44% of the whole), its two Baron tests `15.0 s` + `14.2 s` where the old text says one at 8.5 s.** ⚠️ **Do not read the drift as rot in the pointer sense — the battery GREW on purpose (+79 tests since s1462) and the s1462 figures were honest when written; what is now false is only the CHEAPNESS ARGUMENT'S ARITHMETIC, so it is restated rather than deleted.** ⓘ Practical consequence for the fire that has to schedule it: **3 minutes serial, not one**, and a fire that overlaps it with a second battery will contaminate both (s1536 hung ~19 min doing exactly that on shared `claimed-spec-harness-guard`/`fixture-teardown` fixtures). **Run it ALONE.** ⚠️ **AND THE REASON IS NOT THE HANG — A SELF-CONTAMINATED BATTERY MANUFACTURES FINDINGS, AND ITS RED IS NOT EVIDENCE IN EITHER DIRECTION (F-2462-1, measured s2462).** The sentence above justifies running alone with a HANG (s1536, ~19 min on shared fixtures), which is a COST and reads as a scheduling nicety a hurried fire may trade away. **The load-bearing reason is that a fire's own parallelism is INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A REAL TIMING REGRESSION AT THE ASSERTION.** 📊 **MEASURED s2462 on ONE tree, the only variable being what else was running: `test:power-budget` returned **p95 0.966 ms against a 0.500 ms cap — FAIL** while a playwright battery ran beside it, and **0.334 ms — PASS** (then 0.384 ms) alone. A **2.9× swing on a timing assertion**, against a slice that does not touch `src/systems/PowerGraph.ts` at all.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: NOTHING SHIPPED WRONG and this is not a false green — the contradiction was visible because the same tree was measured twice, and the cost was one wasted battery.** 🎯 **What earns it a clause is the DIRECTION: a fire that reported that run would have filed a FABRICATED REGRESSION — a real cap, a real p95, a real red, and no defect — against another agent's slice.** 🔁 **AND IT CUTS BOTH WAYS ACROSS A HANDOFF, measured in the same fire (F-2462-3): the RUNNER's own headline for this battery read *"558 pass, 5 fail"* where the drain's re-run ALONE read **564 pass / 2 fail / 5 skipped** — three of its five reds were its shell's contention. **Contention inflates RED COUNTS on both sides of a handoff**, so the drain's own re-run is a free control on the runner's headline (F-2166-2) and a runner's reds are a claim (Mistake #4) exactly as its greens are.** 🚫 **No guard is proposed and the restraint is measured: "was anything else running?" is not observable to a battery — the assertion sees only a number — so the cure is this sentence, at the site, read at the moment it applies.** It is still cheap against two days of a red board. 📈 **RE-MEASURED s2099 (F-2099-1) — THE SAME DRIFT AGAIN, NOW 2.2× THE s1537 FIGURE, AND THE SCHEDULING SENTENCE JUST ABOVE IS THE PART THAT WENT FALSE: the battery is `472 tests / 467 pass / 0 fail / 0 cancelled / 5 skipped` in `404.7 s` — SEVEN minutes serial, not three.** Fire shell, node **v26.4.0**, `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` set, file concurrency 1, run ALONE on a dry board at load avg 3.75. `gr-sim.test.mjs` is **still** the dominant term and has grown with the battery: **17 tests / 227.3 s / 56.2% of wall** (was 12 / 79.9 s / 44%); the runner-up is a SINGLE test — fixture-teardown's *"all 34 scripts/\*.test.mjs fixture owners remove their temp directories"* at **69.6 s** — and **14 tests over 5 s account for 313.5 s, 77% of wall**, so the cost is concentrated, not diffuse. ⓘ **The s1462 and s1537 figures above are RESTATED, NOT DELETED, because the SEQUENCE is the finding:** 284 → 363 → 424 (s1613) → 472 tests, and 55.6 → 181.3 → 280.9 → 404.7 s. **It only ever drifts upward, so a fire reading a stale number under-budgets the duty by exactly the amount the factory has grown** — which is why this correction is now the third of its kind and should be expected to be re-taken again, not treated as final. 📈 **RE-MEASURED s2166 (F-2166-2) — AND IT WAS RE-TAKEN, EXACTLY AS PREDICTED, ONE FIRE-DAY LATER: the battery is `503` tests / `498 pass / 0 fail / 5 skipped` in **`529.8 s`** — NINE minutes serial, not seven.** Fire shell, node **v26.4.0**, `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` set, run ALONE on the merged tree of the `c1-assay-fairness` drain. **The sequence is now 284 → 363 → 424 → 472 → `503` tests and 55.6 → 181.3 → 280.9 → 404.7 → `529.8` s** — RESTATED, NOT DELETED, because the sequence IS the finding and the monotone direction is the whole content. ⓘ **This measurement also carries a second, load-bearing result: rc=0.** The runner that produced the slice reported `test:node-guards` **non-green** and blamed its own shell (node **23.11.1** against `.nvmrc` **26.4.0**, one 90 s `gr-sim` file timeout); re-run ALONE in the fire shell the SAME tree is **rc=0, 0 fail**, and `gr-sim` alone is **20 tests / 18 pass / 0 fail / 2 skipped / 265.8 s**. That is the F-2076-1 mechanism confirmed a third time from the other direction — **v23 bounds at FILE granularity and never consults per-test budgets** — so a lane-side red on this battery is a question about WHICH INTERPRETER RAN IT before it is a question about the slice. **The drain's own re-run is the free control on the runner's headline; take it before you inherit a red.** ⚖️ **AND DO NOT INHERIT "the battery reds in the fire shell" FROM F-2076-1 — THAT RED IS LOAD-ATTRIBUTABLE, MEASURED, NOT STRUCTURAL.** All three tests it named as budget-endangered PASSED here with 2.0–2.4× more headroom than it recorded one day earlier: `runtime rush` (90 s budget) **90,003 ms CANCELLED at s2076 → 46,297 ms PASS (51.4%)**, `overtime`-on-both-engines (240 s) **219,418 ms (91.4%) → 91,282 ms (38.0%)**, `Twin Banks` (45 s) **34,896 ms (77.5%) → 16,275 ms (36.2%)**. F-2076-1's MECHANISM stands unrefuted and is the durable half (v26 consults per-test budgets, v23 bounds at FILE granularity and never consults them); what is now measured is that the MARGIN moves with machine load, so a red here is a question about the arrangement before it is a question about the sim — **`sample` the pid and check the load before theorising, and never re-pin to make it go away.** ⚖️ **A failure here is a FINDING, never a re-pin reflex** — see the standing prohibition below and the comment at the pin site: re-pin only with a **named cause**, never to make a red go away (F-1441-3). **Merge-throughput tools (2026-07-06): (a) DISJOINT-PAIR BATCH — two lane drains may share ONE gate battery when their `git diff --name-only` sets vs their bases are disjoint (additive-only Balance.ts / vite-env.d.ts hunks excepted): merge both onto clean main, run one combined battery (both slices' specs + the union of adjacent suites), commit each path-scoped. On ANY failure revert both and fall back to strict serial. Never batch more than two; never batch when one touches files the other's spec exercises. (b) ⛔ **WITHDRAWN s1270 — this clause prescribed the exact thing §3.1 forbids.** It read: "the desktop and mobile playwright projects may run concurrently on two scratch ports when wall-time matters." Both of its premises are now measured false in the fire shell: concurrency is what MANUFACTURES the reds (17 drift reds / 18 at default workers vs 0 / 18 at `--workers=1`), and wall-time does not favour it anyway (**60.74 s concurrent vs 55.94 s serial**). Run the projects SERIALLY under `--workers=1`. The scratch-port family (5199/5231/5234) stays — that is Mistake #12 attribution hygiene and is unrelated. The gate CONTENT is never thinned — these optimize repetition, not rigor.** For lane drains: diff the worktree against ITS base commit (find it via the lane branch), classify LANE-TOUCHED vs MAIN-MOVED-ONLY per file, never blind-copy — 3-way graft when both moved. Merge onto CLEAN main only. Write `reviews/<slice>.md` (verdict, evidence, findings F-<id>, env exceptions with proof) + screenshots to `reviews/shots-<slice>/`. Findings that block: write a corrective task to tasks/queue/<slot>/ instead of merging. Art drains: QA raws vs the request's measured criteria, `node scripts/extract-alpha.mjs --key <hex> --grid CxR`, seam-law height checks, wire contracts, in-game screenshot review, LEDGER update.\012Commit style: slice-scoped message with gates summary (see git log for the house voice). Path-scoped adds ONLY.\012\012## 4. Handoff (mandatory when you took the lock)\012Rewrite STATUS.md line-1: `Last updated: <ISO> s<N> handoff, lock CLEARED — <what landed + evidence pointers + what's next (A/B/C/... priorities for the next fire)>`. Move the previous line-1 to a `- **s<N-1> handoff (line-1 archive):** ...` bullet below the law bullets. ⚠️ **THE PRIOR HANDOFF MUST END UP ARCHIVED — CHECK, DO NOT ASSUME YOUR TOOLING DOES IT.** Dropping the previous line-1 when you take the LOCK and re-archiving it in the handoff commit is the **normal** pattern and `scripts/status-archive-audit.mjs` scores it as such (it reported *"7 dropped at lock time but restored by the fire's own handoff commit — normal, not a defect"* on the very run that checked this fire) — so the duty is about the END STATE, not the order. ⏱️ **AMENDED s2403 (F-2403-1) — THAT LAST CLAUSE IS TRUE ONLY FOR A FIRE THAT SURVIVES TO WRITE ITS HANDOFF, AND THE ONE CASE WHERE IT IS FALSE IS THE CASE §1.1 ALREADY PRICES.** When a fire dies between its lock commit and its handoff, there IS no handoff commit to restore anything — so the ORDER is precisely what decides whether its predecessor's line-1 survives in the file, and this sentence tells a fire the opposite. 🔗 **THE SHARPER HALF, WHICH §1.1 DOES NOT COVER: ITS RECOVERY DUTY IS WRITTEN AS A SINGLE STEP** — *"a fire killed between its lock commit and its handoff leaves the PREVIOUS fire's line-1 dropped and unarchived, so the takeover owes that recovery too"* — **so a CONSECUTIVE CASCADE strands the fire TWO BACK, and nobody owns it.** The taking fire recovers its immediate predecessor, correctly, and stops. 📊 **MEASURED s2403 over the 12 most recent dead fires: 11 were recovered correctly and exactly ONE is a gap — and it is exactly the cascade case.** s2395 died mid-drain (`FIRE END rc=0` 14:30:05); s2396 took over at 14:36, dropping s2395's line-1 per the §4-**normal** pattern this clause blesses, and itself died at 14:42:54; s2397 took over at 14:49, archived s2396's lock line — its bullet still reads *"dead fire, no handoff — archived by s2397"* — and did not walk back one further. **s2395's line-1 was absent from STATUS.md at HEAD for ~5 h, recovered VERBATIM from `64b9817a1` by s2403.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: NOTHING WAS LOST, and this is NOT a false green.** `STATUS.md` is TRACKED, so git holds every version and the RETENTION LAW explicitly permits compaction of tracked files; the era-5 drain s2395 died running was finished by s2397; and **`status-archive-audit`'s `CLEAN` is CORRECT, not blind** — it scopes to HANDOFF line-1s, and s2395 never wrote a handoff, so it was right to say nothing. 🎯 **What earns it a clause is the DIRECTION and the SURFACE: the archive chain is what a fire reads at TRIAGE, and it showed an unbroken `s2397 → s2396 → s2394` with no marker that a third fire had died mid-drain between them.** A fire reconstructing that afternoon from the file alone would not know s2395 existed. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING, ON ANY DEAD-LOCK TAKEOVER: walk the chain BACKWARD until you reach a line-1 that IS archived — one step is not enough when your predecessor died too.** It is one command per session: `grep -c "s<N> " STATUS.md`. 🛠️ **AND THE DISCIPLINE THAT PREVENTS THE WHOLE CLASS COSTS ONE SPLICE — ARCHIVE YOUR PREDECESSOR IN YOUR *LOCK* COMMIT, NOT AT HANDOFF.** The end state §4 requires is identical; only the window differs, and closing it removes both the stranding above and the mid-fire `LOST: 1` that `status-archive-audit` otherwise reports for a fire's entire working life. **Verified s2403: locking this way, the audit reads `CLEAN: 0 permanently absent` while the lock is still held.** 📊 **BUT IT LIVED NOWHERE A FIRE READS: censused over the six `LAW_SURFACES` (imported from `scripts/law-surfaces.mjs`, not transcribed) the cure scored **ZERO** hits — the single hit in `fire.md` is F-2204-1 describing the HAZARD — while adoption ran at **36 of the 60 most recent lock commits (60.0%) and OSCILLATED fire to fire** (s2401 dropped · s2400 archived · s2399 dropped · s2398 dropped · s2397 archived). That oscillation is the signature of a discipline carried in handoff prose: each fire re-derives it, or does not, depending on what its predecessor happened to mention. 💡 **This is the factory's most-repeated finding for the SIXTH time (F-2153-1 · F-2204-1 · F-2350-1 · F-2360-1 · F-2365-1) — a known cure with no reader in the surface that governs the moment it applies — and F-2204-1 diagnosed this exact cure as parked outside the law 199 fires ago.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is measured rather than lazy: the drop-until-handoff pattern is LAWFUL and this clause keeps blessing it, so a red on "did not archive in the lock commit" would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). ⓘ The hazard's own reachability is honest too — 16 self-declared dead-lock takeovers in 875 lock commits (1.8%) — but it CLUSTERS: five of them fall in the last ~40 fires, and two were consecutive on 2026-08-31.** The trap is a handoff step that archives **the wrong line**: s1472's replace-then-archive helper would have archived its own LOCK line and left s1471's entire handoff in no bullet at all (caught and recovered verbatim from `f3f2f01b5`). **Before committing the handoff, `grep -c "s<N-1> handoff (line-1 archive)" STATUS.md` and expect 1.** 🗂️ **AND THE HANDOFF MUST END IN AN EXPLICIT, GREPPABLE DESK SEGMENT — this is now MECHANISED and it will REFUSE (exit 2), not warn (F-1471-3 cured s1472).** Write the desk at the END of line-1 as `🔺 **OWNER'S DESK — <n> awaiting a word.** 🔺 **F-xxxx-x** …`; any of `OWNER DESK` / `OWNER'S DESK` / `OWNERS DESK` (straight or curly apostrophe) is matched, and the LAST occurrence on line-1 wins, so an upstream prose mention is harmless. **If the desk is genuinely EMPTY, still write the header** — `test:desk-declaration` cannot tell "no items" from "I could not read the desk", and it now refuses rather than green over the difference. ⓘ **WHY THIS BECAME A HARD GATE, because it is a real change in fire duty and should not read as pedantry:** the guard used to search the WHOLE file for one spelling and take the FIRST hit, so a headerless or possessively-spelled line-1 silently resolved to an ARCHIVED desk — and archived desks are by construction already declared, so it reported a clean board every time it read the wrong one. It fails OPEN, which is how it passed for 137 fires while 4 desk items sat undeclared, three of them buried in rows marked ✅ SHIPPED. 📉 **The deeper measurement (F-1472-1): the desk header is a LAPSED convention — of 1249 line-1s in STATUS.md only 617 carry a desk word, and among the last ~55 fires roughly 5 did.** The rest route owner items as bare `🔺 **F-xxxx-x OPEN**` runs inside a `NEXT: (A)/(B)/(C)` paragraph, where nothing can check them. The bug and the lapsed convention hid each other, which is precisely why neither was noticed. ✅ Mid-fire the guard SKIPs on an `ACTIVE` lock line (so drain batteries are unaffected); the gate bites in `test:ledger-guards`, your last act. 🔎 **AND BEFORE YOU DECIDE WHAT TO CARRY, ASK THE INSTRUMENT — `node scripts/desk-state-audit.mjs` (shipped s1566 `6001f3a5`, from F-1561-5 with its cure re-scoped).** It classifies every carried desk item `CLOSED` / `OPEN` / `BOTH` / `OPEN-DESK-ONLY` / `UNRECORDED`, printing the BACKLOG line numbers that justify each verdict, and resolves backticked goal slugs from `tasks/goals.json` with their `blockClass`. Classification is **subject-first** — a row states the state only of the FIRST F-ID in its 90-char subject zone, so an id merely CITED inside another finding's subject carries no state; that rule was checked against all three live conflicts before it shipped. It is **advisory, exit 0 always**, by the `drain-block-check` UNKNOWN precedent; `--strict` exits 1 only when a carried item is `CLOSED` and therefore should have left the desk. ⚠️ **It cannot read your own line-1 while you hold the lock** — line 1 carries `ACTIVE` for a fire's entire working life and the tool prints `SKIP` (F-1566-1, non-blocking: a lock line genuinely has no desk). 🕳️ **AND FOR HUNDREDS OF FIRES IT DID NOT — THE SENTENCE YOU JUST READ WAS FALSE UNTIL s2227, AND THE WORD THAT MADE IT FALSE WAS `begins` (F-2227-1, measured and cured s2227).** This clause used to read *"line 1 **begins** `ACTIVE`"*, and so did the code: the lock test now at `desk-state-audit.mjs:72` — **was `:68` s2227–s2262 (re-based s2263, +4, when the F-2263-1 `FINDING_ALL` derivation landed above it — the SECOND rot of this coordinate and the SECOND RUNNING caused by a fire editing the very script the law cites, predicted by this clause and cured in the same landing commit), and `:42` before that; re-based by RE-GREPPING the content, never by adding a remembered delta; cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts** — read `line1.startsWith('ACTIVE')`. **That convention is RETIRED.** Today's lock line reads `Last updated: <stamp> ACTIVE (sNNNN fire) — <intent>`, which does not START with the word — so the `kind:'lock'` branch was UNREACHABLE and every lock line fell through to the `kind:'none'` arm: **`REFUSING — no desk header on line-1` at rc=2**, a message that accuses the wrong subject, since the line has no desk header BECAUSE it is a lock. 📊 **MEASURED over all 4,639 STATUS.md commits: the predicate its three siblings share (`/\bACTIVE\b/ && !/lock CLEARED/`) identifies 2,119 lock lines; `startsWith` identifies 1,696 — 423 disagreements, and the disagreement is CURRENT.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND NOT INFLATED: NOT a false green, NOT a Mistake #8 clearance.** The direction was CONSERVATIVE — a loud refusal — and this advisory is chained bare in NO battery (only its `.test.mjs` is a leg), so nothing ever redded and no verdict was wrong. What rotted was the DOCUMENTED behaviour, in this clause and in `gate-caller-baseline.json`'s F-1566-2 entry, which BOTH promised a benign `SKIP`. ⓘ **F-1566-2's CONCLUSION — never root this advisory in a battery — SURVIVES its refuted reason and is STRENGTHENED by it: an rc=2 refusal would have redded any battery that chained it.** 🔑 **WHY IT ROTTED IS THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT NAMES A BLIND SPOT IN THIS STREAK'S OWN METHOD: a predicate can drift for hundreds of fires precisely BECAUSE its failure is loud and safe, so nobody is ever forced to look at it.** Every discriminator built since s2204 hunts a corpus that goes silently EMPTY; this one went loudly WRONG, and no census keyed on swallows, handlers, guards or trees can see that shape. 🛠️ **CURE: import the sibling predicate rather than write a fifth copy** — `desk-carryforward-guard.mjs` already imports `subjectLedClosure` FROM `desk-state-audit.mjs`, so the cycle predates the change, and F-1261-1's rule (*"there is one implementation of that word in this repo"*) is the same reason that import exists. **Four independent copies of one predicate is HOW this drifted**, so the guard asserts all four agree rather than testing the cured file alone. ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): the PRESCRIBED `--status <inherited handoff>` path is 3/3 BYTE-IDENTICAL across default/`--strict`/`--json`; only the two lock-line arms move, and they move TO the `SKIP` this clause promises.** 🔒 **`scripts/desk-lock-predicate-guard.test.mjs` — 8 arms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards` (battery GREEN, 368 assertions / 0 fail, up from 360).** 🔬 **TEETH: pre-cure verbatim reds 3 of 8 with all four reverse controls green; INVERTED reds 5; NEVER-a-lock reds 4; and the OVER-GENERAL cure — dropping the `lock CLEARED` clause, which would make every desk gate SKIP on every handoff and retire the whole desk-gating layer — reds ARM 4 ALONE.** ⚠️ **One prediction corrected (I expected that variant to red four arms), and per s2226's duty the sweep doubled as a reachability audit: ALL 8 ARMS red in at least one manufactured variant, so none is decoration.** 💡 **AND THE TRAP THAT NEARLY HID THIS, WHICH BIT TWICE IN ONE FIRE AND IS THE THING TO CARRY FORWARD: F-2215-1's validity assertion — *"did the control arm PRODUCE anything?"* — ASKS WHETHER THE TOOL RAN, NOT WHETHER THE VERDICT PATH RAN.** s2227's root-vs-worktree sweep of the three desk guards read `INVARIANT` on all three at 167 B per arm and was **VACUOUS**: the fire held the lock, so all three printed their legitimate `SKIP` and it had compared three pairs of SKIPS. **A SKIP is not silence — it DEFEATS the s2224 agreeing-silences rule by SATISFYING it.** The same trap then bit the guard itself: arm 8 asserts an rc=2 refusal, and this tool resolves `--backlog`/`--goals` **CWD-relatively** (`desk-state-audit.mjs:235` — **was `:180` s2227–s2262, re-based s2263 by +55 when the F-2263-1 `nonSubjectMentions` block landed above it; cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts**), so off the repo root it refused with ENOENT at **the same exit code** and the arm passed for the wrong reason until its MESSAGE assertion caught it. ➡️ **Assert that your arm REACHED THE BRANCH you are measuring, and assert the OBSERVABLE rather than the exit code alone.** 📋 **A SIBLING MEASUREMENT BANKED, NOT CURED (F-2227-2): `test:ledger-guards` exits 0 IDENTICALLY whether its three desk gates evaluated or SKIPPED** — measured mid-fire on the live board, `rc=0` with all three printing the lock SKIP, i.e. three verdict-bearing legs checking nothing while the aggregate code stays silent. **Deliberately NOT cured: the SKIP is correct, and making it refuse would red every lawful mid-fire battery (F-1460-1).** The ordering law already cures it and this was VERIFIED rather than assumed — replaying the **20 most recent REAL handoff commits** through the two file-based desk guards gives **20/20 evaluated, 0 SKIPs, 0 flagged**. ➡️ **So the owed act is a READING HABIT: when the battery is green, check the three desk legs say `PASS`, not `SKIP`. A `SKIP` means you ran it before your handoff commit and the desk is still ungated — re-run it after.** **So point it at what you INHERITED:** extract the previous fire's archived line-1 (the `- **s<N-1> handoff (line-1 archive):** …` bullet, strip that prefix) into a temp file and pass `--status <that file>`. That is the question a handoff actually asks — *what did I inherit, and is any of it already answered?* 📊 **First real run, s1566, over s1565's 22 carried items against main's `BACKLOG.md` and `goals.json`: `CLOSED=0 · OPEN=5 · BOTH=0 · OPEN-DESK-ONLY=17 · UNRECORDED=0`** — nothing was carried that should have been dropped, and no carried item is a ghost. ✅ **THIS RETIRES A STANDING DISCLAIMER: s1561, s1563 and s1564 each carried 22 items forward with an explicit "I did NOT re-verify each item's closure state" note, because no instrument could answer it.** A fire that writes that sentence now is declining a tool it has. ⓘ **A `CLOSED` verdict is a prompt to CHECK, not an order to drop** — the verdict is evidence about what BACKLOG records, and only the owner's word actually closes an owner-desk item. `OPEN-DESK-ONLY` is the normal resting state of a live desk item (17 of 22), not a defect. Update "Robin owes" honestly (close what he delivered, add what emerged). Commit as `s<N> handoff: <summary>`. 🧹 **AND WRITE YOUR ONE-SHOT SPLICE SCRATCH TO `/tmp`, NOT `scripts/` — `scripts/` IS A RUN SURFACE, AND EVERY HELPER A FIRE LEAVES THERE DEGRADES A LANE INSTRUMENT (F-1665-1, measured s1665).** The lock/handoff/ledger/desk helpers a fire writes to splice `STATUS.md` and `goals.json` (the backtick-prose law's prescribed technique) have been committed into `scripts/` by **38 fires, and 151 of the 421 tracked files there — 35.9% — are now single-fire scratch.** `scripts` is one of the nine entries in `lane-usable.mjs`'s `RUN_SURFACE_BASE` (renamed s2349; the surface is now those nine PLUS the gate roots derived from `main:package.json` — F-2349-1) (cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts), whose design comment calls the list *"deliberately narrow: only surfaces that can change whether a task runs or how it is gated"* and excludes `docs/ tasks/ artifacts/ reviews/ logs/` **BY DESIGN**. 📊 **Measured on the live fleet: lane/b's run-surface drift reads 5 files, ALL FIVE s1664 lock/handoff scratch — a 100% false signal** — which suppresses the ✅ *"NONE of it is run-surface … nothing here can stop a task running"* green at `lane-usable.mjs:559` (**was `:540` s2366–s2489 — re-based s2490 by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`if (drift.length === 0) {`, 540 → 559, **+19**), the rot caused BY THE F-2490-1 CURE LANDED IN THIS VERY FILE: `dirt()`'s `--untracked-files=all` flag and its reasoning block sit ~270 lines above it. The FIFTH CONSECUTIVE rot of this coordinate caused by a fire editing the very file the law cites, predicted before committing and cured in its own landing fire. ⓘ **The shift sizes are now +53, +10, +79, +19 — a spread of 69 with no trend, so no remembered delta could ever have been right; and note that a fire whose whole subject was *"a summary view is a lossy projection"* still had to re-measure its own pointer by hand, because a coordinate is a summary view too.** Re-grepped, then eye-checked by reading line 559 back: it printed the `if (drift.length === 0) {` line itself, which is the only proof the new number lands on the right line; was `:461` s2349–s2365 — re-based s2366 by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`if (drift.length === 0) {`, 461 → 540, **+79, THE LARGEST SHIFT THIS COORDINATE HAS EVER TAKEN**), the rot caused BY THE F-2366-1 CURE LANDED IN THIS VERY FILE: the stale-holder classifier and its reasoning block sit ~80 lines above it. Predicted before committing and cured in the same fire. ⓘ **This is the fourth consecutive rot of this coordinate caused by a fire editing the very file the law cites, and the shift sizes so far are +53, +10, +79 — a spread that makes the point better than any warning: there is NO safe constant, and a fire that carried ANY remembered delta forward would have been wrong here by tens of lines. Re-grep, then eye-check with `sed -n <N>p`; the guard proves a pointer MOVED, never that your new number lands on the right line.**; was `:451` FOR ONE HOUR IN s2349, and `:398` s1665–s2348 — re-based TWICE IN ONE FIRE by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`if (drift.length === 0) {`, 398 → 451 → 461), both rots caused BY THIS FIRE'S OWN EDITS to the very file the law cites: first the F-2349-1 derived-run-surface block (+53), then its stderr-leak fix (+10). ⓘ **This is the two-edits-in-one-fire case the GZ-01 clause warns about in its own coordinate, and it bit here exactly as predicted: a fire that re-based once and then carried `+53` forward in its head would have written `:504` and handed the next reader a coordinate 43 lines past the citation. There is no safe constant — the shift is only ever however many lines your own edit happened to add, and you may edit twice.** Re-grepped and eye-checked against `sed -n 461p` both times; cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts**) (**was `:398` s1665–s2348 — re-based s2349 by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`if (drift.length === 0) {`, 398 → 451, **+53**), the rot caused BY THE CURE THAT F-2349-1 LANDED IN THIS VERY FILE: the derived-run-surface block sits ~50 lines above it. Predicted before committing and cured in the same fire; cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts**) and prints an amber telling the next fire to go prove ancestry for dead files; lanes a/c/d read 18/74/34 where only 13/60/20 are real. ⚠️ **DO NOT CURE THIS BY SWEEPING `scripts/tmp-*` — THE OBVIOUS CURE INVERTS ITS OWN METRIC, AND IT IS FORBIDDEN TWICE OVER.** (1) **It makes the symptom dramatically worse:** `git diff --name-only <branch> main -- scripts` lists paths differing in **PRESENCE** as well as content, so deleting a file makes it drift from the other side — measured, a 125-file sweep takes lane/b from **5 → 126** and lanes a/c/d to **139 / 186 / 146**, until all four lanes are refreshed. (2) **26 of the 151 are CITED RETAINED EVIDENCE, not debris:** `tmp-s1046-unshipped.mjs` alone is cited **24×**, is named in a ratified master's NO list as *"a historical artefact retired by comment under the RETENTION LAW; read it, cite it, **never edit or delete it**"*, and is the subject of a live pre-flight `grep -Fc` that a sweep would turn into a false STOP; `gate-battery.mjs:16` cites `tmp-s1146-gate.mjs:51` **by line**. (3) The RETENTION LAW and `CLAUDE.md` §4.10 both say supersede/move, never erase. ➡️ **THEREFORE THE CURE IS AT THE GENERATOR AND COSTS NOTHING: write splice helpers to `/tmp/s<N>-*.mjs`,** outside the repo, where they can never enter the run surface (s1665 wrote every one of its own there; s1045's handoff already called its untracked lock scratch *"harmless"*). ⓘ **A probe that PRODUCED EVIDENCE is the opposite case and still belongs in `scripts/`, committed and cited** — that is the distinction the corpus already draws, and those 26 files are exactly it. 🚫 **No guard is proposed and none should be built:** "one-shot helper vs retained probe" is a judgement, so a red guard would fire on every legitimate probe and be excused into uselessness within a week (the `cross-engine` label's fate, F-1460-1). 🧯 **AND IF YOU DRAINED A MASTER THIS FIRE, SWEEP YOUR OWN OUTGOING LINE-1 FOR ITS NAME BEFORE YOU COMMIT — A DISPATCH INSTRUCTION IS PERISHABLE ACROSS THE VERY DRAIN THAT DISCHARGES IT, AND THE DRAINING FIRE IS THE ONE MOST LIKELY TO CARRY IT FORWARD STALE (F-2373-1, measured s2373).** 🔑 **THE MECHANISM IS THE HANDOFF'S OWN ECONOMY: a fire copies its closing sections forward rather than re-deriving them, so a drain performed EARLIER IN THE SAME FIRE silently invalidates a sentence living in a section the fire never re-reads.** 📊 **MEASURED: s2371 drained `b4b65f0efaa135eb1be34a31661028206f702444` at 17:15, flipping `lane-c-emdash-player-copy-and-guard-denominator.md`'s leaf to `status="merged"` — and its OWN handoff still read *"the very master (E)(A) tells you to dispatch"* and called it *"my banked em-dash master"*. s2372 then copied that section forward again AND re-attributed s2369's `6dbfb83d3` to itself.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO and this is NOT a false green — s2373 ran §2E's prescribed `drain-block-check --queue` and got `⛔ ALREADY SHIPPED — DO NOT QUEUE` in one second, exactly as the law intends. The defence held.** 🎯 **What earns it a clause is the DIRECTION and the SURVIVAL: the stale instruction points at Mistake #8, the 824k Flail — this catalog's single most expensive recorded failure — and it survived TWO consecutive fires, one of which was the fire that merged it.** ➡️ **THE DUTY, and it is