d of re-grepping, it would have written 1418 twice and handed the next fire a coordinate exactly one item wrong.** The two items were filed hours apart around a drain, not back-to-back, which is what makes the remembered-delta error tempting; re-based s2270 TWICE IN ONE FIRE by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`1400 → 1406 → 1412`, +6 each) — ⓘ **a fire that lands TWO player-visible merges files TWO gazette items and must re-base this pointer AFTER EACH ONE; re-basing once at the end works only if you re-grep, and a fire that carried the first delta forward in its head would have written 1406 and been exactly one item wrong** when this fire filed its GZ-01 item for the harness-disclosure-field merge, the SIXTH CONSECUTIVE rot of this coordinate caused by a fire obeying the very duty this clause states, and the sixth to be predicted and cured in its own landing fire — ⚠️ **and note the `:1394` this list did NOT carry: s2251 re-based to 1394 and recorded it, then a later fire moved it to 1400 and updated the NUMBER without extending the history, so one rot went unnarrated. The number is what a reader follows, but the list is what tells them how fast it moves; move BOTH.** Re-based s2251 by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`1388 → 1394`, +6) when this fire filed its GZ-01 item for the ap15-frontier-registry merge, the FIFTH CONSECUTIVE rot of this coordinate caused by a fire obeying the very duty this clause states, and the fifth to be predicted and cured in its own landing fire; re-based s2249 by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`1382 → 1388`, +6) when that fire filed its GZ-01 item for the town-cast merge; re-based s2238 when this fire filed its own GZ-01 item at the top of the queue, +7 lines: the SAME mechanism as the s2193 and s2237 rots, and now the THIRD CONSECUTIVE rot of this coordinate caused by a fire OBEYING the very duty this clause states. ⓘ **That makes it a PREDICTED, ROUTINE part of the GZ-01 lifecycle, not a lapse: every drain that files a gazette item pushes this line down by ~6–7, so a fire filing one should EXPECT `law-pointer-guard` to red once, on its own bookkeeping, and should re-base in the same fire.** ⚠️ **And note the SIZE is not a constant — s2193 and s2237 both moved it +6, this one +7, because the shift is just however many lines your own item happens to occupy. A fire that re-bases by ADDING the remembered delta rather than by RE-GREPPING the content will be off by one and hand the next fire a coordinate that points at a bullet of my item instead of the citation.** Re-based s2281 TWICE IN ONE FIRE by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`1430 → 1436 → 1442`, +6 each) when that fire filed GZ-01 items for the assayer-environment-honesty and door-tick-ceiling-all-epochs merges — the TENTH AND ELEVENTH CONSECUTIVE rots of this coordinate caused by a fire obeying the very duty this clause states, both predicted and cured in their own landing fire. ⓘ **s2281 is the THIRD fire to hit the two-item case (after s2270 and s2279), and it confirms the warning is load-bearing rather than theoretical: its two items were filed ~25 minutes apart around two separate drains, which is exactly the spacing that makes a remembered delta tempting.** Each coordinate was ALSO verified by eye against `sed -n '<N>p'` before being written down, because the guard only proves a pointer MOVED — it cannot prove the new number lands on the right line. ⓘ **TEN CONSECUTIVE +6 SHIFTS NOW, WHICH IS THE PATTERN'S OWN WARNING: the CONSTANCY IS THE TRAP.** A five-line item plus its blank separator has been the house shape for ten fires running, so the remembered delta is RIGHT often enough to feel safe and is wrong exactly when someone writes a four- or six-line item — s2238's +7 is the recorded counter-example. **Re-grep anyway; the cost is one command and the failure is a coordinate that points into the middle of your own item.** Re-based s2280 by RE-GREPPING the cited content (`1424 → 1430`, +6) when that fire filed its GZ-01 item for the door-tick-ceiling-v2 merge — the NINTH CONSECUTIVE rot of this coordinate caused by a fire obeying the very duty this clause states, and the ninth to be predicted and cured in its own landing fire. cite the CONTENT, the coordinate drifts**) as `e7bb88cf`, and the other nine in backticked hash runs in the 08-05/08-06 ticker digests. ⚠️ **This costs MORE than the blindness it resembles: a false-absent set gets BACKFILLED, so the owner receives duplicate news for merges already reported — worse than doing nothing.** ➡️ **THEREFORE THE REAL CURE IS NOT A BETTER NEEDLE — IT IS TO STOP HAND-ROLLING THE GREP: RUN `node scripts/gazette-backfill-sweep.mjs`.** It has existed since s1608 (F-1608-1), it indexes citations **verbatim and tests by PREFIX** — so it is immune to this defect by construction, at any abbreviation width on either side — and since s1613 it prints three numbers (`reported / dismissed / candidates`) with the dismissed list, WARN-level, exit 0 in every state per this clause's own prohibition. **The prose above describes a manual check the factory no longer needs; a fire that reads it as instructions rather than as rationale is the one that gets the wrong answer.** Any fixed width is a bet on someone else's convention, and the outbox is genuinely mixed (86 full + 105 short in the gazette, 618 short in the digests). ✅ **SWEEP STATE: DISCHARGED at s1633 — 0 candidates, confirmed TWICE by independent methods: my hand 8-char truncation over 63 `src`/`assets` merges → 0, and the instrument over its own wider 67-merge player-path set → `reported 63 / dismissed 4 / candidates 0`.** The duty was carried forward as "still owed" by s1631 and s1632 after s1600/s1608/s1610 had already run it, and every drain since s1607 files its GZ-01 item inline at drain-bookkeeping time (visible in the queue file's own log). **Re-measure before carrying it again — this is a Mistake #4 magnet, and the carry costs a fire's whole budget.** Batch backfills into a single `## ROUNDUP —` item per the ≤3/week rule, and break out separately only a merge that carries a live OWNER CHOICE, which is the one thing a roundup can bury. ⚠️ **DO NOT mechanise this as a red guard that reds on any absent hash** — "player-visible" is a judgement, so such a guard fires on every bookkeeping and infrastructure merge and gets excused into uselessness within a week (the `cross-engine` label's fate, F-1460-1). A WARN-level list is the most that should ever be built. — {ledger-voice headline ≤80 chars, ≤3 plain lines, merge hash, screenshot/artifact ref}. Frontier speak per the 063 voice law; NEVER token/price talk; bugs-on-camera get an owner-choice note. ≤3 GAZETTE items/week reach the owner (batch the rest into the weekly roundup item). 🔢 **AND THAT BUDGET IS A *WEEKLY AGGREGATE* THAT NO SINGLE FIRE CAN SEE — SO ASK THE SWEEP, WHICH NOW COUNTS IT FOR YOU, BEFORE YOU FILE (F-2491-1, measured and cured s2491).** A fire files ONE item, for ITS OWN drain, and that act is locally correct **every single time**. The rule's subject is the WEEK, and until this fire nothing in the loop observed it: `gazette-backfill-sweep` counted `reported / dismissed / candidates` and had **no opinion whatever** on how many headlines the week already held. 📊 **MEASURED s2491 for ISO week 2026-W36: `18` standalone items against a budget of `3` — six times over — filed by ELEVEN SEPARATE FIRES (s2459 · s2460 · s2461 · s2462 · s2468 · s2469 · s2470 · s2471 · s2472 · s2473 · s2476), each judging its own merge worth a headline.** ⓘ **Fires DO batch when they think of it — `6` ROUNDUP-CLASS items sit in the very same window, and their own prose says *"the week's three headline slots are spent"* — so this is not neglect. THE COUNTING WAS A HABIT, NOT A STEP**, and during a high-drain burst every fire independently reached the same locally-correct answer. ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: NOT a false green, nothing is broken, and NO VERDICT ANYWHERE WAS WRONG — every one of those 18 items is honest news about a real merge. The queue is a QUEUE the owner approves FROM, so an over-full queue publishes nothing to the family.** 🎯 **What earns it a clause is the DIRECTION: it fails toward MORE owner load, in the one pipeline whose entire purpose is to BOUND owner load — the owner's "one morning action" is handed 18 headlines to weigh where the rule promises 3.** ➡️ **THE DUTY IS NOW ONE LINE OF THE SWEEP YOU ALREADY RUN: it prints `weekly headline budget (<ISO week>): N standalone, M batched` on EVERY run including the happy path (F-2208-1), and when the slots are spent it names what is already standing so the warning is actionable rather than a scold. If N > 3, file as `ROUNDUP-CLASS` — your one item genuinely does look fine alone, which is exactly why the aggregate is the thing to read.** 🚫 **ADVISORY, exit 0 always, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: this tool's own header says "player-visible" is a JUDGEMENT, so a red fires on honest bookkeeping and is excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). The COUNT, however, is mechanical — which is precisely why it belongs in the instrument rather than in a sentence, and is the one thing separating this cure from the twenty that correctly declined a guard.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/gazette-weekly-budget-guard.test.mjs` — 13 arms, 1.3 s, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; `gate-caller-audit` PASS.** 🔬 **TEETH, 11 variants manufactured on scratch copies with the subject restored byte-identical, and per s2226 the sweep doubled as a reachability audit: ALL 13 ARMS red under at least one variant, so none is decoration — but only after TWO more variants were built for two arms that the first pass reddened with nothing.** ⚠️ **THREE METHOD NOTES PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE, and the first is the one worth carrying. (1) THE SWEEP REFUTED AN ARM I HAD JUST WRITTEN AND NAMED AFTER A REAL FINDING: I added an arm claiming to defend F-2391-1 (the UTC/local date-string trap), manufactured that exact defect — `Date.UTC` swapped for a parsed date STRING — and it **reddened NOTHING**. The subject takes y/m/d INTEGERS, so the hazard is unreachable BY CONSTRUCTION; the safety is a property of the SIGNATURE, not of any assertion. The arm was renamed to what it actually tests, because **an arm named for a defence it cannot provide is worse than no arm — it makes the next reader stop looking.** (2) MY FIRST CENSUS WAS A FLOOR, NOT A COUNT: dating items from the sweep's own `rows` (player-path merges inside `--since`) silently drops every item citing a CONTAINED commit, which the existing roundups genuinely do — a week cited entirely that way would report `0 standalone` and NOT warn, a false clean rather than a conservative under-count. Dating from the whole history is `12,563` commits in **~216 ms**, so the honest read is also the cheap one; an arm now reds on that exact regression. (3) MY OWN TEETH HARNESS UNDER-REPORTED COVERAGE: its `^✖ (.+?) \(` matcher truncated every arm name at the first ` (`, so arms whose names carry a parenthetical scored ZERO and looked like decoration. **A reachability audit is itself an instrument, and its blind spot is indistinguishable from the absence of coverage it is built to find.**\012An engine-era registry bump is player-visible news by definition (`tasks/engine-era-law-v3.md`).\012DAILY (first fire after 06:00 local): compile yesterday's merges into ONE ticker digest appended to marketing/outbox/ticker-digest-<date>.md (micro-headlines ≤140 chars + hashes). The owner approves digests in one morning action. Publication stays owner-only, always. 🕰️ **AND VALIDATE YOUR DATE WINDOW ON A DAY YOU KNOW IS BUSY BEFORE YOU BELIEVE ANY ZERO IT REPORTS — A UTC/LOCAL SLIP COLLAPSES THE WINDOW TO ZERO WIDTH AND RETURNS AN AFFIRMATIVE `0` FOR EVERY DAY ALIKE (F-2391-1, measured s2391).** This machine is **UTC+07**, so a window built the obvious way — `new Date(Date.parse(d + 'T00:00:00+07:00') + 86400000).toISOString().slice(0,10)` — renders `+07` midnight as **17:00Z the PREVIOUS day**, and the derived end equals the start. 📊 **MEASURED: that probe returned `0 first-parent commits` for 2026-08-27, 08-28 AND 08-29 — and the 08-29 digest, sitting on disk, records `95`.** The correct form (pure integer calendar arithmetic on the y/m/d components, NO `Date` parsing of the window bounds) reproduces `92 · 1 · 0 · 95 · 121` across 08-26→08-30 and **exactly re-derives the recorded judgement** that 08-27 holds one commit (a fire's lock line) and 08-28 none. ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO, and NO SHIPPED TOOL HAS THIS BUG** — `ledger-mirror-freshness.mjs` avoids `Date` parsing on purpose and says so, which is where this trap was already named for its own two scripts. This was an **ad-hoc triage probe**, the kind a fire types by hand and no battery observes (the F-1582-1 gap). 🎯 **What earns it a clause is the DIRECTION and the UNIFORMITY: the answer is not off by a day, it is `0` for EVERY subject — the empty-corpus false green this file has spent twenty fires curing, arriving through a TIMEZONE rather than a `catch`, a guard, a cwd or a filename. A quiet-day verdict and a 95-commit day are byte-identical, so the probe is right exactly when you are lucky.** ➡️ **THE CURE IS ONE ARM AND IT IS F-2215-1's RULE APPLIED TO A WINDOW RATHER THAN TO A TOOL: before trusting a zero, run the same probe over a day whose count you already know is non-zero. If THAT comes back `0` too, the window is broken, not the day.** 🚫 **No guard is proposed and the restraint is measured: there is no shipped artifact to guard, and "did a fire hand-type a correct date window?" is unobservable to every battery — the same reason F-1669-1 declined a mechanism. This is a READING habit, like §2.0c's runner check.** 💡 **REUSABLE: a corpus can go empty from a TIMEZONE as easily as from a name, a cwd or a stale tree — and a window is a selector nobody in this streak has been keyed to check. Whenever a probe reports "nothing happened", ask what it would have reported had something happened.**\012\012## RT-01 DUTY — the weekly rotation mint (added 2026-09-03, attended — the transfer board's fresh seeds, spec `specs/transfer-board.md` L2/L5)\012ONCE PER ISO WEEK, on the first fire after Sunday 00:00 UTC (check whether `assets/rotations/rotation-seeds.json` already holds the rotation whose `opensAt` is the coming Monday; if yes, DISCHARGE with one line): mint it with `node scripts/rotation-mint.mjs --week <YYYY-Www of the coming Monday> --salt-file ~/.goldrush/rotation-salt --append assets/rotations/rotation-seeds.json`, refresh the fenced block in `public/skill.md` between `skillmd-guard:rotations:start/end` with that rotation's JSON, re-pin the skill.md guard baseline per its own instructions, move `CURRENT_ROTATION_ID` in `site/assay-office.js` to the rotation that is OPEN (not the one just minted), commit path-scoped with prefix `rt:`, push, and note it in the handoff. The salt is owner-held and never committed; a missing salt file is a STOP with one desk line, never a default.\012\012## LB-01 DUTY — the county book's daily offsite pull (added 2026-08-24, attended — l4 follow-up #2)\012ONCE PER COVERAGE DAY (the TK-01 cadence — check whether `artifacts/ledger-backups/` already holds today's `ledger-YYYY-MM-DD.db`; if yes, DISCHARGED, move on): run `node scripts/ledger-backup-pull.mjs`. It is idempotent and bounded; box unreachable = WARN in the handoff, never a red, retry next fire. If it pulled a new file: `git add artifacts/ledger-backups/<file>` path-scoped + commit (`ops: LB-01 ledger mirror <date>`) + push — origin IS the offsite copy (the l4 design: no new secrets on the box). ⚠️ STANDING GATE from the l4 runner's own note: BEFORE the accounts flip onto sqlite, this raw mirror must become an ENCRYPTED artifact — a plaintext ledger of county standings is fine, account data is not. That flip's master must name this line. 🔒 **AND THAT GATE NOW HAS AN INSTRUMENT INSTEAD OF ONLY A SENTENCE — RUN `node scripts/ledger-mirror-exposure.mjs` (F-2353-1, measured and cured s2353).** The gate sat here as PROSE from 2026-08-24 with **zero enforcement surface**, and the manual key-read s2352 added below covers the `--fill-gaps` arm ONLY — so the DAILY arm, the one whose output this duty tells you to `git add` + commit + **push**, had neither an instruction nor an instrument. ⚖️ **NOTHING IS EXPOSED TODAY, measured not assumed: all 6 mirrors hold `kv` only, 32 keys, ZERO account-class rows.** 🚪 **What earns it a tool is that the door is ONE-WAY: a committed account row can only be undone by a history rewrite, i.e. a force-push, which is deny-listed here.** 🔑 **AND THE GATE IS HELD SHUT BY AN OUTAGE, NOT BY DESIGN — F-2353-2 measured the routing rather than inferring it: `wrangler.toml` binds NO `ACCOUNTS`, `ops/droplet/agenttown.app.nginx.conf:17–24` proxies `/api/(request-code|verify|session|save/|delete-account)` to the local sqlite ledger at `127.0.0.1:8791`, and `ops/droplet/ledger-backup.mjs:6` mirrors that SAME `/opt/goldrush-ledger/ledger.db` wholesale. Confirmed live with a zero-side-effect probe (an invalid email 400s only AFTER the ACCOUNTS+RESEND gates at `functions/api/_accounts.ts:87–90`): `agenttown.app` → **400 `invalid_email`**, `gold-rush-3in.pages.dev` → **503 `sign_in_not_enabled`**. So account writes land in the very DB this duty commits, and **fixing F-MAIL-0829 is what ARMS this gate** — cite F-2352-3 before doing it.** ⓘ `ledger-backup-pull.mjs` now hard-stops on account rows at the door on both arms, and the same tool is a leg of `test:ledger-guards`, so a mirror that reaches a commit unverified reds at your last act. **It refuses ONLY on account-class rows (never lawful to commit); an UNRECOGNISED key class declares and warns without refusing, and an ABSENT mirror directory clears — both deliberate, so this cannot be excused into uselessness (F-1460-1).** 🕳️ **AND THAT DISCHARGE TEST CANNOT SEE A HOLE — IT IS EXISTENTIAL AND KEYED ON TODAY, SO ANY DAY IT MISSES IS MISSED FOREVER, UNREPORTED, BY CONSTRUCTION (F-2351-1, measured and cured s2351).** The rule above states its whole test as *"check whether `artifacts/ledger-backups/` already holds today's `ledger-YYYY-MM-DD.db`; if yes, DISCHARGED, move on"*. That question is answerable only about TODAY: **once today's file lands, every future fire reads DISCHARGED and a gap behind it never becomes today again.** No amount of diligence closes this, because the question is never asked. 📊 **MEASURED s2351, and the gap was live: `ledger-2026-08-27.db` is ABSENT from the series (24 · 25 · 26 · __ · 28 · 29).** The 08-27 fire log carries **22 FIRE STARTs and ZERO mentions** of `LB-01` or `ledger-backup-pull`; so does 08-28's — the duty simply did not run, and the 08-28 mirror was finally committed at **2026-08-29T01:33+07:00, a day late**. ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED — THIS IS NOT A DATA-LOSS FINDING: the realised cost is ZERO.** The mirror is a CUMULATIVE sqlite DB; 08-26 and 08-28 are genuinely distinct backups (**different sha256, verified — not merely the same 618496-byte size**) that bracket the hole, and player sign-in was DOWN 2026-08-24→08-29 (F-MAIL-0829), so no new standings could have been written on the missing day. **Nothing was lost.** 🎯 **WHAT EARNS IT A CLAUSE IS THE DIRECTION AND THE FACT THAT NOBODY COULD HAVE KNOWN EITHER WAY: an offsite backup is the one artifact whose entire purpose is to be there on the day the disk dies (Mistake #11, "The Missing Remote" — weeks of work on one disk, no origin). A backup series that silently stops being refreshed while every prescribed probe reports success is that mistake rebuilt one layer up.** 🔎 **AND NOTHING WATCHED IT — VERIFIED BY READING, NOT INFERRED: `artifacts/ledger-backups/` has ZERO consumers anywhere in `scripts/`, `ops/` or `package.json` outside the pull script itself, `health-watch.sh` does not touch it, and LB-01 appears in exactly ONE law surface line (this one) — not in `CLAUDE.md`, not in any skill.** That is F-2153-1 and F-2204-1 a fourth time: **the duty had exactly one reader and no instrument.** ➡️ **THEREFORE, AS PART OF THE LB-01 DUTY: run `node scripts/ledger-mirror-freshness.mjs`** — it names every MISSING interior day and the AGE of the newest mirror, declares its corpus on stdout ALWAYS including the happy path (F-2208-1), and separates **2 = "could not answer"** from **1 = "answered, and the answer refuses"** under `--strict`. 🚫 **IT IS ADVISORY AND EXITS 0 IN EVERY STATE BY DEFAULT, and that restraint is measured rather than lazy: a gap is frequently LAWFUL** (the box is down, the machine slept, the droplet's own cron had not yet run, a fire cycle went to a drain), **so a red would fire during ordinary correct operation and be excused into uselessness inside a week — the `cross-engine` fate this file names at F-1460-1. It is a triage READ, exactly as §2.0c's runner check is, not a gate.** ⓘ **A hole is a JUDGEMENT, not a backfill order: the droplet keeps only its own retention window and may no longer hold the day. Record the judgement where the next fire reads it.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/ledger-mirror-freshness-guard.test.mjs` — 15 arms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`.** ⚠️ **EVERY ARM WAS PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING THE DEFECT, and the sweep doubled as a reachability audit per s2226's duty: 11 variants, and after the first pass FOUR arms (1, 3, 11, 14) had been reddened by NOTHING — i.e. were candidate decoration — so four more variants were built until every one of the 15 reds under at least one.** Each over-general cure is caught by exactly the reverse control built for it: **re-anchoring the corpus to `process.cwd()` reds arm 9 ALONE** (the subject anchors to `import.meta.url` ON PURPOSE, so the only way to aim it at a fixture is to RELOCATE it — which is what makes that arm a genuine control), and **deriving "today" from `toISOString()` reds arm 15 ALONE.** 🧭 **THAT LAST ONE IS THE DATE TRAP, AND IT IS WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU EDIT EITHER FILE: `new Date('2026-08-27')` parses as UTC MIDNIGHT while `new Date()` is LOCAL, and this machine is UTC+07.** `ledger-backup-pull.mjs` computes its own "today" as `new Date().toISOString().slice(0,10)` — **in UTC** — so between 00:00 and 07:00 local it is already reasoning about YESTERDAY. ⚖️ **That skew is NOT claimed as a defect and was deliberately left alone: the droplet's backup lands ~09:30 local, well inside the window where the two agree, and the script self-corrects later the same day. It is named ONLY so the next reader does not "fix" the freshness probe into agreement with it.** The probe therefore does NO `Date` parsing of the series at all — pure integer calendar arithmetic, with "today" from LOCAL components. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF: an EXISTENTIAL check ("does today's artifact exist?") and a SERIES check ("is the series whole?") are different questions, and the first is the one that feels like diligence. Whenever a duty is discharged by the presence of one dated artifact, ask what it would take for that check to report a MISSING EARLIER ONE — and if the answer is "nothing could", the duty has no instrument, only a habit.** 🛠️ **AND WHEN IT NAMES A HOLE, YOU CAN NOW CLOSE ONE — `node scripts/ledger-backup-pull.mjs --fill-gaps` (F-2352-2, measured and cured s2352). THE PROBE ABOVE WAS DETECTION WITH NO RECOVERY PATH FOR ITS ENTIRE FIRST DAY.** The default pull asks the box for `ls -1t <dir>/ledger-*.db | head -n 1` — **the single newest file** — and exits early the moment today's copy is present locally, so it is **structurally incapable of ever fetching a day behind today**: a fire reading `⚠️ MISSING COVERAGE DAY(S)` and reaching for the prescribed tool got a no-op, in every mode. That is the ladder shape this file keeps recording (F-2209-1 exit code → F-2210-1 stdout → F-2211-1 capture → F-2217-1 enumeration): **a cure lands at layer N and the layer be